                "wiki_body": "thumb #4646493 thumb #4568986 thumb #4166206 thumb #4713005 thumb #3849569\r\n\r\n[i]Ovis[/i], members known as [i]Ovines[/i], are a genus of [[caprine|caprines]], referred to as the sheep family. Most members are casually known as \"sheep\", most especially the [[domestic sheep]]. In casual usage, sheep as a term is almost always used to refer to domesticated sheep.\r\n\r\nCompared to other [[bovid|bovids]] and [[caprine|caprines]], sheep tend to have divergent, [[curved horn|spiral curled horns]] and much stockier bodies. They also posses scent glands on their faces and feet, utilized in [[marking_territory|territory marking]]. Domesticated sheep are raised for their [[wool]], [[milk|milks]], and [[meat|meats]].\r\n\r\nThere are many differing terms for sheep depending on things such as age and gender. [[Female]] sheep are called [i]ewes[/i], [[male|males]] are called [i]rams[/i] (sometimes also called [i]bucks[/i] or [i]tups[/i]), and [[young]] sheep are called [i]lambs[/i].\r\n\r\n[b]Contrary to common misconception[/b], a ram is not a separate animal, nor can a [[goat]] be a ram - a ram is simply the word for an adult male sheep.\r\n\r\nh4. Species\r\n\r\n* [[bighorn_sheep]]\r\n* [[dall_sheep]]\r\n* [[domestic_sheep]]\r\n** [[barbados_blackbelly]]\r\n** [[bluefaced_leicester]]\r\n** [[border_leicester]]\r\n** [[cheviot_sheep]]\r\n** [[clun_forest_sheep]]\r\n** [[corriedale]]\r\n** [[dorset_down]]\r\n** [[greyface_dartmoor]]\r\n** [[hampshire_down]]\r\n** [[herdwick]]\r\n** [[hill_radnor]]\r\n** [[icelandic_sheep]]\r\n** [[jacob_sheep]]\r\n** [[kerry_hill_sheep]]\r\n** [[lincoln_sheep]]\r\n** [[merino_sheep]]\r\n** [[navajo-churro]]\r\n** [[oxford_down]]\r\n** [[rough_fell]]\r\n** [[scottish_blackface]]\r\n** [[shropshire_sheep]]\r\n** [[southdown_sheep]]\r\n** [[valais_blacknose]]\r\n** [[welsh_mountain_sheep]]\r\n* [[wild_sheep]]\r\n** [[mouflon]]\r\n** [[urial]]\r\n\r\nh6. Fictional species\r\n\r\n* [[sheep_humanoid]]\r\n* [[shee