                      "wiki_body": "thumb #387128\r\n\r\nThe [b]Chihuahua[/b] is the smallest recognized breed of [[dog]], and is named after the state of Chihuahua in Mexico. However, its exact origins are convoluted and shrouded in mystery.\r\n\r\nThe Chihuahua comes in a variety of colours, with two coat lengths. Dogs of either coat type may be referred to as \"apple head\" or \"deer head\" Chihuahuas. Apple heads have rounded heads, close-set eyes, and relatively short ears and legs. Deer heads have flat-topped heads, more widely set eyes, larger ears, and longer, more slender legs. While deer heads were popular in the mid-20th century, current breed standards defined by registries such as the AKC specify the apple head conformation. Chihuahuas can live anywhere between 12-20 years on average, depending on their types.\r\n\r\nChihuahuas are often seen in shows. Kennels do not generally specify a height for Chihuahuas; only their weight range and overall proportions. Although Chihuahua puppies can vary in size, even amongst the same litter, show dogs are generally between 15-25 cm at the shoulder and 1.8-2.7 kg, though non-show pet Chihuahuas can often be larger. Larger Chihuahua females also generally have less problems related to giving birth.\r\n\r\nAlthough Chihuahuas were once primarily bred as a food source and were unknown to much of the world, they are far more common nowadays, and often seen as lapdogs or showdogs, with a fair few celebrities owning or having owned Chihuahuas. There are still countries that breed them for food, however.\r\n\r\nChihuahuas are often subsceptible to \"small dog syndrome\", in which owners feel they do not need the training and socialisation often given to larger dogs, and as a result, the dog develops behaviour problems. However, early training can overcome this, though their temperament often depends on the genetics of their parents or grandparents and thus varies between individuals. \r\n\r\nSome examples of famous Chihuahuas are [[ren_hoek|Ren Hoek]] from [i][[ren_and_stimpy|Ren and Stimpy]][/i], [[shirley_the_medium|Shirley the Medium]] from [i][[courage_the_cowardly_dog|Courage the Cowardly Dog]][/i], and   [[mammoth_mutt|Mammoth Mutt]] from [i][[krypto_the_superdog|Krypto the Wonderdog]][/i]. In addition, works of fiction will often depict rich ladies with evil or bad-tempered Chihuahuas.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[domestic_dog]]\r\n** [[toy_dog]]\r\n*** [[apso]]\r\n**** [[lhasa apso]]\r\n**** [[tibetan_terrier]]\r\n*** [[bichon]]\r\n**** [[bichon_frise]]\r\n*** [[brussels_griffon]]\r\n*** [[cavalier_king_charles_spaniel]]\r\n*** chihuahua\r\n*** [[chinese_crested_dog]]\r\n*** [[japanese_chin]]\r\n*** [[löwchen]]\r\n*** [[maltese]]\r\n*** [[papillon]]\r\n*** [[pekingese]]\r\n*** [[russkiy_toy]]\r\n*** [[shih_tzu]]\r\n*** [[small_molosser]]\r\n**** [[boston_terrier]]\r\n**** [[french_bulldog]]\r\n**** [[pug]]\r\n*** [[tibetan_spaniel]]\r\n*** [[toy_fox_terrier]]\r\n*** [[toy_poodle]]"
                    }
                  ],
                  "cavalier_king_charles_spaniel": [
                    {
                      "tag": "cavalier_king_charles_spaniel",
                      "kor": "",
                      "count": 888,
                      "wiki_preview": "The [b]Cavalier King Charles Spaniel[/b] is a small spaniel classed as a toy [[dog]] by The Kennel C...",
                      "wiki_body": "The [b]Cavalier King Charles Spaniel[/b] is a small spaniel classed as a toy [[dog]] by The Kennel Club and the American Kennel Club, that originated in the United Kingdom. It has a silky, smooth coat and commonly a smooth undocked tail. It was bred as a companion dog. Until the 1920s, the Cavalier shared the same history as the smaller King Charles Spaniel. Breeders attempted to recreate what they considered to be the original configuration of the breed, a dog resembling Charles II's King Charles Spaniel of the Restoration.\r\n\r\nThe breed is generally friendly, affectionate and good with both children and other animals and breeds of dog, but requires a lot of human interaction. Being a family-oriented dog, leaving them alone for long periods of time is discouraged.\r\n\r\nAn urban legend claims that Charles II issued a special decree granting King Charles Spaniels permission to enter any establishment in the UK, overriding \"no dog except guide dogs\" rules. A variant of this myth relates specifically to the Houses of Parliament. This myth is sometimes instead applied to the Cavalier King Charles Spaniel. The website for the UK Government disproves this, however.\r\n\r\nCavalier King Charles Spaniels have four coat colours: Blenheim (brown/chestnut and white), black and tan (black and brown), ruby (solid brown), and tricolour (black, white and brown)\r\n\r\n[b]Notable characters[/b]\r\n[[Zoe_Trent]]: A purple Cavalier King Charles Spaniel from [[Littlest_Pet_Shop]]."
                    }
                  ],
