                  "wiki_body": "The [b]raccoon dog[/b] ([i]Nyctereutes procyonoides[/i]) is a [[canid]] indigenous to East Asia and Japan while introduced population live in Eastern Europe.\r\n\r\nIn Japan, they have a folkloric significance and are often popular as statues.  Males are depicted as having large [[balls]].\r\n\r\nThey are not related to either [[badger|badgers]] or [[raccoon|raccoons]], both of which they are often mistaken for by the West. Their closest living relatives are in fact the [i]Vulpes[/i] genus, or true [[fox|foxes]], which tanuki are considered to be rivals of in Japanese folklore.\r\n\r\nPlease note: The most well-known variety, the Japanese raccoon dog (tanuki), is one of several subspecies of raccoon dog and is not synonymous with the species as a whole.\r\n\r\nh4. Subspecies\r\n* [[chinese raccoon dog]]\r\n* [[tanuki|japanese raccoon dog]] - Variant most closely associated with the term \"Tanuki\".\r\n* [[korean raccoon dog]]\r\n* [[yunnan raccoon dog]]\r\n* [[ussuri raccoon dog]]\r\n\r\nh4. More information:\r\n* \"Raccoon Dog (Wikipedia)\":http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raccoon_dog\r\n* \"Tanuki (Wikipedia)\":http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_raccoon_dog"
