            "wiki_preview": "The [b]donkey[/b] ([i]Equus africanus asinus[/i]) is a domesticated member of the Equidae or [[horse...",
            "wiki_body": "The [b]donkey[/b] ([i]Equus africanus asinus[/i]) is a domesticated member of the Equidae or [[horse]] family.  The wild ancestor of the donkey is the African wild ass ([i]E. africanus[/i]).  In the western United States, a small donkey is sometimes called a burro (from the Spanish word for the animal) or a mountain canary due to its [i]hee-haw[/i] \"song\". \n\nA male donkey or ass is called a jack, a female a jenny, and an offspring less than one year old a foal (males are colts, female are fillies).\n\nWhile different species of the Equidae family can interbreed, the offspring are almost always sterile.  Nonetheless, horse/donkey hybrids are popular for their durability and vigor.  A mule is the offspring of a jack and a mare.  The much rarer successful mating of a stallion and a jenny produces a hinny.\n\nh4. Genera and species:\n* [[equine|equus]] (genus)\n** [[asinus]] (subgenus)\n*** [[african_wild_ass]] (E. africanus)\n**** [[nubian_wild_ass]] (E. a. africanus)\n**** [[somali_wild_ass]] (E. a. somaliensis)\n**** [[atlas_wild_ass]] (E. a. atlanticus, extinct)\n**** donkey (E. a. asinus)\n\nh3. For more information:\n\n* \"Donkey (Wikipedia)\":http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donkey\n* \"Mule (Wikipedia)\":http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mule"
