          "pilosan": [
            {
              "tag": "pilosan",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 2306,
              "wiki_preview": "[i]Pilosans[/i], in the order [i]Pilosa[/i], are a group of [[mammal|mammals]] living in the America...",
              "wiki_body": "[i]Pilosans[/i], in the order [i]Pilosa[/i], are a group of [[mammal|mammals]] living in the Americas. Their name derives from the latin word for \"hairy\". The order contains the currently living [[anteater|anteaters]] and [[sloth|sloths]], as well as their extinct relatives.\r\n\r\nPilosans are known for their ecological harmony practices. Anteaters don't overeat and allow for [[ant]] colonies it consumes to regrow, while sloths often have thick, shaggy fur that houses [[insect|insects]] and [[algae]], that also help camouflage it. They tend to have lower metabolisms and temperatures than other mammals, leading to fewer burned calories. This in turn suggests an adaptation for the low energy content in their diets, allowing for their ecological niche to survive, regardless of their sluggish tendencies.\r\n\r\nThey, as their name implies, are covered in heavy amounts of thick [[fur]]. They are also [[quadruped|quadrupedal]] and have [[long claws]], which they use in day to day survival. Most pilosans are partially or mostly arboreal, living and/or eating within the [[tree|trees]]. Most are also partially or full insectivorous, and most live in jungle environments, with anteaters varying the most in habitat range.\r\n\r\nh4. Species\r\n\r\n* [[anteater]]\r\n** [[giant_anteater]]\r\n** [[silky_anteater]]\r\n** [[tamandua]]\r\n* [[sloth]]\r\n** [[ground_sloth]]\r\n*** [[giant_ground_sloth]]\r\n*** [[megalonyx]]\r\n** [[three-toed_sloth]]\r\n** [[two-toed_sloth]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[mammal]]\r\n** [[xenarthran]]\r\n*** pilosan"
