                "wiki_body": "[i]Caviids[/i], members of [i]Caviidae[/i] and also known as the [b]cavy family[/b], are a family of [[rodent|rodents]] native to South America. [i]Caviids[/i] are found in habitats ranging from [[marsh|marshy]], [[tropical]] floodplain to [[desolate|dry, rocky, high-altitude meadows]]. Some species live in open [[grassland|grasslands]], others are found in semiarid grasslands, barren zones, or in the dry steppes and high [[mountain|mountains]] of Peru and Argentina.\r\n\r\nCavies have benefited [[human|humans]] for thousands of years. The domestic [[guinea pig]], now found worldwide in captivity, has been bred for [[meat]] for more than three thousand years in South America. It is also utilized commonly for animal testing and is also used widely as a [[pet]].\r\n\r\nWith the exception of the [[mara|maras]], which have a more [[lagomorph]]-like appearance, [i]caviids[/i] have short, heavy bodies and large heads. Most have no visible tails. They range in size from small species that reach 22 cm in body length up to the [[capybara]], the largest of all rodents at 106 to 134 cm in length. \r\n\r\nh4. Species\r\n\r\n* [[capybara]]\r\n* [[guinea_pig]]\r\n* [[mara]]\r\n** [[patagonian_mara]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[mammal]]\r\n** [[rodent]]\r\n*** caviid"
              }
