              "count": 157,
              "wiki_preview": "thumb #988403 thumb #1768637 thumb #1768636\r\n\r\nSynapsida: A clade including mammals and animals more...",
              "wiki_body": "thumb #988403 thumb #1768637 thumb #1768636\r\n\r\nSynapsida: A clade including mammals and animals more closely related to mammals than to non-mammalian amniotes, defined by the presence of a \"temporal fenestra\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skull#Fenestrae in the skull behind each eye.\r\n\r\nFor the sake of tagging this should not tagged on [[mammal]]s, but can be applied to other members of this clade whom do not fit into any other category.  An example of this is the [[dimetrodon]], a [[pelycosaur]] from the permian period (before the age of dinosaurs) and often mistaken as a dinosaur.  It is worth noting that just because a name ends in -saurus it does not automatically mean it is a dinosaur.  An example is the \"secodontosaurus\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secodontosaurus, another sphenacodontid pelycosaur from the permian period and in the same family as dimetrodon.\r\n\r\nh4. Groups, families, and species:\r\n* [[synapsid]]\r\n** [[eupelycosaur]]\r\n*** [[edaphosaurid]]\r\n**** [[edaphosaurus]]\r\n**** [[ianthasaurus]]\r\n** [[pelycosaur]]\r\n*** [[sphenacodontid]]\r\n**** [[dimetrodon]]\r\n\r\nTrivia: [[Reptile]]s and [[bird]]s are \"diapsid\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diapsid while primitive reptiles and \"parareptiles\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parareptilia were \"anapsid\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anapsid.\r\n\r\nh4. More information:\r\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synapsid"
