            "wiki_body": "[i]Lepidopterans[/i], members of the order [i]Lepidoptera[/i], are the second largest order of [[insect|insects]]. They have developed heads with [[mandibles|chewing mouthparts]], have [[multi leg|multiple legs]], relatively large [[wings]] for their body size, and [[scales]] upon their wings. Nearly all lepidopteran [[larva|larvae]] are called [[caterpillar|caterpillars]].\r\n\r\nTheir [[butterfly wings|distinctive, large wings]], which are covered with overlapping scales, often have distinctive and bright color patterns that they use for courtship and intimidation, with characteristics such as [[eyespots]]. Many lepidopterans have tubular [[proboscis]] that lie coiled beneath their heads.  This proboscis acts as a siphon tube for sipping liquid nutrients, such as nectar, from things such as [[flower|flowers]].\r\n\r\nFrom a taxonomic standpoint, the distinction between moths and butterflies is largely artificial; some moths are more similar to butterflies than to other moths.  As a rule, butterflies are diurnal and tend to be very colorful, whereas moths are mostly nocturnal and often come with more drab color schemes.\r\n\r\nh4. Species\r\n\r\n* [[butterfly]]\r\n** [[blue_morpho]]\r\n** [[hairstreak_(butterfly)]]\r\n** [[monarch_butterfly]]\r\n** [[pale_clouded_yellow]]\r\n** [[small_cabbage_white]]\r\n** [[swallowtail_butterfly]]\r\n*** [[black_swallowtail]]\r\n*** [[emerald_swallowtail]]\r\n*** [[tiger_swallowtail]]\r\n* [[moth]]\r\n** [[flannel_moth]]\r\n** [[hawk_moth]]\r\n*** [[death's-head_hawkmoth]]\r\n*** [[pellucid_hawk_moth]]\r\n** [[luna_moth]]\r\n** [[rosy_maple_moth]]\r\n** [[tiger_moth]]\r\n*** [[giant_leopard_moth]]\r\n** [[venezuelan_poodle_moth]]\r\n\r\nh4. General anatomy\r\n\r\n* [[abdomen]]\r\n* [[antennae]]\r\n* [[compound_eyes]]\r\n* [[eyespots]]\r\n* [[mandibles]]\r\n* [[proboscis]]\r\n* [[wings]]\r\n** [[butterfly_wings]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[ambient_insect]]\r\n** [[ambient_butterfly]]\r\n** [[ambient_moth]]\r\n* [[arthropod]]\r\n** [[insect]]\r\n*** lepidopteran"
