            "wiki_preview": "The [b][color=species]Indian python[/color][/b] ([b][i][color=species]Python molurus[/color][/i][/b]...",
            "wiki_body": "The [b][color=species]Indian python[/color][/b] ([b][i][color=species]Python molurus[/color][/i][/b]) is a species of large, non-venomous, true pythons that is the type species of the genus [i][[Python (genus)|Python]][/i] and family [[python|Pythonidae]]. It is also known by other common names such as the [b][color=species]black-tailed python[/color][/b], the [b][color=species]Asian rock python[/color][/b], or the [b][color=species]Indian rock python[/color][/b].\r\n\r\nAs its common name would imply, the Indian python is native to nearly all of the Indian subcontinent. As a python, the species are ambush hunters who [[biting|bite]] prey to hold on to them before [[coiling]] their [[serpentine]] bodies around their victim — both of which can be rather large as adult Indian python average at three-meters-long (9 ft. 10 in.) and weigh in the tens of kilograms, making them one of the largest snakes overall — to constrict the [[lung|lungs]] and [[vein|veins]] until the prey suffocates or the [[heart (organ)|heart]] stops. Females are oviparous, laying clutches of up to 100 [[egg|eggs]] in nests they lay in, incubating them constantly until her 45–60-centimeter-long (18–24 in.) hatchlings emerge.\r\n\r\nThe Indian python was originally and erroneously classified by Carl Linnaeus as a [[boa (snake)|boa]] in 1758, as [i]Boa molura[/i], but was resorted into its own genus in 1803 by François Marie Daudin, who used [i]Python molurus[/i] as the defining species. The closely related [[Burmese python]] ([i]Python bivittatus[/i]) was considered a subspecies of the Indian python from 1820 until 2009.\r\n\r\nThe most famous depiction of an Indian python is the character [[Kaa (Jungle Book)|Kaa]], from Rudyard Kipling's story book [i][[The Jungle Book]][/i] (1894) that has been widely adapted, with the 1967 animated film by [[Disney]] reinterpreting Kaa as an antagonist to reflect negative connotations of snakes in the Western Hemisphere.\r\n\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[reptile]] (class: Reptilia)\r\n** [[snake]] (suborder: Serpentes)\r\n*** [[alethinophidian]] (infraorder: Alethinophidia)\r\n**** [[afrophidian]] (clade: Afrophidia)\r\n***** [[python|pythonid]] (family: Pythonidae)\r\n****** [i][[Python (genus)|Python]][/i]\r\n******* [b]Indian python ([i]Python molurus[/i])[/b]\r\n\r\nh4.External links\r\n* Wikipedia — \"Indian python\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_python"
          },
