          "dromaeosaurid": {
            "_Self (dromaeosaurid)": [
              {
                "tag": "dromaeosaurid",
                "kor": "",
                "count": 14170,
                "wiki_preview": "[b]Dromaeosauridae[/b] is a family of [[feathers|feathered]] [[theropod]] [[dinosaur|dinosaurs]]. Th...",
                "wiki_body": "[b]Dromaeosauridae[/b] is a family of [[feathers|feathered]] [[theropod]] [[dinosaur|dinosaurs]]. They were generally small to medium-sized feathered carnivores that flourished in the Cretaceous Period. The name Dromaeosauridae means 'running lizards', from Greek δρομεῦς (dromeus) meaning 'runner' and σαῦρος (sauros) meaning 'lizard'. In informal usage they are often called [b]raptors[/b] (after [i][[Velociraptor]][/i]), a term popularized by the film [i][[Jurassic Park]][/i]; a few types include the term \"raptor\" directly in their name and have come to emphasize their [[bird]]-like appearance and speculated bird-like behavior.\r\n\r\nThe distinctive dromaeosaurid body plan helped to rekindle theories that dinosaurs may have been active, fast, and closely related to birds. Robert Bakker's illustration for John Ostrom's 1969 monograph, showing the dromaeosaurid [i][[Deinonychus]][/i] in a fast run, is among the most influential paleontological reconstructions in history. The dromaeosaurid body plan includes a relatively large skull, [[sharp teeth|serrated teeth]], narrow snout (an exception being [i][[Utahraptor]][/i]), and forward-facing eyes which indicate some degree of binocular vision.\r\n\r\nDromaeosaurid fossils have been found across the globe in North America, Europe, Africa, Asia, South America and Antarctica, with fossilized teeth giving credence to the possibility that they inhabited Australia as well. They first appeared in the mid-Jurassic Period (late Bathonian stage, about 167 million years ago) and survived until the end of the Cretaceous (Maastrichtian stage, 66 ma), existing until the Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event.\r\n\r\n(source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dromaeosauridae)"
              }
            ],
            "velociraptor": [
              {
                "tag": "velociraptor",
                "kor": "",
                "count": 7180,
                "wiki_preview": "[b][i]Velociraptor[/i][/b] (commonly shortened to \"[b]raptor[/b]\") is a genus of dromaeosaurid thero...",
                "wiki_body": "[b][i]Velociraptor[/i][/b] (commonly shortened to \"[b]raptor[/b]\") is a genus of dromaeosaurid theropod dinosaur that existed approximately 75 to 71 million years ago during the later part of the Cretaceous Period. Two species are currently recognized, although others have been assigned in the past. The type species is [i]V. mongoliensis[/i]; fossils of this species have been discovered in Mongolia. A second species, [i]V. osmolskae[/i], was named in 2008 for skull material from Inner Mongolia, China.\r\n\r\nSmaller than other dromaeosaurids like [i]Deinonychus[/i] and [i]Achillobator[/i], [i]Velociraptor[/i] nevertheless shared many of the same anatomical features. It was a bipedal, feathered carnivore with a long, stiffened tail and an enlarged sickle-shaped claw on each hindfoot, which is thought to have been used to kill its prey. [i]Velociraptor[/i] can be distinguished from other dromaeosaurids by its long and low skull, with an upturned snout."
              }
            ],
