                  "wiki_body": "[b][color=species]Rattlesnakes[/color][/b] are venomous [[pit viper]] [[snake|snakes]] in the genus [b][i][color=species]Crotalus[/color][/i][/b], named for their distinct [[rattle (anatomy)|keratinous rattles]] grown on the end of their tails. They are the type genus of the subfamily Crotalinae.\r\n\r\nAs of 2020, there are over fifty species sorted into in [i]Crotalus[/i], the largest genus in the entire Viperidae family (rivaled only by [i]Bothrops[/i] and [i]Trimeresurus[/i]).\r\n[section,expanded=Species]\r\n* [[eastern diamondback rattlesnake]] — [i]Crotalus adamanteus[/i] (Ambrose Palisot de Beauvois, 1799)\r\n* Ángel de la Guarda Island speckled rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus angelensis[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1963)\r\n* Querétaro dusky rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus aquilus[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1952)\r\n* western dusky rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus armstrongi[/i] (Johnathan A. Campbell, 1979)\r\n* [[western diamondback rattlesnake]] — [i]Crotalus atrox[/i] (Spencer F. Baird & C. Frédéric Girard, 1853)\r\n* Mexican west coast rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus basiliscus[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1864)\r\n* Campbell's rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus campbelli[/i] (Robert W. Bryson Jr. [i]et al.[/i], 2014)\r\n* Santa Catalina rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus catalinensis[/i] (Frank S. Cliff, 1954)\r\n* [[sidewinder]] — [i]Crotalus cerastes[/i] (Edward Hallowell, 1854)\r\n** Mojave Desert sidewinder — [i]Crotalus cerastes cerastes[/i] (Edward Hallowell, 1854)\r\n** Sonoran Desert sidewinder — [i]Crotalus cerastes cercobombus[/i] (Jay Mathers Savage & Frank S. Cliff, 1953)\r\n** Colorado Desert sidewinder — [i]laterorepens[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1944)\r\n* Arizona black rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus cerberus[/i] (Elliott Ladd Coues, 1875)\r\n* midget faded rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus concolor[/i] (Angus Munn Woodbury, 1929)\r\n* northwestern neotropical rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus culminatus[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1952)\r\n* [[South American rattlesnake]] — [i]Crotalus durissus[/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n** Venezuelan rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus durissus cumanensis[/i] (Alexander von Humboldt, 1833)\r\n** Guiana rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus durissus durissus[/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n** Marajon rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus durissus marajoensis[/i] (Alphonse Richard Hoge, 1966)\r\n** [i]Crotalus durissus maricelae[/i] (J.E. García-Pérez, 1995)\r\n** [i]Crotalus durissus ruruima[/i] (Alphonse Richard Hoge, 1966)\r\n** [i]Crotalus durissus terrificus[/i] (Josephus Nicolaus Laurenti, 1768)\r\n** [i]Crotalus durissus trigonicus[/i] (Herbert S. Harris Jr. &  Robert S. Simmons, 1978)\r\n* Tehuantepec Isthmus neotropical rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus ehecatl[/i] (Rubén Alonso Carbajal-Marquez [i]et al.[/i], 2020)\r\n* [[Baja California rattlesnake]] — [i]Crotalus enyo[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1861)\r\n** Cerralvo Island rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus enyo cerralvensis[/i] (Frank S. Cliff, 1954)\r\n** Lower California rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus enyo enyo[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1861)\r\n** Rosario rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus enyo furvus[/i] (C. Herbert Lowe Jr. & Kenneth S. Norris, 1954)\r\n* Guerreran long-tailed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus ericsmithi[/i] (Johnathan A. Campbell & Oscar Flores-Villela, 2008)\r\n* San Esteban Island rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus estebanensis[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1949)\r\n* black diamond rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus helleri[/i] (Seth Eugene Meek, 1905)\r\n* [[timber rattlesnake]] — [i]Crotalus horridus[/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n* Mexican small-headed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus intermedius[/i] (Franz Hermann Troschel, 1865)\r\n** Totalcan small-headed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus intermedius intermedius[/i] (Franz Hermann Troschel, 1865)\r\n** Oaxacan small-headed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus intermedius gloydi[/i] (Edward Harrison Taylor, 1941)\r\n** Omilteman small-headed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus intermedius omiltemanus[/i] (Albert K.L.G. Günther, 1895)\r\n* Autlán rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus lannomi[/i] (Wilmer Webster Tanner, 1966)\r\n* [[rock rattlesnake]] — [i]Crotalus lepidus[/i] (Robert Kennicott, 1861)\r\n** banded rock rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus lepidus klauberi[/i] (Howard Kay Gloyd, 1936)\r\n** mottled rock rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus lepidus lepidus[/i] (Robert Kennicott, 1861)\r\n** Durango rock rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus lepidus maculosus[/i] (Wilmer Webster Tanner, James R. Dixon, & Herbert S. Harris; 1972)\r\n** Tamaulipan rock rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus lepidus morulus[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1952)\r\n* San Lorenzo Island rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus lorenzoensis[/i] (Charles W. Radcliffe & T. Paul Maslin, 1975)\r\n* Great Basin rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus lutosus[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1930)\r\n* Veracruz neotropical rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus mictlantecuhtli[/i] (Carbajal-Marquez 2020)\r\n* Mitchell's rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus mitchellii[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1861)\r\n** San Lucan speckled rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus mitchellii mitchellii[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1861)\r\n** El Muerto Island speckled rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus mitchellii muertensis[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1949)\r\n* [[black-tailed rattlesnake]] — [i]Crotalus molossus[/i] (Spencer F. Baird & C. Frédéric Girard, 1853)\r\n** Northern black-tailed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus molossus molossus[/i] (Spencer F. Baird & C. Frédéric Girard, 1853)\r\n** Mexican black-tailed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus molossus nigrescens[/i] (Howard Kay Gloyd, 1936)\r\n** Oaxacan black-tailed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus molossus oaxacus[/i] (Howard Kay Gloyd, 1948)\r\n* Tamaulipan rock rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus morulus[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1952)\r\n* [[western rattlesnake]] — [i]Crotalus oreganus[/i] (John Edwards Holbrook, 1840)\r\n* eastern black-tailed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus ornatus[/i] (Edward Hallowell, 1854)\r\n* Horsehead Island speckled rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus polisi[/i] (Jesse Meik [i]et al.[/i], 2018)\r\n* Mexican lance-headed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus polystictus[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1865)\r\n* twin-spotted rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus pricei[/i] (John Van Denburgh, 1895)\r\n** eastern twin-spotted rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus pricei miquihuanus[/i] (Howard Kay Gloyd, 1940)\r\n** western twin-spotted rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus pricei pricei[/i] (John Van Denburgh, 1895)\r\n* Tancitaran dusky rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus pusillus[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1952)\r\n* southwestern speckled rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus pyrrhus[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1867)\r\n* Mexican pygmy rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus ravus[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1865)\r\n** Oaxacan pygmy rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus ravus brunneus[/i] (Herbert S. Harris & Robert S. Simmons, 1977)\r\n** Guerreran pygmy rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus ravus exiguus[/i] (Johnathan A. Campbell & Armstrong, 1979)\r\n** Central Mexican pygmy rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus ravus ravus[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1865)\r\n* [[red diamond rattlesnake]] — [i]Crotalus ruber[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1892)\r\n** Cedros Island red diamond rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus ruber exsui[/i] (Samuel Walton Garman, 1884)\r\n** San Lucan diamond rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus ruber lucasensis[/i] (John Van Denburgh, 1920)\r\n** [i]Crotalus ruber ruber[/i] (Edward Drinker Cope, 1892)\r\n* Mojave rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus scutulatus[/i] (Robert Kennicott, 1861)\r\n** Huamantlan rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus scutulatus salvini[/i] (Albert K.L.G. Günther, 1895)\r\n** Northern Mojave rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus scutulatus scutulatus[/i] (Robert Kennicott, 1861)\r\n* Central American rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus simus[/i] (Pierre André Latreille, 1801)\r\n* Sinaloan long-tailed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus stejnegeri[/i] (Emmett Reid Dunn, 1919)\r\n* Panamint rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus stephensi[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1930)\r\n* Tancitaro rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus tancitarensis[/i] (Javier Alvarado-Díaz & Johnathan A. Campbell, 2004)\r\n* Isla Piojo speckled rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus thalassoporus[/i] (Jesse Meik [i]et al.[/i], 2018)\r\n* tiger rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus tigris[/i] (Robert Kennicott, 1859)\r\n* Tláloc's rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus tlaloci[/i] (Bryson [i]et al.[/i], 2014)\r\n* Totonacan rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus totonacus[/i] (Howard Kay Gloyd & Carl Frederick Kauffeld, 1940)\r\n* cross-banded mountain rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus transversus[/i] (Edward Harrison Taylor, 1944)\r\n* Mexican dusky rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus triseriatus[/i] (Johann Georg Wagler, 1830)\r\n** [i]Crotalus triseriatus armstrongi[/i] (Johnathan A. Campbell, 1979)\r\n** [i]Crotalus triseriatus triseriatus[/i] (Johann Georg Wagler, 1830)\r\n* Yucatán neotropical rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus tzabcan[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1952)\r\n* Aruba rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus unicolor[/i] (T.W. van Lidth de Jeude, 1887)\r\n* Uracoan rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus vegrandis[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1941)\r\n* prairie rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus viridis[/i] (Constantine S. Rafinesque-Schmaltz, 1818)\r\n** Hopi rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus viridis nuntius[/i] (Laurence Monroe Klauber, 1935)\r\n** [i]Crotalus viridis viridis[/i] (Constantine S. Rafinesque-Schmaltz, 1818)\r\n* Willard's ridge-nosed rattlesnake — [i]Crotalus willardi[/i] (Seth Eugene Meek, 1905)\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nThe species of the related genus [i]Sistrurus[/i] are sometimes also called rattlesnakes; those three species are also called ground rattlesnakes, pygmy rattlesnakes, or massasaugas to distinguish from the true rattlesnakes of [i]Crotalus[/i].\r\n\r\nh4. See also \r\n* [[reptile]] (class: Reptilia)\r\n** [[snake]] (suborder: Serpentes)\r\n*** [[alethinophidian]] (infradorder: Alethinophidia)\r\n**** [[afrophidian]] ([i]clade[/i]: Afrophidia)\r\n***** [[caenophidian]] ([i]clade[/i]: Caenophidia)\r\n****** [[colubroid]] ([i]clade[/i]: Colubroides)\r\n******* [[viper]] (family: Viperidae)\r\n******** [[pit viper]] (subfamily: Crotalinae)\r\n********* [b]rattlesnake ([i]Crotalus[/i])[/b]\r\n\r\nh4. External links\r\n* Wikipedia \r\n** \"[i]Crotalus[/i]\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crotalus\r\n** \"rattlesnake\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rattlesnake"
