              "wiki_body": "[b][color=species]Pythonidae[/color][/b] is a taxonomic family of non-venomous alethinophid [[snake|snakes]] with the common name of [b][color=species]pythons[/color][/b] and the collateral adjective of [b][color=species]pythonid[/color][/b].\r\n\r\nThe snakes in the Pythonidae family are native to the tropics and subtropics of the sub-Saharan Africa, Southeast Asia, and Australia (however there are a couple of infamous invasive populations of certain species in [[Puerto Rico]] and the [[Florida]] Everglades). As they do not have venom glands, pythons use the act of [[biting]] solely to grab their ambushed prey as they suffocate them via constriction. Superficially similar to [[boa (snake)|boas]], with Pythonidae once being misidentified as subfamily of Boidae (the former defined by Austrian zoologist Leopold Fitzinger in 1826), pythons are physically and evolutionarily distinct; unlike boas, they are oviparous and do not have as elongated supratemporal and quadrate bones.\r\n\r\nAs of 2024, there are currently 39 extant species recognized in Pythonidae across ten genii, including the type genus [i]Python[/i] from which these snakes are named (etymologically, the Latin [i]python[/i] comes from the Ancient Greek [i]Πύθων[/i] (the name of a [[mythological scalie]] from Delphi slain by the god [[Apollo (Greek mythology)|Apollo]]).\r\n\r\n[section,expanded=Species]\r\n* [i]Antaresia[/i] (Richard W. Wells & Cliff R. Wellington, 1984)\r\n** Children's python — [i]Antaresia childreni[/i] (John Edward Gray, 1842)\r\n** spotted python — [i]Antaresia maculosa[/i] (Wilhelm K.H. Peters, 1873)\r\n** Papuan spotted python — [i]Antaresia papuensis[/i] (Damien Esquerré [i]et al.[/i], 2021)\r\n** pygmy python — [i]Antaresia perthensis[/i] (Olive G. Stull, 1932)\r\n* [i]Apodora[/i] (Arnold G. Klugg, 1993)\r\n** Papuan python — [i]Apodora papuana[/i] (Wilhelm K.H. Peters & Giacomo Doria, 1878)\r\n* [i]Aspidites[/i] (Wilhelm K.H. Peters, 1877)\r\n** black-headed python — [i]Aspidites [/i] (Gerard Krefft, 1864)\r\n** woma python — [i]Aspidites ramsayi[/i] (William J. Macleay, 1882)\r\n* [i]Bothrochilus[/i] (Leopold J.F.J. Fitzinger, 1843)\r\n** Bismarck ringed python — [i]Bothrochilus boa[/i] (Hermann Schlegel, 1837)\r\n* [i][[white-lipped python|Leiopython]][/i] (Ambrosius A.W. Hubrecht, 1879)\r\n** D'Albertis' python — [i]Leiopython albertisii[/i] (Wilhelm K.H. Peters & Giacomo Doria, 1878)\r\n** Karimui Basin whitelip python — [i]Leiopython fredparkeri[/i] (Wulf D. Schleip, 2008)\r\n** Biak white-lipped python — [i]Leiopython biakensis[/i] (Wulf D. Schleip, 2008)\r\n* [i]Liasis[/i] (John Edward Gray, 1842)\r\n** [[water python]] — [i]Liasis fuscus[/i] (Wilhelm K.H. Peters, 1873)\r\n** Macklot's python — [i]Liasis mackloti[/i] (André M.C. Duméril & Gabriel Bibron, 1844)\r\n** olive python — [i]Liasis olivaceus[/i] (John Edward Gray, 1842)\r\n* [i]Malayopython[/i] (R. Graham Reynolds [i]et al.[/i], 2014)\r\n** [[reticulated python]] — [i]Malayopython reticulatus[/i] (Johann G.T. Schneider, 1801)\r\n** [[Timor python]] — [i]Malayopython timoriensis[/i] (Wilhelm K.H. Peters, 1876)\r\n* [i][[Morelia (snake)|Morelia]][/i] (John Edward Gray, 1842)\r\n** northern green tree python — [i]Morelia azurea[/i] (Adolf B. Meyer, 1874)\r\n** Bredl's python — [i]Morelia bredli[/i] (Graeme Francis Gow, 1981)\r\n** [[rough-scaled python]] — [i]Morelia carinata[/i] (Louise Aloys Smith, 1981)\r\n** southwestern carpet python — [i]Morelia imbricata[/i] (Louise Aloys Smith, 1981)\r\n** carpet python — [i]Morelia spilota[/i] (Bernard Germain de Lacépède, 1804)\r\n** [[green tree python]] — [i]Morelia viridis[/i] (Hermann Schlegel, 1872)\r\n* [i]Nyctophilopython[/i] (Richard W. Wells & Cliff R. Wellington, 1985)\r\n** Oenpelli python — [i]Nyctophilopython oenpelliensis[/i] (Graeme Francis Gow, 1977)\r\n* [i][[Python (genus)|Python]][/i] (François Marie Daudin, 1803)\r\n** Anchieta's dwarf python — [i]Python anchietae[/i] (José Vicente Barbosa du Bocage, 1887)\r\n** [[Burmese python]] — [i]Python bivittatus[/i] (Heinrich Kuhl, 1820)\r\n** Borneo python — [i]Python breitensteini[/i] (Franz Steindachner, 1880)\r\n** [[blood python]] — [i]Python brongersmai[/i] (Olive G. Stull, 1938)\r\n** Sumatran short-tailed python — [i]Python curtus[/i] (Hermann Schlegel, 1872)\r\n** Myanmar short-tailed python — [i]Python kyaiktiyo[/i] (George R. Zug, Steve W. Gotte & Jeremy F. Jacobs; 2011)\r\n** [[Indian python]] — [i]Python molurus[/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n** Southern African rock python — [i]Python natalensis[/i] (Andrew Smith, 1833)\r\n** [[ball python]] — [i]Python regius[/i] (George Kearsley Shaw, 1802)\r\n** Central African rock python — [i]Python sebae[/i] (Johann Friedrich Gmelin, 1789)\r\n** †[i]Python europaeus[/i] (Zbigniew Szyndlar & Jean-Claude Rage, 2003)\r\n* [i]Simalia[/i] (John Edward Gray, 1849)\r\n** amethystine python — [i]Simalia amethistina[/i] (Johann G.T. Schneider, 1801)\r\n** Boelen's python — [i]Simalia boeleni[/i] (Leo Daniel Brongersma, 1953)\r\n** Moluccan python — [i]Simalia clastolepis[/i] (Michael B. Harvey [i]et al.[/i], 2000)\r\n** Australian scrub python — [i]Simalia kinghorni[/i] (Olive G. Stull, 1933)\r\n** Tanimbar python — [i]Simalia nauta[/i] (Michael B. Harvey [i]et al.[/i], 2000)\r\n** Halmahera python — [i]Simalia tracyae[/i] (Michael B. Harvey [i]et al.[/i], 2000)\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nDespite its name, the [[Mexican python]] ([i]Loxocemus bicolor[/i]) is not part of the Pythonidae family, however the Loxocemindae and [[sunbeam snake|Xenopeltidae]] families are the closest relatives of Pythonidae, forming the Pythonidea superfamily in the snake infraorder Alethinophida. \r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[reptile]] (class: Reptilia)\r\n** [[snake]] (suborder: Serpentes)\r\n*** [[alethinophidian]] (infraorder: Alethinophidia)\r\n**** [[afrophidian]] (clade: Afrophidia)\r\n***** [b]pythonid (family: Pythonidae)[/b]\r\n\r\nh4.External links\r\n* Wikipedia — \"Pythonidae\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pythonidae"
