            "count": 609,
            "wiki_preview": "[b][i][color=species]Python[/color][/i][/b] is a genus of non-venomous snakes that are the type genu...",
            "wiki_body": "[b][i][color=species]Python[/color][/i][/b] is a genus of non-venomous snakes that are the type genus of the family [[python|Pythonidae]], and thus considered [b][color=species]true pythons[/color][/b]. \r\n\r\nLike other pythons, true pythons are ambush hunters who latch onto their prey with a [[biting|bite]] and [[killing|kill]] by [[coiling]] and constricting until the [[heart (organ)|heart]] or [[lung|lungs]] fail, lay and nest on clutches of eggs, and generally have flecked scales and a long [[forked tongue]]. Size varies between species within a range of a few meters, with the largest [i]Python[/i] being one of the largest species of snakes overall.\r\n\r\nThe various species are native to areas from sub-Saharan Africa to the islands of Southeast Asia, but individuals have been spread worldwide due to some's sale as [[domestic pet|domestic pets]], with an infamous invasive presence arising out of the [[United States]] in the [[Florida]] Everglades.\r\n\r\nAs of 2024, the genus is recognized to contain ten extant species and one [[extinct]], and is the largest genus in Pythonidae.\r\n[section,expanded=Species]\r\n* Anchieta's dwarf python — [i]Python anchietae[/i] (José Vicente Barbosa du Bocage, 1887)\r\n* [[Burmese python]] — [i]Python bivittatus[/i] (Heinrich Kuhl, 1820)\r\n* Borneo python — [i]Python breitensteini[/i] (Franz Steindachner, 1880)\r\n* [[blood python]] — [i]Python brongersmai[/i] (Olive G. Stull, 1938)\r\n* Sumatran short-tailed python — [i]Python curtus[/i] (Hermann Schlegel, 1872)\r\n* Myanmar short-tailed python — [i]Python kyaiktiyo[/i] (George R. Zug, Steve W. Gotte & Jeremy F. Jacobs; 2011)\r\n* [[Indian python]] — [i]Python molurus[/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n* Southern African rock python — [i]Python natalensis[/i] (Andrew Smith, 1833)\r\n* [[ball python]] — [i]Python regius[/i] (George Kearsley Shaw, 1802)\r\n* Central African rock python — [i]Python sebae[/i] (Johann Friedrich Gmelin, 1789)\r\n* †[i]Python europaeus[/i] (Zbigniew Szyndlar & Jean-Claude Rage, 2003)\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nPythons were originally classified as members of the genii [i][[Boa (genus)|Boa]][/i] and [i][[Colubrid|Coluber]][/i] when Swedish biologist Carl Linnaeus defined both taxons in 1758, but [i]B. molura[/i] and [i]C. sebae[/i] were resorted into their current genus [i]Python[/i] by French zoologist François Marie Daudin in 1803, and became the type genus for Pythonidae in 1826 as Austrian zoologist Leopold Fitzinger defined it.\r\n\r\nThe genus' etymology derives from the Ancient Greek [i]Πύθων[/i], the name of a [[mythological scalie]] slain by the god [[Apollo (Greek mythology)|Apollo]] according to the [i]Homeric Hymns[/i].\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[reptile]] (class: Reptilia)\r\n** [[snake]] (suborder: Serpentes)\r\n*** [[alethinophidian]] (infraorder: Alethinophidia)\r\n**** [[afrophidian]] (clade: Afrophidia)\r\n***** [[python|pythonid]] (family: Pythonidae)\r\n****** [b][i]Python[/i][/b]\r\n\r\nh4.External links\r\n* Wikipedia — \"Python (genus)\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(genus)?wprov=sfti1#"
          },
