              "wiki_body": "thumb #1169220 thumb #4279128 thumb #1566139 thumb #777884\r\n\r\nThe [b]lagomorphs[/b] are the members of the taxonomic order [i]Lagomorpha[/i], of which there are two living families: the Leporidae ([[hare|hares]] and [[rabbit|rabbits]]) and the Ochotonidae ([[pika|pikas]]). The name of the order is derived from the Ancient Greek lagṓs (λαγώς, \"hare\") + morphē (μορφή, \"form\"), essentially making the term mean \"rabbit- or hare-shaped\". Lagomorphs share a common ancestor with [[rodent|rodents]], though unlike popular misconception they are not classified as rodents.\r\n\r\nLagomorphs are similar to other [[mammal|mammals]] in that they all have [[fur]] and [[lactate]]. Although lagomorphs are more closely related to rodents than any other mammals (sharing the clade Glires; from Latin [i]glīrēs[/i], \"dormice\"), the two orders still have some major differences. Lagomorphs differ from rodents in that the former have four [[buckteeth|incisors]] in the upper jaw instead of two. Also, lagomorphs are almost strictly herbivorous, unlike rodents, many of which are omnivorous. Similar to rodents, their incisor teeth grow continuously, thus necessitating constant chewing to prevent the teeth from growing too long.\r\n\r\nh4. Species\r\n\r\n* [[lagomorph]] - order Lagomorpha (J.F. von Brandt, 1855)\r\n** [[pika]] - family Ochotonidae (M. Oldfield Thomas, 1897), genus [i]Ochotona[/i] (Johann H.F. Link, 1795)\r\n** [[leporid]] - family Leporidae (Fischer de Waldheim, 1817)\r\n*** [[hare]] - genus [i]Lepus[/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n**** [[Arctic hare]] -  [i]Lepus arcticus[/i] (John Ross, 1819)\r\n**** [[black-tailed jackrabbit]] - [i]Lepus californicus[/i] (John Edward Gray, 1837)\r\n**** [[desert hare]] - [i]Lepus tibetanus[/i] (George R. Waterhouse, 1841)\r\n**** [[European hare]] - [i]Lepus europaeus[/i] (Peter Simon Pallas, 1778)\r\n**** [[Japanese hare]] - [i]Lepus brachyurus[/i] (Coenraad J. Temminck, 1845)\r\n**** [[mountain hare]] - [i]Lepus timidus[/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n**** [[snowshoe hare]] - [i]Lepus americanus[/i] (Johann C.P Erxleben, 1777)\r\n*** [[rabbit]]\r\n**** [i][[Brachylagus]][/i] (Gerrit S. Miller Jr, 1900)\r\n***** [[pygmy rabbit]] - [i]Brachylagus idahoensis[/i] (Clinton Hart Merriam, 1891)\r\n****** [[Columbia Basin pygmy rabbit]]\r\n**** [i][[Oryctolagus]][/i] (Wilhelm Lilljeborg, 1873)\r\n***** [[European rabbit]] - [i]Oryctolagus cuniculus[/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n****** [[domestic rabbit]] - [i]Oryctolagus cuniculus [[domestication|domesticus]][/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n******* [[Angora rabbit]]\r\n******* [[Blanc de Hotot]]\r\n******* [[Dutch rabbit]]\r\n******* [[dwarf rabbit]]\r\n******** [[Dwarf Hotot]]\r\n******** [[Netherland Dwarf rabbit]]\r\n******* [[Flemish giant]]\r\n******* [[harlequin rabbit]]\r\n******* [[lionhead rabbit]]\r\n******* [[lop rabbit]]\r\n******** [[American fuzzy lop]]\r\n******** [[English lop]]\r\n******** [[French lop]]\r\n******** [[Holland lop]]\r\n******* [[Panon rabbit]]\r\n******* [[Rex rabbit]]\r\n******* [[Thrianta rabbit]]\r\n**** [i][[Nesolagus]][/i] (Charles Forsyth-Major, 1899)\r\n***** [[Annamite striped rabbit]] - [i]Nesolagus timminsi[/i] (Alexander O. Averianov; Alexei V. Abramov; & Alexei N. Tikhonov, 2000)\r\n**** [i][[Sylvilagus]][/i] (John Edward Gray, 1867)\r\n***** [[cottontail rabbit]]\r\n****** [[desert cottontail rabbit]] - [i]Sylvilagus audubonii[/i] (Spencer F. Baird, 1858)\r\n****** [[marsh rabbit]] - [i]Sylvilagus palustris[/i] (John Bachman, 1837)\r\n******* [[Lower Keys marsh rabbit]] - [i]Sylvilagus palustris [[Playboy|hefneri]][/i] (James D. Lazell Jr, 1984)\r\n*** †[i][[Nuralagus]][/i] (Josep Quintana; Mike Kohler; Salvador Moya-Sola; 2011)\r\n*** †[i][[Palaeolagus]][/i] (Joseph Mellick Leidy, 1856)\r\n\r\nh6. Fictional/hybrid/mythological species\r\n\r\n* [[al-mi'raj]] - [[Middle Eastern mythology]]\r\n** [[spiked hare]] - [[Dungeon Quest]]\r\n* [[birbit]] - [[bird]] [[rabbit]] [[hybrid]].\r\n* 