            "count": 832,
            "wiki_preview": "The [b]honey badger[/b], or [b]ratel[/b], is a [[mustelid]] that is widely distributed in Africa, So...",
            "wiki_body": "The [b]honey badger[/b], or [b]ratel[/b], is a [[mustelid]] that is widely distributed in Africa, Southwest Asia, and the Indian subcontinent. Although its most common name bears the word \"badger,\" it is more closely related to [[marten|martens]] than it is badgers.\r\n\r\nIt is very recognisable from its distinctive reverse countershaded [[black_fur|black]] and [[white_fur|white]] body, which serves as an honest signal to its strength and ferocity, traits for which it is well-known. It is also famed for its fearlessness, with the 2002 edition of the [i]Guinness Book of World Records[/i] listing it as \"world's most fearless animal\" and the 2011 viral [[YouTube]] video, \"[i]The Crazy Nastyass Honey Badger[/i]\" further popularizing this trait. The latter lead to the memetic spread of the phrase \"honey badger don't care.\"\r\n\r\nThe honey badger's thick, loose skin protects it from the [[bee|bees]] attempting to defend the hives which it raids for the honey and grubs. This skin also helps protect the animal from mid-sized larger predators such as dogs, and its looseness allows the animal to twist around and bite any handler or predator unless grabbed on the back of the neck.\r\n\r\nThe [[honeyguide]], a [[bird]] which eats beeswax, is often rumored to guide honey badgers to bee colonies, but this is false; the behaviour is actually aimed at humans.\r\n\r\nh4. Taxonomy:\r\n* [[musteline|mustelinae]]\r\n** ([i]Mellivora[/i])\r\n*** honey_badger\r\n\r\nh4. More information:\r\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey_badger"
