                "wiki_body": "thumb #318308 thumb #1022226 thumb #2881116 thumb #4386623 thumb #3881985 thumb #942854\n\nA type of [b][[dragon]][/b] seen in [[East Asian mythology]], known as [i]lóng[/i] in Chinese, [i]ryū[/i] in Japanese, [i]ryong[/i] in Korean, and sometimes called a noodle dragon. \n\nThey are characterized by a long [[serpentine]] body, a mustache (or [[flesh whiskers]] / [[barbel_(anatomy)|barbels]]) and [[antlers]]. Eastern dragons tend to also have a more or less [[avian]] style foot structure, and if they have [[scales]] they will sometimes be fish-like rather than reptilian. Dragons of this type are additionally usually [[wingless_dragon|wingless]] yet still capable of [[flight]]. \n\nWith few exceptions, eastern dragons are traditionally benevolent and regarded as [[deity|deities]]. In mythology, they are often personifications of elements (e.g. water, light) or geological forms & conditions (e.g. rain, mountains). Eastern dragons are additionally strongly associated with both luck and nobility. These associations tend to be visualized as extra digits on the feet(and/or wings) or additional sets of legs beyond the usual two pairs in older media.\n\nWhile eastern dragons tend to be benevolent, there are rare examples of malevolent ones, a famous one being the Yamata-no-Orochi of [[japanese_mythology|Japanese myth]].\n\nh4. Tags About Dragons\n\n[section=Named species of dragons]\n\nthumb #1526077\n\n* [[amphithere]]\n* [[cockatrice]]\n* eastern_dragon\n* [[hydra]]\n* [[leviathan]]\n* [[lindwurm]]\n* [[kirin]]\n* [[quetzalcoatl]]\n* [[western_dragon]]\n* [[wyrm_(dragon)|wyrm]]\n* [[wyvern]]\n\n[/section]\n\n[section=Types of dragons]\n\n* [[airplane_dragon]]\n* [[bone_dragon]]\n* [[dracolich]]\n* [[dragon_humanoid]] - human form with few draconic features\n* [[elemental_dragon]]\n** [[aquatic_dragon]]\n** [[crystal_dragon]]\n** [[ice_dragon]]\n** [[plant_dragon]]\n*** [[leaf_dragon]]\n* [[fairy_dragon]]\n* [[feathered_dragon]]\n* [[food_drag