              "wiki_body": "thumb #356687 thumb #4602943 thumb #84794 thumb #1428115 thumb #747146 thumb #4602663\n\nA type of [b][[dragon]][/b] seen in [[European mythology]], known as [i]draco[/i] in Latin, known as [i]draig[/i] in Wales and sometimes called a drake.\n\nThey are characterized by a generally [[stocky]] or compact body possessing [[wings]] in addition to arms/forelegs. Western dragons will usually also have [[spikes]]/[[horn|horns]] and a body covered in [[skin]] or [[scales]], as well as a natural [[breath powers|breath power]], such as breathing fire or poison. Western dragons sometimes have opposable thumbs and are capable of switching between [[quadruped]] and [[biped]] locomotion (in [[feral]] bodyform).\n\nWestern dragons have traditionally been depicted as malevolent or chaotic in their nature, but have been increasingly depicted as benevolent in modern media. Regardless of whether they are malevolent or benevolent, a near-universal trait is a penchant for collecting piles of things in a lair. In mythology, they are often personifications of chaos, evil, or the unknown, featuring prominently on old maps and symbols of unforgiving or unexplored environments. They are also popular as [[crest (heraldry)|heraldic]] animals in Europe.\n\n[[Fafnir]] (of Norse mythology) is considered one of the oldest examples of a Western dragon, and influences many aspects of their design even up to the modern day.\n\nh4. Tags About Dragons\n\n[section=Named species of dragons]\n\nthumb #1526077\n\n* [[amphithere]]\n* [[cockatrice]]\n* [[eastern_dragon]]\n* [[hydra]]\n* [[leviathan]]\n* [[lindwurm]]\n* [[kirin]]\n* [[quetzalcoatl]]\n* [b]western_dragon[/b]\n* [[wyrm_(dragon)|wyrm]]\n* [[wyvern]]\n\n[/section]\n\n[section=Types of dragons]\n\n* [[airplane_dragon]]\n* [[bone_dragon]]\n* [[dracolich]]\n* [[dragon_humanoid]] - A humanoid form with few draconic features.\n* [[elemental_dragon]]\n** [[aquatic_dragon]]\n** [[crystal_dragon]]\n** [[ice_dragon]]\n** [[pla