              "wiki_preview": "thumb #1710210 thumb #1618206 thumb #708784\r\n\r\nA [b]kobold[/b] is part of a race of [[short]], draco...",
              "wiki_body": "thumb #1710210 thumb #1618206 thumb #708784\r\n\r\nA [b]kobold[/b] is part of a race of [[short]], draconic humanoids originating from the pen and paper role-playing game, [i][[Dungeons and Dragons|Dungeons & Dragons]][/i].\r\n\r\nThe kobolds of [i]Dungeons & Dragons[/i] were inspired by the kobold sprites of German folklore who were dog-headed goblins that tormented coal miners. Which aside from inheriting their association with mining and sporting a small stature, the kobolds of [i]D&D[/i] have little in common with the original mythology.\r\n\r\n[i]D&D[/i] kobolds have gone through different designs with each edition of [i]Dungeons & Dragons[/i], with the 3rd edition being their most well-known incarnation. The furry fandom itself has since then adopted different takes on the design of the kobold species.\r\n\r\nh4. Not to be confused with\r\n\r\n* [[dragonborn_(dnd)]] - Another draconic race from the same franchise as [i]Dungeons & Dragons[/i], the dragonborn are larger, have hair-like \"[[dreadlocks]]\", and tend to be more muscular.\r\n\r\nh4. Related tags\r\n\r\n* [[furred_kobold]]\r\n* [[japanese_kobold]]\r\n* [[kobold_(5e)]]\r\n* [[kobold_(pf2e)]]\r\n* [[kobold_(warcraft)]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* \"Wikipedia article about D&D's Kobolds \":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kobold_%28Dungeons_%26_Dragons%29\r\n* \"Forgotten Realms page about the Kobold race\":https://forgottenrealms.fandom.com/wiki/Kobold\r\n* \"Canonical Kobold Deviance\":https://1d4chan.org/wiki/Kobold#Monstergirls"
