              "wiki_body": "thumb #1426299 thumb #296545 thumb #294897 thumb #1735082 thumb #279821 thumb #1745105 thumb #1411461\r\n\r\n[b]Rodents[/b], members of [b]Rodentia[/b], are an order of [[mammal|mammals]] characterized by a single pair of continuously growing [[teeth|incisors]] in each of the upper and lower jaws. About 40% of all mammals are rodents, and they are the most diversified mammalian order, living in a variety of habitats, including [[human]]-made environments.\r\n\r\nTheir distinguishing incisors do not stop growing, so the animal must continue to wear them down so that they do not pierce the [[skull]]. A gap, or diastema, occurs between the incisors and the cheek teeth in most species, allowing rodents to suck in their cheeks or lips to shield their mouth and throat from wood shavings and other inedible material, discarding it later. Rodents generally have well-developed senses of smell, hearing, and vision, with nocturnal species often having [[big eyes|enlarged eyes]]. Many species have long, sensitive [[whiskers]] or vibrissae for touch or \"whisking\".\r\n\r\nWhile the largest species, the [[capybara]], can weigh as much as 146 lb, most rodents weigh less than 100 g. Rodents have wide-ranging body forms, but typically have squat bodies with short limbs. The [[claws|nails]] of burrowing species tend to be [[long claws|long]] and strong, while arboreal rodents have shorter, [[sharp claws|sharper]] nails. Rodent species use a wide variety of methods of locomotion including [[quadruped|quadrupedal]] [[walking]], [[running]], burrowing, [[climbing]], [[biped|bipedal]] [[hopping]], [[swimming]] and even [[gliding]]. The majority of rodents have tails, which can be of many shapes and sizes. Some tails are [[prehensile tail|prehensile]], and the [[fur]] on the tails can vary from bushy to [[rat tail|completely bald]]. Some have vestigial tails or no tails at all.\r\n\r\nh4. General anatomy\r\n* [[buckteeth]]\r\n* [[claws]]\r\n** [[long claws]]\r\n** [[sharp claws]]\r\n* [[fur]]\r\n* tail \r\n** [[fluffy tail]]\r\n** [[prehensile tail]]\r\n** [[rat tail]]\r\n* [[whiskers]]\r\n\r\nh4.Subfamilies, genera, and species\r\n[section,expanded=Non-fictional species]\r\n* [b]rodent[/b]  - order [b]Rodentia[/b] (Thomas Edward Bowdich, 1821)\r\n** suborder Anomaluromorpha (Jørgen Bugge, 1974)\r\n*** [[anomalure]] - family Anomaluridae (Paul Gervais; Alcide d'Orbigny, 1849)\r\n**** [i]Anomalurus[/i] (George R. Waterhouse, 1843)\r\n**** [i]Idiurus[/i] (Paul Matschie, 1894)\r\n*** family Pedetidae (John Edward Gray, 1825)\r\n**** [[springhare]] - [i]Pedetes[/i] (Johann K.W. Illiger, 1811)\r\n**** †[i]Megapedetes[/i] (D.G. MacInnes, 1957)\r\n*** family Zenkerellidae (Paul Matschie, 1898)\r\n**** [i]Zenkerella[/i] (Paul Matschie, 1898)\r\n** suborder Castorimorpha (Albert Elmer Wood, 1955)\r\n*** family Castoridae (Wilhelm Friedrich Hemprich, 1820)\r\n**** [[beaver]] - genus [i][[Castor (genus)|Castor]][/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n***** [[Eurasian beaver]] - [i]Castor fiber[/i] (Carl Linnaeus, 1758)\r\n***** [[North American beaver]] - [i]Castor canadensis[/i] (Heinrich Kuhl, 1820)\r\n**** †[i][[Castoroides]][/i] (John Wells Foster, 1838)\r\n*** superfamily Geomyoidea (Charles Lucien Bonaparte, 1845)\r\n**** [[gopher]] - family Geomyidae (Charles Lucien Bonaparte, 1845)\r\n**** [[heteromyid]] - family Heteromyidae (John Edward Gray, 1868)\r\n***** subfamily Dipodomyinae (F.L. Paul Gervais, 1853)\r\n****** [[kangaroo rat]] - [i]Dipodomys[/i] (John Edward Gray, 1841)\r\n****** [[kangaroo mouse]] - [i]Microdipodops[/i] (Clinton Hart Merriam, 1891)\r\n***** subfamily Heteromyinae (John Edward Gray, 1868)\r\n****** †[i]Diprionomys[/i] (Louise M. Kellogg, 1910)\r\n****** [[spiny pocket mouse]] - family [i]Heteromys[/i] (Anselme Gaëtan Desmarest, 1817)\r\n****** †[i]Metaliomys[/i] (William W. Korth; Donald D. De Blieux, 2010)\r\n***** [[pocket mouse]] - subfamily Perognathinae (Elliott Ladd Coues, 1875)\r\n****** [i]Chaetodipus[/