              "wiki_preview": "h4. Definition\r\n\r\nA [b]Yōkai[/b] (妖怪, ghost) is a class of supernatural monsters and spirits that ap...",
              "wiki_body": "h4. Definition\r\n\r\nA [b]Yōkai[/b] (妖怪, ghost) is a class of supernatural monsters and spirits that appear in [[Japanese mythology|Japanese folklore]]. The word 'yōkai' is composed of the kanji for \"bewitching; attractive; calamity\" and \"spectre; apparition; mystery; suspicious.\"Yōkai are also referred to as [b]ayakashi[/b] (あやかし), [b]mononoke[/b] (物の怪) or [b]mamono[/b] (魔物). \r\n\r\nRooted in Japanese animism, they can be considered personifications of animals, objects and even events and other phenomena. The characteristics of yōkai range diversely from malevolent and mischievous entities believed to cause misfortune and harm, to those who are considered to bring good fortune to those who encounter them.\r\n\r\nh4. Notable examples of yōkai:\r\n* [[tanuki|Tanuki (raccoon dog)]]\r\n* [[kitsune|Kitsune (multi tailed fox)]]\r\n* [[oni|Oni (ogre)]]\r\n* [[kappa|Kappa (water imp)]]\r\n* [[tengu|Tengu (crow-like humanoid/winged humanoid)]]\r\n\r\nh4. Notable media with yōkai or yōkai-esque characters:\r\n* [[gegege_no_kitaro|Gegege no Kitarō]]\r\n* [[Inuyasha]]\r\n* [[Pokémon]]\r\n* [[Digimon]]\r\n* [[Princess Mononoke]]\r\n* [[Spirited Away]]\r\n* [[Touhou]] \r\n* [[Yo-Kai Watch]]\r\n\r\nh4. Not to be confused with\r\n* [[yaoguai]]\r\n\r\nH4. See also\r\n* \"Wikipedia article for Yōkai\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yōkai"
            }
          ],
