            "wiki_preview": "Crickets are a species of [[insect]] in the order [i]Orthoptera[/i]. Many species are well known for...",
            "wiki_body": "Crickets are a species of [[insect]] in the order [i]Orthoptera[/i]. Many species are well known for their chirping songs, which they create by rubbing parts of their wings together. They are quite often seen in popular literature as anthropomorphic characters and are also kept as pets in many countries such as [[China]].\r\n\r\nThe term [i]cricket[/i] is popularly used for any cricket-like insect in the Orthopteran sub-order [[ensiferid|Ensifera]].\r\n\r\nh4. Species\r\n\r\n* [[ensiferid]]\r\n** [[gryllid]] (True crickets)\r\n*** [[field_cricket]]\r\n**** [[european_field_cricket]]\r\n**** [[fall_field_cricket]]\r\n**** [[jamaican_field_cricket]]\r\n*** [[house_cricket]]\r\n** Other types\r\n*** [[ant_cricket]]\r\n*** [[camel_cricket]] (Cave crickets)\r\n*** [[jerusalem_cricket]]\r\n*** [[katydid]] (Bush crickets)\r\n*** [[mole_cricket]]\r\n\r\nAll these insects have four tarsal segments and are probably more closely related to each other than they are to the true crickets, Gryllidae.\r\n\r\n\r\nh4. Fictional Characters\r\n\r\n* [[disney]]\r\n** [[pinocchio (movie)|pinocchio]]\r\n*** [[jiminy cricket]]\r\n\r\nh4. Not to be confused with\r\n\r\n* The [[sport]] of [[cricket_(sport)|Cricket]].\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[arthropod]]\r\n** [[insect]]\r\n*** [[orthopterid]]\r\n**** [[ensiferid]]\r\n***** [b]cricket[/b]\r\n****** [[gryllid]] (True crickets)\r\n******* [[field_cricket]]\r\n******* [[house_cricket]]\r\n****** [[katydid]]\r\n****** [[weta]]\r\n******* [[camel_cricket]] (Cave weta, or cave cricket)\r\n**** [[grasshopper]]\r\n***** [[locust]]"
