          "animatronic": [
            {
              "tag": "animatronic",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 29409,
              "wiki_preview": "thumb #794077 thumb #524011\r\n\r\nA [[robot]] that consists of an [[endoskeleton]] and a [[mascot]] [[c...",
              "wiki_body": "thumb #794077 thumb #524011\r\n\r\nA [[robot]] that consists of an [[endoskeleton]] and a [[mascot]] [[costume]]/shell. Unlike the infiltrator robots like the ones from the [i][[terminator_(franchise)|Terminator]][/i] franchise, which were designed to look as real as possible to fool people, these mechanical mimics are intended for entertainment purposes and are employed as automated mascots/entertainers. Their exterior are designed to resemble those of a particular fictional character, usually a mascot character.\r\n\r\nWhile the word itself has been in use for many years in the field of robotics, especially with [[Disney]]'s long-running and trademarked Audio-Animatronics, the video game [i][[Five Nights at Freddy's]][/i] is largely responsible for its sudden popularity.\r\n\r\nFor tagging purposes, the difference between an [[android]] and an animatronic is that the former are made to resemble humans (or [[anthro|anthros]]) with \"skin\" that is integrated into the design, whereas animatronics wear a removable shell or a costume that mask their true robotic appearance.\r\n\r\nh4.See also:\r\n\r\n* [[machine]]\r\n** [[robot]]\r\n*** [[android]]\r\n*** animatronic\r\n\r\nForum discussion:\r\n\r\n* forum #141131 - Cyborgs, Androids, and Robots. Oh my! (Oct. 2014)\r\n* forum #145008 - Calling all robot, android, mecha, etc. experts! (wiki project) (Dec. 2014)"
            }
          ],
          "android": [
            {
              "tag": "android",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 12539,
              "wiki_preview": "thumb #1257753 thumb #482402 thumb #219961 thumb #1585811\r\n\r\n[b]Androids[/b] are [[humanoid]] or [[a...",
              "wiki_body": "thumb #1257753 thumb #482402 thumb #219961 thumb #1585811\r\n\r\n[b]Androids[/b] are [[humanoid]] or [[anthro|anthropomorphic]] [[robot|robots]] that are biomimetic; Meaning that their machinery and overall visage are designed to emulate the functionalities, behavior, and appearance of a biological organism. A mechanical artificial lifeform if you will.\r\n\r\nh4. Traits\r\n* Superficially, the android tag can apply to any [[robot humanoid|humanoid robots]] or [[robot anthro|robotic furries]] that leans more towards looking more life-like and less mechanical looking.\r\n** One of the telltale signs of an android is if their skin has an unusual coloring, a synthetic texture, panel linings, industrial iconographies (such as serial numbers, barcodes, hazard signs, and company brand logos), [[tron lines]], or screws or rivets.\r\n* Internally they possess many parts that are similar to those organics such as [[synthetic muscles]], a flexible skin-like exterior covering, organ-like internal components, and a [[endoskeleton|skeletal internal frame]].\r\n** This means that they can do just about anything that their flesh and blood counterpart can such as eating, drinking, feeling, and they're fully equipped to engage in sexual activities.\r\n* Behaviorally most androids are fully autonomous with their learning and decision-making processes primarily conducted by their on-board [[computer]] brains which, overtime, develops its own personality and behavioral quirks (if they weren't already prepackaged/preprogrammed with one).\r\n\r\nh4. Terminology\r\nThe word [i]\"android\"[/i] is a combination of the prefix [i]\"andro-\"[/i] (\"[[human]]\" or \"[[male]]\") and the suffix [i]\"-oid\"[/i] (with the appearance/form/likeness of something). This would make the literal meaning of the word pertain to a being that is \"human-like\" or \"man-like\".\r\n\r\nDue to this some have proposed the term [i]\"gynoid\"[/i] which replaces the prefix with [i]\"gyno-\"[/i] ([[female]]) to refer to feminine androids.\r\n\r\nh4. Tagging notes\r\n* Due to the [[tag what you see]] policy, characters that are androids in lore but are visually indistinguishable from organic life should not be tags as such.\r\n* Biomimetic [[robotic ferals]] should not be tagged as androids.\r\n\r\nh4.Not to be confused with\r\n* [[cyborg]] - Organic lifeforms fitted with mechanical components.\r\n* [[Android_(os)]] - An operating system developed by [[Google]] for mobile devices.\r\n\r\nh4. See also:\r\n* [[machine]]\r\n** [[robot]]\r\n*** [b]android[/b]\r\n*** [[animatronic]]\r\n*** [[robot_humanoid]]\r\n*** [[robot_anthro]]\r\n*** [[robot_feral]]\r\n\r\nh4. Forum discussions:\r\n* forum #141131 - Cyborgs, Androids, and Robots. Oh my! (Oct. 2014)\r\n* forum #145008 - Calling all robot, android, mecha, etc. experts! (wiki project) (Dec. 2014)"
            }
