          "wiki_body": "thumb #4257957 thumb #3749131 thumb #4454696\r\n\r\nh4. Kirin\r\n\r\nKirin is a legendary hooved creature known in many East Asian cultures. It originated in China as qilin (麒麟; pronounced chee-leen). The appearance of a qilin is an auspicious sign, signaling the reign of a wise ruler. One qilin appeared to the mother of Confucius as a sign he was to become \"a king without a throne\". Qilin is one of four spiritual animals, along with the [[eastern_dragon|dragon]], the [[phoenix]] and the [[tortoise]]. It has extremely pacifistic disposition, refusing to bend even one blade of grass. Some depictions have qilins breathe fire and bring \"rui\" (serenity, prosperity) to the people.\r\n\r\nKirin is described as having the body of an elk, the tail of an ox and a single horn on the head. Artistic depictions are often inspired by the appearance of the dragon and include body covered in scales and two antlers instead of one horn.\r\n\r\nh4. Physical features\r\n* Body of a [[deer]] or [[horse]], usually covered in scales\r\n* Cloven [[hooves]]\r\n* Head similar to a [[eastern_dragon|dragon]]\r\n* One or two antlers\r\n* Long tail with a small tuft of hair on the end\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\"Qilin in Wikipedia\":http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qilin"
