          "tag": "virus_(organism)",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 131,
          "wiki_preview": "A virus is a tiny infectious agent that reproduces inside the cells of living hosts. They are often ...",
          "wiki_body": "A virus is a tiny infectious agent that reproduces inside the cells of living hosts. They are often comprised of a protein \"coat\" (capsid) covered in \"spikes\" that bind to receptors in the membrane of host cells; and a strand of genetic material, either RNA or [[dna|DNA]].\r\nThey can cause a wide variety of viral [[disease|diseases]], though some, such as a few retroviruses and bacteriophages, may be beneficial, being found to be crucial members of the skin and digestive flora of many animals. \r\n\r\n* See also:\r\n** [[coronavirus_(organism)|SARS-CoV-2 betacoronavirus]], the lethal virus responsible for the [[covid-19_pandemic|COVID-19 pandemic]], a devastating mass infection which changed the lives of millions worldwide;\r\n** [[bacteriophage_(organism)|bacteriophages]], a type of complex virus which targets and infects [[bacteria_(organism)|bacteria]], the most notable being the T4-Bacteriophages, arguably the second most recognisable virus;\r\n** [[ebola_(organism)|ebola]], a lethal, worm-shaped virus which caused a severe pandemic in 2014.\r\n\r\n** [[chill_(dr._mario)|Chill]], [[fever_(dr._mario)|Fever]], and [[weird_(dr._mario)|Weird]], fictional viruses which aptly cause their namesake ailments. More information can be found on the [[virus_(dr._mario)|Virus (Dr. Mario)]] page, as these are fairly different enough from their real-world viral counterparts.\r\n\r\n\r\n* Not to be confused with:\r\n** [[computer_virus|computer viruses]]\r\n\r\n\"Virus - From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virus"
        },
