          "count": 124,
          "wiki_preview": "[b]Darknuts[/b] are recurring enemies in [[The [i]Legend of Zelda[/i]]] series, first appearing in t...",
          "wiki_body": "[b]Darknuts[/b] are recurring enemies in [[The [i]Legend of Zelda[/i]]] series, first appearing in the original [i]The Legend of Zelda[/i]. They are similar to the Iron Knuckle, in that both resemble large, heavily-armored [[knight|knights]], but Darknuts are usually more aggressive in comparison to the stoic guardian-like nature of the Iron Knuckle. In addition, Darknuts have appeared in more games than Iron Knuckles, and Darknuts and Iron Knuckles have never co-existed in any of the games.\r\n\r\nDarknuts sometimes have differently-colored armor, which may denote ranks, but these are often functionally identical. [i]Twilight Princess[/i] also features a single unique-looking Darknut with black armor, found in the Temple of Time. This Darknut's appearance is used for the Darknuts in [i]Link's Crossbow Training[/i] and [i]Hyrule Warriors[/i].\r\n\r\nDarknuts are highly adaptable to losing their weapons, and much more mobile when their armor is removed, as seen in [i]The Wind Waker[/i] and [i]Twilight Princess[/i], respectively. \r\n\r\nIn [i]The Wind Waker[/i], unarmed Darknuts will grab any nearby dropped weapon, or, if there are none around, they will attack with martial arts melee attacks.\r\n\r\nIn [i]Twilight Princess[/i], once their outer armor is removed to reveal chain mail under-armor, they discard their large heavy sword by throwing it at Link, and then pull out a smaller, lighter longsword, continuing to fight with increased mobility and the ability to dodge certain attacks and weapons.\r\n\r\nWhile most incarnations of the Darknut have their face remain unseen, in [i]The Wind Waker[/i], Darknuts resemble [[anthro|anthropomorphic]] [[dobermann|dobermann pinschers]], albeit with more [[felid]] snouts and noses."
