          {
            "tag": "term_of_endearment",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 1642,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #949050   thumb #2882134   thumb #3735336    thumb #4775644   thumb #4800250\n\nA [color=general][b]term of endearment[/b][/color] is a term with a generally positive connotation used to address a character. \n\nTerms of endearment are most often used to address someone of emotional attachment, such as friends, family, and partners. It is not always done between people in a relationship, as terms of endearment may be given by a person; [[talking to self|talking to themself]], [[flirting]] with a stranger, in a parasocial relationship, or addressing an inanimate object of importance to them. Terms of endearment are entirely linguistic in nature. They are usual given either verbally or via [[text]], and body movements can only convey one through [[sign language]].\n\nThe term doesn't have to be taken in a flattering way or requited for this concept to apply. With frequent use, a term of endearment may become a [[catchphrase]] for the user, or likewise, a [[nickname]] for the recipient. The general wordage is not exclusive to expressing endearment, as terms may be used [[sarcasm|sarcastically]] or in other [[mocking|mockery]].\n\nTerms of endearment are a counterpart (and sometimes are) [[compliment|compliments]]. While both are a form of praise like, the former is distinguished by being used as a form of address. The opposite concept to this tag is an [[insult]], but there is [[insults of endearment|some overlap]].\n\nh4. See also\n* [[bullying]] — often incorporates insults.\n* [[compliment]] — the counterpart to terms of endearment.\n* [[dialogue]]\n** [[talking_to_another]]\n** [[talking_to_partner]]\n** [[talking_to_viewer]]\n* [[dude]]\n* [[flirting]]\n* [[honorific]]\n** [[master]]\n* [[insult]] — the opposite of a term of endearment\n** [[insult_of_endearment]] — an insult with the of a term of endearment.\n* [[praise]]\n** [[pet_praise]]\n*** [[good_boy]]\n*** [[good_girl]]\n* [[worship]] — often incorporates terms of endearment\n\n\n\n"
          },
