          "_Self (horn)": [
            {
              "tag": "horn",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 738647,
              "wiki_body": "A [b]horn[/b] is a pointed projection of the skin on the head of various animals, consisting of a covering of keratin (also called horn) surrounding a core of living bone. [[hooves|Hooves]], [[claws]], [[scutes]], [[beak|beaks]], [[hair]], [[fur]] and fingernails are also chiefly consisted of keratin, although it is also present in the skin.\n\nthumb #963156 thumb #961713 thumb #955997\n\nh4. Not to be confused with\n\n* Horn-like gestures: [[bunny_ears_(gesture)|cuckold's_horns]], [[devil horns (gesture)|devil_horns]]\n* Noise making horns:\n** [[air_horn]]\n** [[bike_horn]]\n** [[horn_(musical_instrument)]]\n** [[party_horn]]\n* [[tusks]]\n\nh4. Tags About Horns\n\n\nThese tags are used to describe [b]the shape and structure[/b] of the horns, [b]not the specie of the character[/b]. By example, a bovine character may have ram horns. A character can have any combination of horn types regardless of their original specie. The focus is purely anatomical and visual.\n\n[section=Horn Types]\n\n[table]\n\t[thead]\n\t\t[tr]\n\t\t\t[th] Type [/th]\n\t\t\t[th] Characteristic (Usual/Iconic) [/th]\n\t\t\t[th] Variations (Frequent deviations)[/th]\n\t\t\t[th] Example [/th]\n\t\t[/tr]\n\t[/thead]\n\t[tbody]\n\t\t[tr]\n\t\t\t[td] [[antelope_horn|antelope horn]]  [/td]\n\t\t\t[td] \n\t\t\t\t* Slender and elongated  \n\t\t\t\t* Usually straight or slightly curved  \n\t\t\t\t* Unbranched  \n\t\t\t\t* Often ridged with growth rings  \n\t\t\t[/td]\n\t\t\t[td] \n\t\t\t\t* Straight (e.g., oryx) [sup](see thumbnail 1)[/sup]\n\t\t\t\t* Loosely spiraled (e.g., eland) [sup](see thumbnail 2)[/sup]\n\t\t\t\t* Gently curved (e.g., gazelle) [sup](see thumbnail 3)[/sup]  \n\t\t\t[/td]\n\t\t\t[td] thumb #5402961 thumb #1775651 thumb #5645309 [/td]\n\t\t[/tr]\n\t\t[tr]\n\t\t\t[td] [[bovine_horn|bovine horn]] [/td]\n\t\t\t[td] \n\t\t\t\t* Growing outward from the side of the head \n\t\t\t\t* Thick (Broad-Based)\n\t\t\t\t* Curved\n\t\t\t\t* Unbranched\n\t\t\t[/td]\n\t\t\t[td] \n\t\t\t\t* Smoothed, or ridged\n\t\t\t\t* Curving outward, upward, or forward\n\t\t\t[/td]\n\t\t\t[td] thumb #5387559 thumb #4867613 thumb #4451775 [/td]\n\t\t[/tr]\n\t\t[tr]\n\t\t\t[td] [[deer_horn|deer horn]] \n\t\t\t\t[[antlers]][/td]\n\t\t\t[td]\n\t\t\t\t* Grows from the top or sides of the head  \n\t\t\t\t* Typically wide and flat or multi-tined  \n\t\t\t\t* Branched, with multiple points or forks  \n\t\t\t\t* Textured with grooves or rough surfaces  \n\t\t\t[/td]\n\t\t\t[td] \n\t\t\t\t* Multi-tined (e.g., whitetail deer)  [sup](see thumbnail 1)[/sup]\n\t\t\t\t* Palmate (e.g., moose)  [sup](see thumbnail 2)[/sup]\n\t\t\t\t* Upright or wide spread [sup](see thumbnail 3)[/sup]\n\t\t\t[/td]\n\t\t\t[td] thumb #3042394 thumb #5267187 thumb #5685019 [/td]\n\t\t[/tr]\n\t\t[tr]\n\t\t\t[td] [[oni_horn|oni horn]] [/td]\n\t\t\t[td]\n\t\t\t\t* Growing upward from the forehead or temples  \n\t\t\t\t* Short to medium length  \n\t\t\t\t* Tapered to a point  \n\t\t\t\t* Smooth  \n\t\t\t\t* Unbranched  \n\t\t\t[/td]\n\t\t\t[td]\n\t\t\t\t* Single, or paired  \n\t\t\t\t* Very, slightly curved, or straight  \n\t\t\t\t* Thick base or needle-like  \n\t\t\t[/td]\n\t\t\t[td] thumb #3809303 thumb #5680322 thumb #4508823 [/td]\n\t\t[/tr]\n\t\t[tr]\n\t\t\t[td] [[ram_horn|ram horn]] [/td]\n\t\t\t[td]\n\t\t\t\t* Growing back or outward from the back of the head \n\t\t\t\t* Thick (Broad-Based)\n\t\t\t\t* Near-Circular Coil\n\t\t\t\t* Ridged\n\t\t\t\t* Unbranched \n\t\t\t[/td]\n\t\t\t[td]\n\t\t\t\t* Semi-circle, or Full spiral \n\t\t\t\t* Growth rings\n\t\t\t[/td]\n\t\t\t[td] thumb #4855656 thumb #4855688 thumb #3259721 [/td]\n\t\t[/tr]\n\t[/tbody]\n[/table]\n\n\n[/section]\n\n[section=Horn Categories]\n\n* [[ossicone]]\n* [[penis_horn]] - A [[penis]] taking the place of a horn. Probably shouldn't be tagged as horn unless there is a visible horn structure.\n* [[unicorn_horn]] - A single horn centered on the head. It tends to be straight with a pointed tip. Some unicorn horns have a twisting spiral shape.\n* [[unusual_horn]]\n\nh6. By Location\n\n* [[head_horn]]\n* [[nose_horn]]\n\n[/section]\n\n[section=Horn Colors]\n\n* [[black_horn]]\n* [[blue_horn]]\n* [[brown_horn]]\n* [[green_horn]]\n* [[grey_horn]]\n* [[maroon_horn]]\n* [[orange_horn]]\n* [[pink_horn]]\n* [[purple_horn]]\n* [[red_horn]]\n* [[tan_horn]]\n* [[white_horn]]\n* [[yellow_horn]]\n\n* [[monotone_horn]] - only one distinct color.\n* [[two_tone_horn]] - only two distinct colors.\n* [[multi_tone_horn]] - more than two distinct colors.\n* [[multicolored_horn]] - at least two distinct colors.\n** [[rainbow_horn]]\n\n* [[dark_horn]] - Dark colored\n* [[light_horn]] - Light colored\n\n[/section]\n\n[section=Horn Traits]\n\nh4. Texturing and Appearance\n\n* [[dipstick_horn]]\n* [[glistening_horn]]\n* [[glowing_horn]]\n* [[ridged_horn]]\n* [[smooth_horn]]\n* [[spotted_horn]]\n* [[striped_horn]]\n* [[translucent_horn]]\n* [[wooden_horn]]\n\n* bodily_fluids_on_horn\n** [[blood_on_horn]]\n** [[cum_on_horn]]\n** [[pussy_juice_on_horn]]\n** [[saliva_on_horn]]\n** [[sweat_on_horn]]\n** [[urine_on_horn]]\n* [[feces_on_horn]]\n* [[vomit_on_horn]]\n\nh4. Form and Shape\n\nh6. Horn shaft\n\n* [[broken_horn]]\n* [[curved_horn]]\n** [[curled_horn]]\n** [[wavy_horn]]\n* [[forked_horn]]\n* [[long_horn]]\n* [[short_horn]]\n* [[spiral_horn]] - Not to be confused with [[ridged_horn]] or [[curled_horn]].\n* [[straight_horn]]\n\nh6. Horn tip\n\n* [[blunted_horn]]\n* [[rounded_horn]]\n* [[sharp_horn]]\n\n[/section]\n\n[section=Horn Quantity]\n\n* [[1_horn]]\n* [[2_horns]]\n* [[multi_horn]] - Three or more horns on a single character.\n** [[3_horns]], [[4_horns]], [[5_horns]], [[6_horns]], [[7_horns]], [[8_horns]], [[9_horns]], [[10_horns]]\n\n[/section]\n\n[section=Horn Clothing, Accessories, or Attachments]\n\n* [[horn_accessory]]\n** [[horn_bell]]\n** [[horn_bow]]\n** [[horn_jewelry]]\n*** [[horn_ring]]\n** [[horn_ornament]]\n** [[horn_piercing]]\n** [[horn_ribbon]]\n** [[horn_wraps]]\n* [[horn_bondage]]\n* [[horn_clothing]]\n\n[/section]\n\n[section=Horn Actions]\n\n* [[holding_horn]]\n* [[horn_grab]]\n* [[horn_fetish]]\n** [[horngasm]]\n** [[horn_play]]\n*** [[horn_lick]]\n*** [[horn_sex]]\n**** [[hornjob]] \n*** [[horn_masturbation]]\n*** [[horn_penetration]]\n**** [[horn_in_ass]]\n**** [[horn_in_cloaca]]\n**** [[horn_in_mouth]] - May not be considered penetration.\n***** [[horn_bite]]\n***** [[horn_suck]]\n**** [[horn_in_pussy]]\n**** [[horn_in_urethra]]\n***** [[horn_in_penis]]\n*** [[horn_vore]]\n** [[after_horn_play]]\n*** [[after_horn_sex]]\n**** [[after_hornjob]]\n*** [[after_horn_masturbation]]\n*** [[after_horn_penetration]]\n* horn_transformation\n** [[horn_expansion]]\n** [[horn_growth]]\n\n[/section]\n\n[section=Other]\n\n* [[horn_magic]]\n* [[this_isn't_my_horn]]\n\n[/section]\n\nh4. Trivia info\nSome trivia unrelated to e621 tagging.\n\n[section,expanded=Trivia]\nBesides fantastical creatures such as [[dragon|dragons]], [[unicorn|unicorns]] and [[demon|demons]], horns (and antlers and ossicones) are overwhelmingly associated with ruminants, of which 6 families currently exist.\n\nOssicones are only present in [[giraffid|giraffids]], specifically the male [[okapi]] and all [[giraffe|giraffes]]. They're already born with tiny, vascularized ossicones, albeit they're soft and dettached from the cranium so as to not affect birth. Within few weeks, they attach, harden and grow, but result in relatively short, straight protuberances covered by skin and fur. Some extinct giraffids produced long and/or branched structures. In some populations, males tend to develop a third horn, between its eyes, after reaching maturity. Rarely, a male giraffe may be born with a second pair of ossicones, immediately behind the first.\n\nAntlers are the telltale feature of [[cervid|cervids]], a.k.a. true deers. They sprout straight from the cranium in male, juvenile individuals, remaining covered in skin and sparse fur (\"velvet\"), which slough off once the antlers are fully grown, exposing the typically large, branched bone. This also incurs further hardening and the atrophy of the blood vessels within. However, the hard antlers are shed every year, and regrown anew for the rest of the animal's life. Females rarely present antlers, which is, usually, a sign of hormonal imbalance. The sole exception to that are [[reindeer|reindeers]], in which all females have them, altho typically smaller than those of males.\n\nTrue horns, as mentioned above, refers to bony protrusions and their covering made of keratin, of which the word horn also is a synonym. These are sported by all wild male specimens [[bovid|bovids]] ([[cattle]], [[goat|goats]], [[sheep]], [[antelope|antelopes]] etc.), and its presence in female varies wildly by species, as does the horn's shape and size. Unlike antlers, they never have branches, and they keep growing for as long as the animal lives. Barring very few exceptions (e.g. [[four-horned_antelope|four-horned antelope]]), it's limited to one pair, but the trait of two or more is occasionally seen in the wild.\n\nCertain domesticated breeds have higher likelihood of sporting more than a pair (\"polycerate\"), or even none. The latter are said to be \"polled\", which can only be reliably verified by seeing male specimens -- it should be noted that not a single breed (or species, for that matter) has only females presenting horns. As for multiple pairs, sheep present the vast majority of cases, because some breeds have much higher chances of being born with more than one pair (e.g. [[navajo-churro|Navajo-Churro]], Hebridean), to the point where it's the norm (e.g. [[jacob_sheep|Jacob]], Manx Loaghtan), with males having a higher probability. These extra paits have a much higher probability of being asymmetrical.\n\nOf the remaining 3 families, [[tragulid|tragulids]] (mouse-deer a.k.a. chevrotain) and [[moschid|moschids]] (musk deer) are the odd men out, having foregone any kind of head protrusions completely, developing [[fangs]] instead for the purpose of sexual selection. It's a striking sight, given that they're herbivores. The former present fangs in both sexes, and the latter, only on the males.\n\nThe odder men out are the [[water_deer|water deer]], the [[tufted_deer|tufted deer]] and the [[muntjac]], which present fangs instead of antlers, despite being part of the cervid family. Similar to their antlers' tho, only the males have fangs.\n\nThe oddest man out is the sole species in the antilocaprid family, the [[pronghorn]]. Both sexes grow true horns like bovids do, yet the keratin layer alone is shed every year and regrown, while the bone core remains, in a weird twist to cervids' antlers.\n\nThe man so out it doesn't even count is the [[horned_screamer|horned screamer]], which is actually a bird with a single silly-looking, long and spiny \"horn\" made of pure keratin growing from its forehead, on both sexes. It keeps growing for life, but it's so thin it constantly breaks at the tip, remaining mostly about the same size.\n[/section]\n\nh4. See also\n\n* [[antennae_(anatomy)]]"
