          "balancing_on_tail": [
            {
              "tag": "balancing_on_tail",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 393,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #1072706 thumb #2309958 thumb #3110133 thumb #1516442 \r\n\r\nA character supporting most or all of their weight with their [[tail]]. Usually, the character's tail is their only body part contacting the ground, raising the rest of their body into the air.\r\n\r\nH5. [[Apode]]\r\n\r\nthumb #1478462\r\n\r\nIn general, the tail of apodes begins after their genital region, which is usually located near the end of their body if anatomically correct, so [i]balancing_on_tail[/i] is most appropriate when only that part of their body contacts the ground.\r\n\r\nNon-feral apodes often have their genitals at their midsection, making the above distinction irrelevant for them, and their standard upright posture may look like other creatures' [i]balancing_on_tail[/i]. However, an apode in its standard upright posture likely is not [i][[balancing]][/i] any more than a normal [[biped]] does when [[standing]] upright. Moreover, the point of [i]balancing_on_tail[/i] is to highlight an unconventional posture. Thus, this tag is best applied to non-feral apodes in abnormal [[on_tail]] postures that can reasonably be described as balancing.\r\n\r\nh4.See Also:\r\n* [[balancing]]\r\n* [[on_tail]]\r\n** [[sitting_on_tail]]\r\n* [[reclining]]\r\n* [[sitting]]\r\n* \"tripod stance\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripod_stance\r\n\r\n* [[tag group:pose]]"
            }
