          "tag": "syringe",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 3161,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #916237\r\n\r\nA [b]syringe[/b] is a simple reciprocating pump consisting of a plunger (or a piston) that fits tightly within a cylindrical tube called a barrel. The plunger can be linearly pulled and pushed along the inside of the tube, allowing the syringe to take in and expel liquid or gas through a discharge orifice at the front (open) end of the tube. The open end of the syringe may be fitted with a hypodermic needle, a nozzle or a tubing to help direct the flow into and out of the barrel. Syringes are frequently used in clinical medicine to administer injections, infuse intravenous therapy into the bloodstream, apply compounds such as glue or lubricant, and draw/measure liquids.\r\n\r\nh4. Easily Confused Tags\r\n** [[injection]], which refers to the act of actually [i]using[/i] a syringe on someone, rather than a syringe simply being present somewhere.\r\n\r\nh4. External:\r\n\r\n* \"Wikipedia - Syringe\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syringe"
