          "count": 153,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #29637 thumb #3400949 thumb #104837\r\n\r\n[b]Vertical staggering[/b] is an image layout term that refers to a situation when columns (vertically-aligned) image frames/panels share a common edge (an unbroken line between adjacent frames), but the positions at which the image frames meet on that common edge do not form an intersection/grid pattern. In other words, there is a common edge, but the shapes of the panels do not meet in the same areas along that common edge.\r\n\r\nh4. Tagging notes\r\n\r\nStaggering only occurs along the border between adjacent image frames. The intersection where the border terminates does not apply towards this tag's definition.\r\n\r\nthumb #3289679 This example has horizontal staggering in it, but not vertical staggering, because the border is terminated by the top row.\r\n\r\nExtra note: A little extra border space can be ignored for the purposes of layout tagging.\r\n\r\nh4. Related themes\r\n\r\n* [[staggered_grid]] - The image frames appear to form a grid, but the intersections of the image frames do not line up.\r\n* [[staggered_grid_layout]] - The entire image layout consists of a staggered grid. This should not be confused with a typical [[grid layout]].\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[comic]]\r\n* [[sequence]]\r\n\r\n[b]Layout tags[/b]\r\n\r\n* [[blockage_(layout)]]\r\n** [[horizontal_blockage]]\r\n** [[vertical_blockage]]\r\n* [[grid_layout]]\r\n* [[staggered_grid_layout]]\r\n* [[staggering_(layout)]]\r\n** [[horizontal_staggering]]\r\n** [b]vertical_staggering[/b]"
