            "tag": "plantar_flexion",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 529,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #2221725 thumb #2198170\r\n[b]Plantar flexion[/b] is the downward foot movement responsible for any tiptoe-like posture. (Think, \"ballerina's toes\".)\r\n\r\nWhen performed delicately, this pose can express demure, cute, refined, or sexy overtones. It can also imply comfort and a desire to linger because the feet are not primed for travel. More actively, plantar flexion features in [[dancing]], [[fighting]], and other kinds of [[action pose]]s.\r\n\r\nCompared to [[tiptoes]], the performer does [b]not[/b] need to be standing.\r\n\r\nh5. Guidelines\r\n\r\nThis tag applies to any emphasis on flexion beyond normal walking angles. Note that different animal foot types, such as [[digitigrade]], have steeper normal walking angles than humans and thus more of an angle to overcome for this pose. \r\n\r\nTag also [[plantar overflexion]] when the angle exceeds 180°.\r\n\r\nh5. Editor's Notes\r\nThe `tiptoes` tag may implicate `plantar flexion`.\r\n\r\nh5. See also:\r\n\r\n* [[plantar overflexion]]\r\n* [[tiptoes]]\r\n* [[toe curl]]"
