                "count": 34475,
                "wiki_body": "thumb #3971756 thumb #2945265 thumb #1975377\r\n\r\n[b]Cellphones[/b] are portable, wireless [[phone|telephones]]. \r\n\r\nBy far the most commonly depicted type of cellphone is the ubiquitous modern [[smartphone]], however, \"[i]feature phones[/i][sup]*[/sup]\" and earlier smartphones may be seen as well, especially in period themed artwork.\r\n\r\nh5. Types of Cellphone\r\n[i]While there are other minor types of cellphone, such as slider phones, these four tags cover the vast majority of cellular phones on e621[/i]\r\n\r\n* [[brick phone]]: very large, boxy mobile phones produced during the 1980s, the first cellphones\r\n* [[bar phone]]: pocket sized cellphones shaped like soap bars or chocolate bars, can describe both \"dumb\" phones made from the 1990s to present and early smartphones (see example 1 above)\r\n* [[flip phone]]: \"clam shell\" style phones, very popular during the 2000s\r\n* [[smartphone]]: Usually refers to modern day slim, rectangular touch screen cellular devices\r\n\r\n[sup]*\"Feature phone\" is a modern term for non-touch screen and/or non-smartphone mobile phones sold from the 2010s on, these often take the form of a bar phone or flip phone.[/sup]\r\n\r\nh5. Cellphone Related Tags\r\n\r\n* [[holding cellphone]]\r\n* [[looking at cellphone]]\r\n* [[phone call]]\r\n* [[phone charm]]\r\n* [[selfie]]\r\n* [[texting]]\r\n* [[text message]]\r\n\r\n\r\nh4. See Also\r\n* [[phone]]\r\n** [[landline phone]]\r\n*** [[cordless phone]]\r\n*** [[rotary phone]]\r\n** [[payphone]]"
              }
            ],
            "smartphone": [
              {
                "tag": "smartphone",
                "kor": "",
                "count": 19125,
                "wiki_body": "thumb #3028646 thumb #2292485 thumb #2578234 thumb #2816004\r\n\r\n[b]Smartphone[/b] describes a [[cellphone]] that is essentially a pocket-sized [[computer]]. Most modern smartphones are thin, rectangular devices, with a full face touch screen for controlling the device and one or more cameras on the device's rear face.\r\n\r\nSmartphones combine the functionalities of a [[personal digital assistant]], [[camera]], [[handheld console]], and [[portable music player]], in addition to the abilities to browse the internet, watch and record videos, and use applications (often referred to as \"apps\"). \r\n\r\nh5. Tagging Notes\r\n\r\n* Older smartphones may feature physical buttons at the bottom of the phone's touch screen for navigation and calling, some may even have full QWERTY keyboards\r\n** Depending on design, older smartphones should also be tagged as [[bar phone]] — see page for details\r\n* As long as a science fiction device is clearly a futuristic, advanced, or alien analog of a smartphone, the device should be tagged as a smartphone\r\n* Smartphones and [[tablet computer|tablet computers]] should not be confused, while visually similar, tablet computers are inherently much larger devices\r\n\r\n\r\nh5. Related Tags\r\n* [[holding phone]]\r\n** [[holding smartphone]]\r\n* [[looking at phone]]\r\n* [[phone call]]\r\n* [[selfie]]\r\n** [[selfie stick]]\r\n\r\nh4. See Also\r\n* [[phone]]\r\n** [[cellphone]]\r\n*** [[bar phone]]\r\n*** [[brick phone]]\r\n*** [[flip phone]]\r\n*** [b]smartphone[/b]\r\n** [[landline phone]]\r\n** [[rotary phone]]\r\n** [[payphone]]\r\n\r\nh4. Related Tags\r\n* [[holding phone]]\r\n* [[looking at phone]]\r\n* [[phone call]]"
              }
            ],
            "flip_phone": [
              {
                "tag": "flip_phone",
                "kor": "",
                "count": 499,
                "wiki_body": "thumb #2637226 thumb #4338707 thumb #257061\r\n\r\nA [b]flip phone[/b] is a [[cellphone]] with a \"clam shell\" design, these phones are made of two sections connected by a hinge, typically, one section will have a keyboard/keypad and receiver to speak into, and the other will have a display and speaker to hear from. \r\n\r\nFlip phones entered the market between 1993 and 1996, changing in size, shape and adding capabilities over the years; by the 2000s the flip phone was ubiquitous. \r\n\r\nWhile largely overtaken in the market by touchscreen smartphones, flip phones continue to be produced and sold into present day, including some examples of [[smartphone|smartphones]] that use a clam shell design — these devices feature dual touch screens, or a single, flexible touch screen (if such a phone ever appears in a post, use both `flip phone` and `smartphone` for tags).\r\n\r\nh4. See Also\r\n* [[phone]]\r\n** [[cellphone]]\r\n*** [[bar phone]]\r\n*** [[brick phone]]\r\n*** [b]flip phone[/b]\r\n*** [[smartphone]]\r\n\r\nh4. Related Tags\r\n* [[holding phone]]\r\n* [[looking at phone]]\r\n* [[phone call]]\r\n* [[selfie]]"
              }
            ]
          },
          "landline_phone": [
            {
              "tag": "landline_phone",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 929,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #3630191 thumb #1619802 thumb #1774\r\n\r\nA [b]landline phone[/b], sometimes called a \"home phone\" is a [[phone|telephone]] hardwired to a telephone line for transmission. Landline phones are comprised of two parts, the handset — which has a speaker and receiver (to hear through and speak into, respectively), and the base — which is where the handset rests when not in use.\r\n\r\nThere are two forms of landline phone...\r\n\r\n* wired or corded phones have a physical cord that tethers the handset to the base of the phone, the base of the phone usually has buttons for dialing numbers\r\n** [[rotary phone|rotary phones]], which have a rotary disk to dial numbers, instead of a button keypad, are almost always corded phones\r\n* [[cordless phone|cordless phones]] have wireless handsets, which can be used within an area around the base, the handset often has buttons for dialing, rather than the base\r\n\r\nh4. Related Tags\r\n* [[holding phone]]\r\n* [[phone call]]\r\n\r\nh4. See Also\r\n* [[phone]]\r\n** [[cellphone]]\r\n*** [[bar phone]]\r\n*** [[brick phone]]\r\n*** [[flip phone]]\r\n*** [[smartphone]]\r\n** [b]landline phone[/b]\r\n*** [[cordless phone]]\r\n*** [[rotary phone]]\r\n** [[payphone]]"
            }
          ],
          "rotary_phone": [
            {
              "tag": "rotary_phone",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 172,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #92255 thumb #2932605\r\n\r\nA [[phone|telephone]] with a rotary disk with finger holes for each 10 numbers, which is used to dial the selected number. Rotary phones mostly fell out of use in the 1980s, and were replaced by push-button phones.\r\n\r\nh4. See Also\r\n\r\n* [[phone]]\r\n** [[cellphone]]\r\n*** [[bar phone]]\r\n*** [[brick phone]]\r\n*** [[flip phone]]\r\n*** [[smartphone]]\r\n** [[landline phone]]\r\n** [[rotary phone]]"
            }
          ]
        },
