            "gun": {
              "_Self (gun)": [
                {
                  "tag": "gun",
                  "kor": "",
                  "count": 40820,
                  "wiki_body": "A gun is a muzzle or breech-loaded projectile-firing [[weapon]]. There are various definitions depending on the nation and branch of service. A \"gun\" may be distinguished from other firearms in being a crew-served [[weapon]] such as a howitzer or mortar, as opposed to a small arm like a rifle or pistol, but there are exceptions, such as the U.S. Air Force's GUU5/P. At one time, land-based artillery tubes were called cannon and sea-based naval cannon were called guns. The term \"gun\" evolved into a generic term for any tube-launched projectile-firing weapon used by sailors, including boarding parties and marines.\r\n\r\nh4.See also:\r\n\r\n* [[weapon]]\r\n* [[ranged weapon]]\r\n* [[tag group:weapons]]"
                }
              ],
              "rifle": {
                "_Self (rifle)": [
                  {
                    "tag": "rifle",
                    "kor": "",
                    "count": 7763,
                    "wiki_body": "A [b]rifle[/b] is a [[gun|firearm]] designed to be fired from the shoulder, with a barrel that has a helical groove or pattern of grooves (\"rifling\") cut into the barrel walls.\r\n\r\nh4. Types\r\n\r\n* [[assault_rifle]] - A rifle known for its automatic weapon fire.\r\n* [[repeating_rifle]] - also known as a repeater is a rifle that could discharge multiple times in between ammunition reloads. This term is mainly for older style weaponry and not assault rifles.\r\n* [[sniper_rifle]] - A rifle known for its accuracy at long range distances.\r\n\r\nh4. Brands\r\n\r\n* [[winchester]] - A type of lever-action repeating rifle produced by the [i]Winchester Repeating Arms Company[/i].\r\n\r\nh4. Related tags\r\n\r\n* [[battle|combat]]\r\n* [[hunting]]\r\n* [[military]]\r\n** [[soldier]]\r\n** [[war]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[ranged weapon]]\r\n** [[gun]]\r\n*** [[handgun]]\r\n*** [b]rifle[/b]\r\n*** [[shotgun]]"
                  }
                ],
                "sniper_rifle": [
                  {
                    "tag": "sniper_rifle",
                    "kor": "",
                    "count": 1687,
                    "wiki_body": "thumb #363603 thumb #362245 thumb #258469\r\n\r\nA sniper rifle is a precision-[[rifle]] used to ensure more accurate placement of bullets at longer ranges than other [[gun|firearms]]. A sniper rifle almost always has a [[scope]] and is easily distinguished from other [[rifle|rifles]] by its very long barrel.\r\n\r\nSniper rifles are commonly used in assassination operations by either the [[military]] or [[assassin|assassins]], especially for targets that are too dangerous or risky to approach, or to remain hidden after the shot has been made.\r\n\r\nh4. See also:\r\n\r\n* [[scope]]\r\n* [[gun]]\r\n* [[rifle]] \r\n* [[ranged weapon]]"
                  }
                ]
              },
              "pistol": [
                {
                  "tag": "pistol",
                  "kor": "",
                  "count": 7136,
                  "wiki_body": "A type of [[handgun]], usually referring to [[handgun|handguns]] with a single chamber, as opposed to the [[revolver]], so do not tag those as pistols.\r\n\r\nh4. Types of pistol\r\n* [[beretta 92]]\r\n* [[desert eagle]]\r\n* [[glock]]\r\n* [[luger]]\r\n* [[m1911 pistol]]\r\n* [[walther_p99]]\r\n\r\nh4. Not to be confused with\r\n* [[revolver]]\r\n\r\nh4.See also:\r\n* [[gun]]\r\n** [[handgun]]\r\n* [[ranged_weapon]]"
                }
              ],
              "machine_gun": {
                "_Self (machine_gun)": [
                  {
                    "tag": "machine_gun",
                    "kor": "",
                    "count": 1369,
                    "wiki_body": "A machine gun refers to any type of firearm designed for [i]sustained[/i] automatic fire. This includes anything from the massive, powered [[gatling_gun]] to the hand-held [[submachine_gun]].\r\n\r\nA light machine gun may be easily mistaken for an [[assault_rifle]], particularly in cases such as \"this\":http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:AK-74_RPK-74_DA-ST-89-06612.jpg. Machine guns can be distinguished by a longer barrel, larger magazine, and the presence of a bipod.\r\n\r\nAn [[assault_rifle]] is considered an entirely different class of weapon.\r\n\r\nh4.See also:\r\n\r\n* [[submachine_gun]]\r\n* [[gatling_gun]]\r\n* [[assault_rifle]]\r\n* [[gun]]"
                  }
                ],
                "gatling_gun": {
                  "_Self (gatling_gun)": [
                    {
                      "tag": "gatling_gun",
                      "kor": "",
                      "count": 607,
                      "wiki_body": "thumb #2176861 thumb #1795515 thumb #533855\r\n\r\nUse this tag when you see a gun with multiple rotating barrels.\r\n\r\nIf a Gatling gun is small enough to be (conceivably) carried by a roughly human-sized individual, it should [i]also[/i] be tagged with [[minigun]].\r\n\r\nThe \"Gatling Gun\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gatling_gun is a hand crank driven multi-barreled gun invented in 1861 by Richard Jordan Gatling. Modern \"rotary guns\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rotary_cannon are based on the same principle of using a rotating formation of multiple barrels to simultaneously load bullets, fire, and eject spent casings in different barrels to increase fire rates. Given the fundamental similarity in operation, \"Gatling gun\" is now widely understood to refer to any rotating multi-barreled gun, rather than just the hand-cranked 1861 weapon.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag_group:weapons]]\r\n* [[machine_gun]]\r\n** [[minigun]]\r\n* [[ranged weapon]]"
                    }
                  ],
                  "minigun": [
                    {
                      "tag": "minigun",
                      "kor": "",
                      "count": 435,
                      "wiki_body": "thumb #2566514 thumb #2610110 thumb #3068411\r\n\r\nUse this tag when you see a gun with multiple rotating barrels [i]that is small enough to (conceivably) be wielded by a human-sized individual.[/i]\r\n\r\nIf the weapon in question is large enough to be considered a cannon (for instance, if it is clearly too large for a human-sized individual to carry around) then [b]only[/b] [[gatling gun]] should be used.\r\n\r\nTechnically, the term \"minigun\" refers specifically to the General Electric \"M134 Minigun\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M134_Minigun which is chambered for 7.62mm NATO and is named in comparison to its much larger predecessors, like the 20mm \"M61 Vulcan\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M61_Vulcan often seen on military aircraft. Everyone knows rotary guns like these are awesome, but since \"rotary gun\" is a bit of a mouthful, \"minigun\" has become genericized as a way to refer to any \"man-portable\" bullet hose with multiple rotating barrels. In light of this, \"minigun\" on e621 should be used to tag any image where such a weapon is depicted. \r\n\r\nDon't actually try to fire one of these unmounted.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag_group:weapons]]\r\n* [[machine gun]]\r\n** [[gatling gun]]\r\n* [[ranged weapon]]"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              },
              "submachine_gun": {
                "_Self (submachine_gun)": [
                  {
                    "tag": "submachine_gun",
                    "kor": "",
                    "count": 1237,
                    "wiki_body": "A submachine gun (SMG for short) is an automatic carbine, designed to fire pistol cartridges. It combines the automatic fire of a [[machine gun]] with the cartridge of a [[pistol]].\r\n\r\nThey are distinguished from other, similar [[firearm|firearms]] by being smaller than [[rifle|rifles]] and, unlike most [[pistol|pistols]], [i]not[/i] designed to be held in one hand.\r\n\r\n[table][tbody][tr][td]\r\n[[kriss vector]]\r\nthumb #933084\r\n[/td][td]\r\n[[mac-10]]\r\nthumb #146878\r\n[/td][td]\r\n[[mp5]]\r\nthumb #2545384\r\n[/td][td]\r\n[[mp 40]]\r\nthumb #31188\r\n[/td][td]\r\n[[p90]]\r\nthumb #642410\r\n[/td][/tr][/tbody][tbody][tr][td]\r\n[[ppsh-41]]\r\nthumb #49234\r\n[/td][td]\r\n[[thompson gun]]\r\nthumb #738438\r\n[/td][td]\r\n[[uzi]]\r\nthumb #868903\r\n[/td][/tr][/tbody][/table]\r\n\r\nh4. See also: \r\n* [[ranged_weapon]]\r\n* [[gun]]\r\n** [[assault rifle]]\r\n** [[machine gun]]\r\n"
                  }
                ],
                "thompson_gun": [
                  {
                    "tag": "thompson_gun",
                    "kor": "",
                    "count": 222,
                    "wiki_body": "thumb #738438 thumb #447077 thumb #793187\r\n\r\nThe Thompson submachine gun, sometimes informally called a \"Tommy Gun\", is a [[submachine gun]] made infamous by the gangsters of the 1920s and 1930s (but used by [[soldier|soldiers]], civilians, [[police|law enforcement]] and criminals alike), it is easily identified by its drum magazine and dual grips. It is among the most well known firearms in pop culture, and popular among collectors.\r\n\r\nThe Thompson was also known informally as the \"Chicago Typewriter\", \"The Chopper\", and \"Trench Sweeper\".\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nh4.See also:\r\n\r\n* [[submachine gun]]\r\n* [[ranged weapon]]\r\n"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            "bow_(weapon)": [
              {
                "tag": "bow_(weapon)",
                "kor": "",
                "count": 5363,
                "wiki_body": "A projectile weapon that predates recorded history and is common to most cultures. \r\n\r\nA bow is a flexible arc that shoots aerodynamic projectiles (arrows or quarrels, sometimes stones) by means of elastic energy. Essentially, the bow is a form of spring powered by a string or cord. As this string is drawn, energy is stored in the flexible limbs of the bow; the energy is transferred to the arrow when the string is released, projecting it much farther than a weapon could be thrown. \r\n\r\nThe bow is considered as a specialist weapon, since training with a bow requires a lifetime of training and physical demand for the usage of the bow to be efficient. \r\n\r\nh4. See Also: \r\n* [[tag_group:weapons]]\r\n* [[arrow_(weapon)|arrow]] - the projectile specially designed to be shot using a bow. \r\n* [[ranged_weapon]]\r\n* [[weapon]]\r\n* [[archer]]"
              }
            ],
