          {
            "tag": "rainbow_pride_flag",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 1043,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #679651\r\n\r\nThe [b]rainbow flag[/b] was popularized as a symbol of lesbian, gay, bisexual, and transgender (LGBT) people by San Francisco artist Gilbert Baker in 1978. It has since become internationally famous.\r\n\r\nThe [[flag]] is typically flown horizontally, with the red stripe on top (as it would be in a natural [[rainbow]]). The most common variant consists of six stripes in total: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, and violet (from top to bottom).\r\n\r\nh4. Tag usage\r\n\r\nThis tag should only be used for examples that have a distinctly flag-shaped appearance, and anything else should use [[rainbow pride colors]] instead. There is an \"active BUR\":https://e621.net/forum_topics/30728 requesting an alias of this tag to `rainbow_pride_colors`. If that alias goes through the [[rainbow flag]] tag will persist as the tag for any flag containing a rainbow theme.\r\n\r\nh4. Design variations\r\n\r\n* [[progress_pride_colors]] — Design variant with a layered chevron on the side.\r\n* [[philadelphia_rainbow_pride_colors]] — Design variant with 8 stripes, with black and brown at the top.\r\n* [[1978_rainbow_pride_colors]] — Design variant with 8 stripes, with pink at the top and teal included.\r\n* [[seven-stripe_rainbow_pride_colors]] — Design variant with 7 stripes, with cyan/teal included.\r\n* [[six-stripe_rainbow_pride_colors]] — Design variant with 6 stripes.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[flag]]\r\n** [[french_flag]]\r\n** [[irish_flag]]\r\n** [[russian_flag]]\r\n** [[stars_and_stripes]]\r\n** [[swedish_flag]]\r\n* [[lgbt_pride]]\r\n* [[pride_colors]]\r\n** [[aromantic_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[asexual_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[bisexual_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[demisexual_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[genderfluid_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[genderqueer_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[intersex_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[lesbian_pride_colors]]\r\n*** [[orange_lesbian_pride_colors]] — A flag with seven stripes or five stripes, arranged orange-white-pink, popularized in 2018.\r\n*** [[pink_lesbian_pride_colors]] — A pink flag with seven stripes most commonly used between 2013 and 2018, visually the same as [[lipstick lesbian flag|lipstick_lesbian_pride_colors]] without the kiss symbol.\r\n*** [[lipstick_lesbian_pride_colors]] — A pink flag with seven stripes and a kiss symbol meant for femme lesbians, visually the same as [[pink lesbian flag|pink_lesbian_pride_colors]] with a kiss symbol.\r\n*** [[labrys_lesbian_pride_colors]] — A purple flag featuring a white labrys symbol inside an inverted black triangle.\r\n*** [[alternate_lesbian_pride_colors]] — A catch-all for other lesbian flag designs.\r\n** [[vincian_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[nonbinary_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[pansexual_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[rainbow_pride_colors]] — LGBT pride colors.\r\n*** [[progress_pride_colors]] — Rainbow flag variant with a layered chevron on the side.\r\n*** [[philadelphia_rainbow_pride_colors]] — Rainbow flag variant with 8 stripes, with black and brown at the top.\r\n*** [[1978_rainbow_pride_colors]] — Rainbow flag variant with 8 stripes, with pink at the top and teal included.\r\n*** [[seven-stripe_rainbow_pride_colors]] — Rainbow flag variant with 7 stripes, with teal included.\r\n*** [[six-stripe_rainbow_pride_colors]]\r\n** [[straight_ally_colors]]\r\n** [[transgender_pride_colors]]\r\n* [[rainbow_symbol]]\r\n** [[rainbow_flag]]"
          },
