        {
          "tag": "using_pokeball",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 494,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #1896187 thumb #4305697 thumb #2595392 thumb #3484452\n\nWhenever the functions of a [[pokeball]] are being actively used. These include: capturing, sending out returning, releasing, dynamaxing, and terastalization. Note that this does not include imagery of characters [i]just[/i] being inside of pokéballs, and such images should not use this tag.\n\n* Capture: Throwing a ball at, or just pressing the button against, a character. This causes the pokéball to shoot a beam of light at the character, transforming them into light before recalling the beam back inside of the pokéball and closing. Characters can struggle to break free before the capture is permanent, which causes the pokéball to wiggle side to side a few times before an iconic sound, the button turning green, a few stars and/or a little firework shooting up to signal success. \n\n* Sending Out: Often involves the Trainer (or anyone holding the ball) throwing it into the air or at the ground to cause the ball to open up, spewing forth a beam of light that quickly shapes into the silhouette of the character that was inside of it before they properly materialize. Pokémon have shown that they can send themselves out from inside of the ball voluntarily, even without the consent of the Trainer, though this is usually just for comedic effect and a rare trait.\n\n* Returning: The Trainer holding the ball and pointing the button in the direction of the Pokémon belonging to it. The ball does not open, but a (typically) red beam shoots out at the character. If it hits, the character is transformed into red light and then the beam retracts into the button. This only works if they have been captured previously by that specific pokeball, effectively making it their home.\n\n* Releasing: When the ball is activated, either thrown or just used without a physical action, a (typically) blue light shoots out that quickly shapes into the silhouette of the character that was inside, converting the light back into the character but with the notable function of undoing the capture, meaning they can no longer be recalled by it and are once again considered wild/free.\n\n* Dynamax and Terastalization: These mechanics were the gimmicks of generation 8 and 9 respectively. What separates them from other specialties from earlier generations, i.e. Mega Evolution and Z-Moves, is that the pokeball is directly involved in activating these powers. The pokeball will take on a special appearance during this time that resembles the mechanic's appearance on the Pokemon before being thrown to activate the power. Therefore any time you see a pokeball in those relevant appearances, they are considered to be in use.\n\nh4. See Also\n\n* [[pokeball]]\n* [[pokemon]]\n* [[transformation_by_pokeball]]"
