            "tag": "breaking_the_fourth_wall",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 1819,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #3110788 thumb #3387968\r\nA term originating from the three-walled stage setup typical of theaters, the fourth wall being the imaginary wall separating the audience from the stage.\r\n\r\nA breaking of the fourth wall occurs when a fictional character goes out of character and actively addresses the audience [i]as an audience[/i], or in some other way acknowledges the viewer as being outside of the character's world, or otherwise demonstrates awareness of themselves not actually being real or in a real setting. Here, a character does not necessarily have to address the viewer but a character may break the fourth wall toward others in the picture itself by referencing their fictional nature.\r\n\r\nnot to be confused with [[outside_border]] or [[outside_panel]]."
          },
