          {
            "tag": "city",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 25316,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #2665132 thumb #35274 thumb #3838497 thumb #3155450\r\n\r\nA [b]city[/b] is a large settlement that acts as a hub of commerce, culture and population. Cities can vary in physical size, density, and population, but can range from tens of thousands to tens of millions of residents.\r\n\r\nA city may be depicted in many ways, such as a [[skyline]] of towering buildings in the distance, or sprawling urban area from a high angle or aerial view, or be shown as in a street level scene with visibly densely packed buildings all around.\r\n\r\nh5. Tagging Notes\r\n\r\nh6. city vs. [[town]]\r\nThis distinction is visual and subjective, as in reality a large town can be a city, and a small city can be referred to as a town. [b][i]For tagging purposes[/i][/b], \"town scenes\" should show little to no high rise buildings — a town scene may feature a [[street]] lined with low rise buildings (less than ten storeys), a mix of housing and shops, and/or a generally a more open and less dense urban area with low rise buildings.\r\n\r\nh6. city vs. [[cityscape]]\r\nA cityscape is the urban equivalent of a landscape, where a broad view of an urban area is depicted. If a city is depicted in low detail, as a [[city_background|background]], or within a scene that is mostly focused on a character, rather than the scenery, use city alone, rather than cityscape.\r\n\r\nh6. city vs. [[skyline]]\r\n\r\nA skyline is a distant view of tall buildings, sometimes only depicted as simple shapes or silhouettes. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nh4. See Also\r\n\r\n* [[building]]\r\n* [[city_background]]\r\n* [[cityscape]]\r\n* [[town]] \r\n* [[urban]]\r\n* [[village]]\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n"
          },
