          "tag": "decorative_scarab",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 199,
          "wiki_body": "[b]Decorative scarabs[/b] made an incredibly common appearance in general [[Egyptian]] culture, such as in [[egyptian clothing|their clothing]], [[accessory|accessories]], [[sculpture|sculpted/carved artworks]], and [[hieroglyphics|hieroglyphs]].\r\n\r\nScarab beetles, most often the [[Sacred scarab]] or other related [[Dung Beetle|dung beetles]], were of high importance in the culture for several speculated reasons. One reason, for example, is said to be due to the [[deity|god]], [[Khepri]], being a [[animal head|man with the head of a scarab]]. Khepri had much religious significance, so Scarabs were seen in things such as amulets and seals. The god, [[Ra]], may have also contributed to the popularity of scarab imagery, as he would roll the [[sun]] across the [[sky]] in a similar manner to dung beetles.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[egypt]]\r\n* [[egyptian]]\r\n* [[egyptian_clothing]]\r\n* [[scarabaeid]]\r\n** [[dung_beetle]]\r\n*** [[sacred_scarab]]"
