          "wiki_body": "A café is an establishment that primarily serves coffee (of various types, e.g. espresso, latte, cappuccino).\r\n\r\nWhile café may refer to a coffeehouse, the term \"café\" generally refers to a diner, British café (colloquially called a \"caff\"), \"greasy spoon\" (a small and inexpensive restaurant), transport café, teahouse or tea room, or other casual eating and drinking place.\r\n\r\nA coffeehouse may share some of the same characteristics of a bar or restaurant, but it is different from a cafeteria.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag_group:locations]]"
        },
        {
          "tag": "prison_cell",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 1427,
          "wiki_body": "A small room, or partitioned area where [[prisoner|prisoners]] are held. Prison cells are part of a larger facility known as a [[prison]].\r\n\r\nh4. Design traits\r\n\r\nA traditional prison cell is secured with iron [[bars]], but other designs exist. Forcefields are common in futuristic settings.\r\n\r\nh5. Features\r\n\r\n* [[bars]]\r\n** [[barred_window]]\r\n* [[concrete]]\r\n** [[concrete_floor]]\r\n** [[concrete_wall]]\r\n\r\nh4. Related tags\r\n\r\n* [[contained]] / [[containment]] - The general concept for someone held in an enclosed space.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[cage]]"
        },
        {
          "tag": "town",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 1377,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #3975104 thumb #2982744 thumb #2201378 thumb #3142082 thumb #3421607 \r\nA [b]town[/b] is a settlement that is larger than a [[village]] but smaller than a [[city]]. A town will typically have tens of thousands of residents.\r\n\r\nh5. Tagging Notes\r\n\r\n* A scene set in a modern town may show a mix of \"low\" and \"medium\" density development, with small, multistory buildings for apartments, shops, and other businesses in built up downtown/\"Main Street\" areas, neighbourhoods of houses, and detached shops/businesses, with buildings becoming more sparse and scattered towards the edges of town\r\n** but lacks the dense high rise development, skyscrapers, and other \"big city\" features that would make the `city` tag more appropriate\r\n* This tag can be especially helpful for and commonly applied to depictions of large settlements in fantasy or medieval settings, to distinguish them from more \"typical\" depictions of a \"modern city\"\r\n\r\nFor tagging purposes, a village is a small town, and a city is a large town. If it is hard to tell what kind of town it is, tagging town is always acceptable. Also [[townscape]] is an actual theme that should exist. Please only use the cityscape tag when depicting a city, and townscape for other examples.\r\n\r\nh4. See Also\r\n* [[city]]\r\n** [[cityscape]]\r\n** [[skyline]]\r\n* [[urban]]\r\n* [[village]]\r\n\r\n"
        },
