          {
            "tag": "element_drop_shadow",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 595,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #2947076 thumb #3604414 thumb #3021851 thumb #2849580\r\n\r\nA narrative element, or other symbol that features a [[drop shadow]]. The main idea of this tag is that the drop shadow is a part of something that doesn't actually exist within the scene. In the case of [[picture in picture]], the scene most relevant to the element should be used to reason the application of this tag.\r\n\r\nFor drop shadows associated with scene props and characters, see [[drop_shadow]].\r\n\r\nThis tag should rarely be tagged alone, and the more specific drop shadow element tag should be used instead. However until implications are in place, both tags need to be added individually.\r\n\r\nh4. General guidelines\r\n\r\n- The placement of the shadow may not be directly under the element. It is still okay to tag if the drop shadow is placed, or drawn in a strange or inconsistent way.\r\n- It is uncommon, but drop shadows are sometimes colored.\r\n\r\nh4. Categories\r\n\r\n* [[heart_drop_shadow]]\r\n* Text containers\r\n** [[speech_bubble_drop_shadow]]\r\n** [[text_box_drop_shadow]]\r\n** [[thought_bubble_drop_shadow]]\r\n* [[text_drop_shadow]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[tag_group:narrative_elements]]\r\n\r\n* [[drip_effect]]\r\n* [[shadow]]"
          },
          {
            "tag": "drop_shadow",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 588,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #2676294 thumb #3215051 thumb #2425567 \r\n\r\n\r\nA [b]drop shadow[/b] is one of two basic shadow effects applied in drawn imagery, the other being cast shadows. A drop shadow is placed in an area nearby, and slightly offset to whatever the shadow applies to. This gives the appearance that the subject whether character, object, or narrative element is above (in front of) the region containing the drop shadow. Contrast with cast shadows, which appear roughly where light is supposed to be blocked, often appearing underneath a character in some kind of basic shape.\r\n\r\nThe drop shadow effect can be manipulated to create shadowy instances of figures on the screen in ways that mimic an [[afterimage]]. It is uncommon, but drop shadows are sometimes colored.\r\n\r\nFor narrative elements featuring drop shadows, see [[element_drop_shadow]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[afterimage]]\r\n* [[shadow]]\r\n** [[cast_shadow]]\r\n** [b]drop_shadow[/b]"
