        {
          "tag": "mistletoe",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 3824,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #14706\r\n\r\n[b]Mistletoe[/b] is a white-berried ([i]Viscum album[/i] and [i]Phoradendron leucarpum[/i]), or red-berried ([i]Viscum cruciatum[/i] or [i]Viscum rotundifolium[/i]), or pink-berried ([i]Phoradendron californicum[/i]) evergreen parasitic plant with smooth, teardrop-shaped or oval leaves, often associated with [[Christmas]]. The tradition of kissing under the mistletoe originated with the Roman festival of Saturnalia. Mistletoe is frequently incorporated into a [[kissing_bough]]\r\n\r\nMistletoe was considered to be sacred by druids, and is still used in contemporary witchcraft.\r\n\r\nOften confused with [[holly (plant)|holly]], which is entirely different plant. Attempting to kiss someone under a holly is considered bad form, even if the holly is used as a kissing bough.\r\n\r\nh4. Related Tags:\r\n\r\n* [[Christmas]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also:\r\n\r\n* [[holly_(plant)]], sharp-leaved plant with (commonly) red or (sometimes) white berries\r\n* [[lingonberry]], an evergreen with tear-shaped leaves and edible red berries, generally associated with tasty Scandinavian dishes and not kissed under or used by druids.\r\n* [[accidental_holly]], a situation in which holly is mistakenly used in place of mistletoe\r\n"
        },
        {
          "tag": "breath_powers",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 3570,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #1133567 thumb #1073212 thumb #736424\r\n\r\nUsed when a character, typically a [[dragon]], is exhaling some sort of magical energy.  This may take the form of [[fire breathing|fire]], [[ice breathing|ice]], [[lightning breathing|lightning]] or any other form of magical energy.\r\n\r\nOther common forms of breath weapon may include a poisonous breath, exhaling some form of disease or plague, colored energy bolts, raw arcane magic, raw life energy, or any other imaginable manifested energy.\r\n\r\nh4. See Also:\r\n\r\n* breath_powers\r\n** [[fire_breathing]]\r\n** [[ice_breathing]]\r\n** [[lightning_breathing]]\r\n* [[magic]]"
        },
        {
          "tag": "wind",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 2871,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #1108692 thumb #1400374 thumb #1981986 thumb #1376007\r\n\r\nWikipedia currently defines wind as \"the flow of gases on a large scale.\" Rarely, wind is directly depicted in art with wispy, cloud-like trails or a [[toony]] [[motion_lines|motion line]] effect. Usually, however, the gases comprising wind are not directly depicted, meaning the presence of wind must instead be inferred from an invisible force appearing to act upon the objects that are depicted. To this end, wind is often simulated in art with an [i]otherwise inexplicable[/i] raising or pushing of loose, light objects in a specific direction. Usually these objects would have been in a resting state without wind's intervention, but occasionally wind is depicted altering the expected pathing or movement of an object already in motion (e.g., [[falling]] objects, objects attached to a [[running]] character).\r\n\r\nSometimes [[magic]] creates wind or wind-like phenomena, and sometimes visible gases like [[smoke]] plainly convey wind's flowing of gases. Wind is a complex force with many possible manifestations. What's more, art conveys a simplified version of wind, and artists may depict wind selectively, with oversights, or inaccurately. For tagging purposes, forces which are not strictly wind but do look like it and currently lack proper organization, like lift or decompression, can also use this tag.\r\n\r\nh6.Wind-specific tags:\r\n\r\n* [[flowing_hair]]\r\n* [[wind_lift]]\r\n\r\n[section=Non-exhaustive list of objects commonly acted upon by wind]\r\n\r\n* [[accessory]]\r\n** [[necklace]]\r\n** [[ribbons]]\r\n* [[clothing]]\r\n** [[bottomwear]]\r\n*** [[skirt]]\r\n** [[hat]]\r\n** worn at the shoulders\r\n*** [[cape]]\r\n*** [[cloak]]\r\n*** [[dress]]\r\n*** [[scarf]]\r\n* [[hair]]\r\n* [[particles]]\r\n** [[dust]]\r\n** [[sand]]\r\n** [[snow]]\r\n* [[plant]]\r\n** [[flower_petals]]\r\n** [[grass]]\r\n** [[leaf]]\r\n** [[tree]]\r\n** [[tumbleweed]]\r\n* [[water]]\r\n** [[raining]]\r\n** [[ripple]]\r\n** [[wave]]\r\n* weather\r\n** [[storm]]\r\n** [[whirlwind]]\r\n*** [[dust_devil]]\r\n*** [[tornado]]\r\n*** [[waterspout]]\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nh5. See also\r\n\r\n* [[blowing]]\r\n* [[falling]]\r\n* [[floating]]\r\n* [[midair]]\r\n* [[levitation]]"
