        {
          "tag": "english_honorific",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 980,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #4799316 thumb #3035398 thumb #480345\r\n\r\nAn [b]English [[honorific]][/b] is a title in the English language that expresses esteem, courtesy or [[respect]] for a position or [[rank]] when used to address or refer to a person.\r\n\r\nThe title [i]must[/i] be in English for this tag to be used, although the text outside of the title do not have to be in English.\r\n\r\nIn English, the most used are [b]Master[/b], [b]Mr[/b] ([b]Mister[/b]), [b]Mrs[/b] ([b]Missus[/b]), [b]Miss[/b], and [b]Ms[/b]. Note that in American English, there is a period/full stop (.) after the abbreviated titles (e.g. \"Mr. Smith\" instead of \"Mr Smith\").\r\n\r\nh4. Examples of utilization\r\n* When a [[student]] is speaking to a [[teacher]]\r\n* When a [[employee]] addresses their [[boss]]\r\n* When a [[patient]] greets their [[doctor]]\r\n\r\nh4.List of English honorifics\r\n[section=Honorific titles]\r\nh4.Common titles\r\n* \"[[Master]]\"\r\n* \"Mr\"\r\n* \"Miss\"\r\n* \"Mrs\"\r\n* \"Ms\"\r\n* \"Mx\" (a gender-neutral/non-binary honorific)\r\n\r\nh4.Formal titles\r\n* \"Sir\"\r\n* \"[[Gentleman]]\"\r\n* \"Sire\"\r\n* \"Mistress\"\r\n* \"Madam\" or \"Ma'am\"\r\n* \"Dame\"\r\n* \"Lord\"\r\n* \"Lady\"\r\n* \"Esq\" (\"Esquire\")\r\n* \"Excellency\"\r\n* \"Your/Her/His Honour\" (\"Honor\" in American English)\r\n\r\nh4. Professional titles\r\n* \"Dr\" (\"[[Doctor]]\")\r\n* \"Professor\"\r\n* \"[[Teacher]]\"\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nh4. Related tags:\r\n* [[japanese honorific]]\r\n* [[greeting]]\r\n** [[greeting viewer]] – When the greeting is made for the viewer\r\n* [[name in dialogue]]/[[name drop]] – When a name is placed before or after a title\r\n\r\nh4. See also:\r\n* [[nobility]]\r\n* [[profanity]]\r\n** [[insult]] – Used also for derogatory titles\r\n* [[term of endearment]]"
        },
