        {
          "tag": "style_emulation",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 2309,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #3047061 thumb #5032487 thumb #690396 thumb #4821562\r\n\r\n[b]\"Style emulation\"[/b] describes art that imitates the distinct visual style of another artist, medium, art genre, or specific piece of media (e.g. a cartoon, anime, video game, etc.) \r\n\r\nStyle emulation is often done for comedic effect or to depict an unlikely or bizarre crossover of art style and content (e.g. a character that is normally drawn in a simplistic, toony style depicted in a gritty, realistic art style). Note that the image doesn't need to reference or parody the property it is an emulating.\r\n\r\nh6. Style emulation may include...\r\n* art made to look like [[poster|posters]], [[book cover|book covers]], or [[cover page|comic book covers]]\r\n* [[fake screenshot|fake screenshots]], usually with [[caption|captioned dialogue text]]\r\n* characters rendered in [[low poly]] to resemble early 3d video games (e.g. graphics of the Nintendo 64 or Sony Playstation)\r\n* characters rendered in [[pixel_(artwork)|pixel art]] to emulate the look of 8bit or 16bit graphics\r\n\r\nStyle emulations of video games may involve game elements such as the [[user interface]] being visible, [[text box|text boxes]] for dialogue, [[health bar|health bars]] etc. \r\n\r\n[quote]h5. Not to be confused with...\r\n\r\nh6. [[on model]]\r\n\r\nThis describes a copyrighted character being drawn or rendered in a way that is identical to the official source material\r\n\r\nh6. [[style crossover]]\r\n\r\nThis describes two or more characters within the same scene who are drawn in different distinctive art styles\r\n\r\nh6. [[inspired by formal art]]\r\n\r\nThis describes a piece made to emulate a specific piece of [[formal art]], while sometimes emulating of the style of the artist, these are not necessarily style emulations\r\n[/quote]\r\n\r\nh4. Related Tags\r\n* [[multiple styles]] - when a character is portrayed in several art styles\r\n* [[kris where are we]] - a meme involving style emulation"
