        {
          "tag": "tag_question",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 707,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #3510648 thumb #3624494 thumb #2555287 \r\n\r\n[b]Tag question[/b] is a special form of question that appears after a statement that needs to be confirmed, clarified, or agreed / disagreed with. \r\n\r\nh4. Common forms\r\n\r\nLook for these phrases at the end of statements.\r\n\r\n* \"Right?\", \"Do you?\", \"Don't you?\", \"Will you?\", \"Would you?\", \"Hmm?\", \"Huh?\"\r\n\r\n* \"This isn't going to hurt, right?\"\r\n* \"You really like this, don't you?\"\r\n* \"Amazing, right?\"\r\n\r\nh4. Tagging notes\r\n\r\n- The punctuation between the statement and the question is optional.\r\n- Tag questions are usually a form of [[yes-no question]].\r\n\r\nOther languages have their own form of tag question. Further instructions will be provided on how to use this tag for other languages in the future.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[asking]]\r\n* [[question]]"
        },
        {
          "tag": "time_lapse",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 701,
          "wiki_body": "The [b]time lapse[/b] tag has two general use cases:\r\n\r\nh4. Single frame time elapses\r\n\r\nPosts that depict a span of time within a single scene.\r\n\r\nthumb #151789\r\n\r\nh4. Sequence transitions\r\n\r\nPosts that have an obvious gap in time between frames. The gap between images should not be evenly distributed like a [[sequence]]. It should be a sudden or abrupt change such as what is seen in [[instant loss 2koma]]. The time that has elapsed should be referenced in the scene, but this is not a hard requirement.\r\n\r\nthumb #598902 thumb #121484 thumb #158779\r\n\r\nh4. Not to be confused with\r\n\r\n* [[art_progression]] - The tag for sped up depictions of the drawing process.\r\n* [[sequence]] - A progression of an event represented as a series of images. Most examples of sequences are not useful to have this tag.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[slideshow]]"
        },
