        {
          "tag": "anime_style",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 455,
          "wiki_body": "Anime is a style of animation that originated in [[Japanese|Japan]] and has become popular worldwide. It is characterized by its vibrant artwork, fantastical themes, and colorful characters. Anime can encompass a wide range of genres, from drama and action to romance and horror, making it a versatile form of storytelling.\n\nAnime is unique in its depth of characterization and plot development, often exploring complex themes and presenting multi-dimensional characters. It's not just for children; many anime series and movies are designed for teenagers and adults.\n\nThe art style in anime can vary greatly, but some common characteristics include large, expressive eyes, exaggerated physical features, and artistically vibrant color palettes. Backgrounds often have a lot of detail, contributing to the overall aesthetic appeal of the animation.\n\nWhile anime is a product of Japanese culture, its popularity has spread globally. Many artists and animators outside of Japan have been influenced by anime, incorporating its distinctive style into their own work. As a result, anime has had a significant impact on global pop culture.\n\nh4. Related tag:\n* [[kemono]]\n\nh4. See also\n* [[toony]]"
        },
