            {
              "tag": "macro",
              "kor": "거대",
              "count": 41168,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #3420193  thumb #3472499 thumb #1698877 thumb #1933715  thumb #4293753 \n\n[b]Macro[/b] describes characters who are [b]giant[/b] compared to the world around them. To be tagged as `macro`, the character should be clearly \"abnormally very large\" — scenery and/or props should suggest their abnormal size, [b][i]simply interacting with smaller characters does not make a character \"macro\"[/i][/b]. \n\n\n[quote]\n\n\nh5. Tagging Notes\n\n* Macro is derived from \"macrophilia\" (the paraphilia for giants and giantesses), however, this tag doesn't indicate any sexual or fetishistic intent, merely the size of the character, and so a macro image can be rated [color=green]Safe[/color]\n* Monsters or mythological beasts that are naturally very large should usually not fall under this tag — e.g. feral dragons that are larger than human or anthro characters shouldn't be tagged as `macro` unless they are [i]extremely large[/i] even for the typical depiction of feral dragons\n\nh6. [color=yellow]IMPORTANT NOTE:  MACRO [i]AND [/i]MICRO SHOULD ALMOST NEVER BE TAGGED TOGETHER ON THE SAME POST:[/color]\n* A macro sized character interacting with a normal sized character [b]does not[/b] make the smaller character [[micro]]\n* A normal sized character interacting with a [[micro]] sized character shouldn't be tagged as macro\n* When a macro character is shown with or interacting with [u]normal sized characters[/u], the post [i]should[/i] be tagged as [b][[extreme size difference]][/b]\n** if there are no visible smaller characters, then the post should only be tagged as `macro`\n\n[/quote]\n\nh2. Tags Used In Macro\n\n[i]Tags related to, or frequently used in macro content. Note that not all of the following tags are inherently tied to macro art, nor necessarily inherent to micro or extreme size difference.[/i]\n\n[section,expanded=All Tags]\n[section,expanded= ]\n\nh4. Scales/Subcategories\n[i]These tags apply to different scales of macro character, with \"macro\" acting as an umbrella tag[/i]\n\nh5. [i][[Giga|Giga Macro]] [/i]\n[i]A character that is larger than the \"average\" macro, these characters are often depicted as larger than entire cities, they may use geographic features in unusual ways, or dwarf entire landscapes[/i]\n** [[landscape dwarfing]] - a [[giga]] macro character large enough that entire cities and natural features (e.g. mountains) are dwarfed by their size, the character is often drawn in a way that emphasizes the size, sometimes so distant and so large that they are tinted blue to show atmosphere between the giga sized character and the viewer's perspective\n\nh5. [i][[Terra Macro]] [/i]\n[i]A character that is equal to, or larger than the size of planets, or other celestial bodies[/i]\n** [[planet dwarfing]] - a [[terra macro]] character that is overwhelmingly larger in size than a planet, which may be the size of a gumball or even as small as a bead or grain of rice in comparison to the terra sized character, their body often taking up most of the frame\n** [[intergalactic macro]] - a [[terra macro]] character that is equal to, or larger than galaxies — or the entire universe/multiverse — and is shown interacting with them\n\n[/section]\n\n[section,expanded= ]\nh5. Miscellaneous\n* [[body size growth]] - a character growing in size, a comic, sequence, or animation may feature a normal sized character growing to a macro scale\n* [[low angle view]]\n** [[worm's-eye view]]\n* [[macro on macro]] - sexual interactions between two giant sized characters\n\n[/section]\n\n[section,expanded= ]\nh5. Interaction with Smaller Characters\n[i]If normal sized characters are visible in a scene with the macro sized character, the post should be tagged as [b][[extreme size difference]][/b]:[/i]\n\nh6. [i][[Multilevel Macro]][/i]\n[i]This describes two characters who are both macro sized but have considerable amounts of [[size difference]] between them, potentially including giga or terra sized characters within the same scene or comic[/i]\n\n\nh6. General Interactions\n* [[holding character]]\n* [[holding viewer]]\n* [[sitting on another]] - this should apply if [[butt crush]] or [[butt smother]] do not seem appropriate\n* [[trapped in clothing]] - a character trapped within an article of clothing worn by another character, more common in micro content, but macro examples exist\n* [[mass crush]] - a group or crowd of characters being crushed all at once, usually involves a massive character stepping on a crowd\n* [[size play]] - interaction between a larger character and smaller character where the size difference is a focal point of the scene\n\nh6. Sexual Interactions\n* [[living insertion]] - a character inserting a smaller character, entirely or partially, into an orifice for sexual pleasure\n* [[bodyjob]] - a character using their whole body to stimulate the penis of a larger character\n[/section]\n\n[section,expanded= ]\nh5. Interaction With Objects, Buildings and Scenery\n[i]How a macro sized character is physically interacting with things around them:[/i]\n\nh6. General Interactions\n\n* [b]Buildings[/b]\n** [[breasts on building]]\n** [[building between breasts]]\n** [[holding building]] \n** [[leaning on building]]\n** [[sitting on building]]\n\n* [b]Planets[/b]\n** [[holding planet]]\n** [[laying on planet]]\n** [[planet between breasts]]\n** [[sitting on planet]]\n** [[standing on planet]]\n\n* [b]Vehicles[/b]\n** [[holding vehicle]]\n** [[sitting on vehicle]]\n\nh6. Sexual Interactions\n\n* [[building sex]] - this tag describes any sexual interaction with a building including, but not limited to, penetration and insertion\n* [[cum on building]]\n* [[cum on planet]]\n* [[pussy juice on building]]\n* [[pussy juice on planet]]\n* [b]Insertion into an orifice (e.g. anus or vagina)[/b]\n** [[building insertion]]\n** [[planet insertion]]\n** [[vehicle insertion]]\n* [b]Character penetrating the subject[/b]\n** [[building penetrated]]\n** [[planet penetrate