            "wiki_body": "[b]Gagging[/b] is the triggering of one's gag reflex, resulting in an involuntary muscular contraction in the throat. The gag reflex is triggered by a foreign object touching the roof of the mouth. The contraction is characterized by the sudden urge to expel anything inside the character's mouth. A character may induce [[vomit|vomiting]] by triggering the gag reflex.\r\n\r\nA gagging character will probably make all sorts of noises before, and after obstructions are removed. It is not uncommon for a gagging character to have their airflow obstructed, resulting in a [[gasp]] for air once the obstruction is removed.\r\n\r\nh4. Sound effect tagging\r\n\r\nNoises associated with gagging may sometimes include [[swallowing]] sounds as well as a couple sounds exclusive to gagging including, but not limited to the [[onomatopoeia]] \"gag\". Be careful when applying the [[sound effects]] tag, as [[muffled]] speech, including [[vocalization|vocalized]] sounds are common with [[gagged]] characters, do not get tagged as `sound_effects`.\r\n\r\nh6. How to tell the difference?\r\n\r\nMuffled, or vocalized speech usually will start with an \"M\", or \"N\". Sound effects will rarely start with these letters. \"H\" is probably a vocalization, and if it isn't, it is probably a variation of \"Hurk\". Several swallowing sound effects start with \"G\" such as \"Gluck\", \"Glurk\", and \"Gulp\". \"Gah\", and \"Gack\" are vocalizations, not sound effects. \r\n\r\nh4. Not to be confused with\r\n\r\n* [[choking]] - The act of strangling a character with one's hands. Gagging should be used if a character is choking on something specific.\r\n\r\nh4. Related tags\r\n\r\n* [[deep_throat]]\r\n* [[swallowing]]"
