          "wiki_body": "thumb #393006 thumb #4137393 thumb #4186883 thumb #2688299 thumb #2912743 thumb #949929 thumb #2736867\r\n\r\nPosts showing a character who first dislikes something, but later they enjoy that thing after experiencing it enough.\r\n\r\nHere [i]\"until\"[/i] implies lengthy or repeated exposure to a negative experience, but often this is not directly shown and instead only implied through a [[time lapse]] or [[text]]. Sometimes the character's breaking point is shown with an abrupt change in tone mid-sentence, from rejection to enjoyment.\r\n\r\nThis tag is very strongly associated with [[rape]] but can apply to any other [[forced]] situation where a character does not control what happens to them. Rarely, this tag can even apply to consenting situations where, for example, a character begrudgingly undertakes an ordeal and over time starts to enjoy it, which people often experience in real life toward things like exercise, projects, and work.\r\n\r\n[b][u]This tag does not invalidate the [[rape]] tag.[/u][/b] In spite of the character later enjoying their experience, please keep all tags that applied when the character disliked their experience.\r\n\r\n[section,expanded]\r\nh5. Commentary\r\n\r\nThis tag was originally intended for the rape trope where the victim starts enjoying the actions of their abuser. This trope [i]suggests[/i] that a rape victim still feels pleasure from sex, and that a persistent rapist can outlast their victim's resistance until the victim only feels pleasure, which they can no longer deny. This trope is fantasy and does not happen in real life. Do not attempt it.\r\n\r\nAlso, this tag's origin has created a stigma that [i]expects[/i] an abuser or victimizing force, but those things are not strictly required to use this tag. A character's preexisting preferences and prejudices can cause them to dislike something, which they can overcome through experience, learning to enjoy the thing they once disliked.\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\n[section,expanded]\r\nh5.Do not confuse with\r\n\r\nPosts with this tag [b]must[/b] show both (1) a character disliking something and (2) that same character later liking it. \r\n\r\n* [[comic]], [[story_in_description]], pools, image sets, parent/child posts, post descriptions, etc - these tell stories and can show [i]until_they_like_it[/i] situations in detail. However, only tag [i]until_they_like_it[/i] on the posts showing both the character's old dislike and new enjoyment toward a certain experience. [u]Do not use this tag on posts only showing one of these things.[/u] Sometimes, a group of posts or story does have an [i]until_they_like_it[/i] subplot but does not show both of this tag's requirements on any single post. These posts [u]cannot[/u] be tagged with [i]until_they_like_it[/i]. This is a limitation of boorus that cannot tag and search collections of posts (e.g., tagging entire pools by theme).\r\n\r\n* [[forced_orgasm]], [[unwanted_erection]], etc - a character's body acting against their will. This is not proof of pleasure or consent, so [i]until_they_like_it[/i] requires something more definitive.\r\n\r\n* [[mind_break]] - a character mentally overwhelmed by extreme stimuli, forcing them to become unresponsive or their personality to drastically change. This is commonly the transition between non-consent and enjoyment in [i]until_they_like_it[/i]. Many [i]mind_break[/i] posts do not show the broken character's previous displeasure, which [i]until_they_like_it[/i] requires.\r\n\r\n* [[mind_control]] - using special means to influence a character's ability to think normally. This often compels a resisting character to become submissive, obedient, and take pleasure in things they normally wouldn't. For [i]mind_control[/i] posts to qualify for [i]until_they_like_it[/i], they must still show the mind controlled character's dislike toward an experience or the idea of it.\r\n\r\n* [[questionable_consent]] - situations that raise doubt about consent but lack proof of non-consent. A character who does not [u]clearly[/u] first show a dislike and later enjoyment toward an experience should not get tagged with [i]until_they_like_it[/i]. In general, [i]until_they_like_it[/i] will clearly demonstrate dislike and enjoyment whereas [i]questionable_consent[/i] distorts dislike and/or enjoyment.\r\n\r\n* [[rape_play]] - consenting partners [[roleplay|roleplaying]] a rape fantasy.\r\n\r\n* [[secretly_loves_it]] - a character concealing their enjoyment of something, often by vocally expressing displeasure or suppressing their reactions. This can overlap with [i]until_they_like_it[/i], but usually the viewer sees that the character expressing displeasure in [i]secretly_loves_it[/i] has probably enjoyed their experience the entire time.\r\n\r\n* standard [[explicitly_stated_nonconsent]], [[forced]], [[rape]], etc - only tag [i]until_they_like_it[/i] if the victim does eventually express enjoyment. Do not tag [i]until_they_like_it[/i] just because the abuser is persistent or vows to continue their abuse \"until you like it.\" That is just standard non-consent, nothing special.\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nh5.Related tags\r\n\r\n* [[anal]] - a character may first experience discomfort or pain from anal stimulation but later enjoy it.\r\n** [[anal_penetration]]\r\n\r\n* [[defloration]], [[virgin]] - a character who hasn't experienced sex may feel discomfort as they start their first time, especially pain from a ruptured [[hymen]], but over time their discomfort eases and is replaced with pleasure.\r\n\r\n* [[instant_loss_2koma]] - a short image sequence showing a challenging or defiant character who next experiences \"instant loss.\"\r\n\r\n* [[masochism]] - a character feeling pleasure from pain or humiliation.\r\n\r\n* [[orientation_play]] - a post revealing a character's sexual orientation with events that challenge it.\r\n** [[gay_to_straight]]\r\n** [[straight_to_gay]]\r\n\r\n* [[stockholm_syndrome]] - a theorized behavior where a v