            "kor": "쿵쿵 뛰는",
            "count": 240,
            "wiki_body": "[b]Thumping[/b] is a dull sound produced when something impacts a surface that absorbs soundwaves. This sound effect is sometimes seen in [[vore]] to represent the muffled impacts of a [[struggling]] prey. It is also an alternative sound effect for a [[heartbeat]].\r\n\r\nAlthough this is an impact-related [[onomatopoeia]] (unless presented as \"Thmp\"), it is most likely better to use [[contact_onomatopoeia]] instead. Heartbeats probably should not be treated as impact sounds.\r\n\r\n[b]Sound effect forms:[/b] \"Thump\", \"Thmp\", \"Tump\", \"Ba-thump\", \"Ba-dump\", \"Ba-dum\"\r\n\r\nIf represented by \"Thmp\", or \"Tump\", tag as [[shortened_sound_effect]].\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[tag_group:sound_effects]]\r\n\r\n* [[sound_effects]]\r\n** [[bumping]]"
          },
          {
            "tag": "crucifixion",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 236,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #3586387 thumb #2293348 thumb #4795280\n\n[b]Crucifixion[/b] is a type of [[torture]] and [[execution]] invented by the Roman Empire as a more 'humane' alternative to [[impalement]] - in actual fact, it was no less [[cruelty|cruel]]. The condemned is [[bound|tied]] or [[nailed]] to a wooden beam in such a way that they need continuously to pull themselves up with their arms to breathe. Eventually, the victim either tires out, or their own body weight dislocates their arms, causing a slow, inevitable death by [[asphyxiation]]. Some executioners would prolong the victim's [[suffering]] with a [[cornu_(device)|cornu]] - a spiked seat that would prevent the victim from sagging too far down and quickly suffocating, with the drawback of [[genital_torture|impaling the victim's genitals]] if sat upon. The pain would incentivise the victim to continue pulling themselves up even when nearly exhausted, and prevent a quick death once they couldn't fight to hold themselves up anymore.\n\nDepending on their health and strength, a crucified person can remain alive for anywhere from several hours to several days, during which they would be subject to additional tortures like [[whipping]] and [[burning]]. Sometimes, if the condemned lingers for [i]too[/i] long, their [[crurifragium|legs are broken]] to ensure that they can't hold themselves up, or they are [[stabbed heart|stabbed through the heart]] to ensure death.\n\nBecause of its historical and sometimes religious significance, this [b][u]distinctive pose and predicament[/u][/b] has been shown in art for numerous symbolic reasons over the centuries.\n\nAlso, a similar pose and predicament is sometimes created using [[bondage]] and other tools during [[bdsm]] play. Obviously, the bondage version is temporary and non-fatal (much like the difference between a [[one-bar prison]] and an actual execution by [[impalement]]).\n\nh5.[b]Please note: This tag is used for the distinctive pose, situation and concept of crucifixion - even if no torture or death is depicted.[/b] \n\nh4. Related tags:\n\n* [[execution]] - The act of deliberately putting someone to death as a penalty for an offense\n* [[gore]] - The artistic depiction of extreme violence performed upon a character.\n* [[torture]] - A character inflicts severe mental or physical suffering on another character.\n* [[nailed]] - when nails or other small implements are driven through the flesh to secure a character in place\n* [[Cornu_(device)]] - a spike added to a crucifixion to impale the [[anus]] or [[vagina]].\n* [[bound]] - when rope or other types of bondage are used to bind a character in place\n* [[cross]] - a t-shaped cross, used for any purpose\n* [[saint_andrew's_cross]] - an x-shaped cross, most often used as bondage furniture and with BDSM play\n* [[crurifragium]] - term used for the procedure of breaking legs in the context of crucifixion\n* [[humiliation]] and [[public_humiliation]] - it is commonly believed that crucifixion as practised by Romans was intended to be both painful and humiliating form of execution. Related to this, common consensus among historians is that victims were crucified naked as opposed to the loinclothes usually seen in art and popular culture\n\nh4. See also:\n\n* [[death]] - A character is no longer living.\n* [[dying]] - A character is in the actual process of  succumbing to death.\n* [[impalement]] - Execution via penetrating the body with a stake or pole.\n* [[killing]] - The depiction of a character being killed.\n* [[murdered]] - The depiction of a character being killed [i]without[/i] any justification.\n* [[sadism]] - A character derives pleasure from inflicting pain, suffering, or humiliation on others.\n* [[snuff]] - A character derives sexual pleasure from death."
