              "count": 31283,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #2647597 thumb #2643278 thumb #2616008 thumb #2607764 thumb #2600502 thumb #4558816\n\nA [b] \"diaper\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diaper [/b] (also called a [b]nappy[/b]) is a type of [[underwear]], recognized by its puffed out appearance, that allows one to [[pooping|defecate]] or [[peeing|urinate]] without the use of a [[toilet]].\n\nThe release of feces into a diaper is known as [[soiling_diaper|soiling]], and the release of urine is known as [[wetting_diaper|wetting]]. Discoloration of a diaper is an indication that it is either [[soiled_diaper|soiled]] or [[wet_diaper|wet]]. When a diaper becomes soiled, it requires [[diaper_change|changing]], generally by a second person, such as a parent or caregiver. Failure to change a diaper can result in skin irritation known as [[diaper rash]].\n\nDiapers are primarily worn by [[baby|babies]] or [[child|children]], but are frequently depicted being worn by adult characters too.\n\n[section,expanded=Ratings]\n\n* Diapers may be rated [b][color=green]Safe[/color][/b] if the content isn't being fetishized. [i]Example: Children wearing diapers is perfectly normal and expected. Adults wearing them are not.[/i] Content with fetish material involving diapers should at the minimum be rated [b][color=yellow]Questionable[/color][/b].\n* Depictions of [[soiling]] or [[wetting]] involving diapers in all cases should be rated [color=tomato][b]Explicit[/b][/color].\n\n[/section]\n\n[section=Extra info]\nDiapers are made of cloth or synthetic disposable materials. Cloth diapers are composed of layers of fabric such as cotton, hemp, bamboo or microfiber, and can be washed and reused multiple times. Disposable diapers contain absorbent chemicals, and are thrown away after use. Plastic pants can be worn over diapers to avoid leaks, but with modern cloth diapers, this is no longer necessary.\n\nDiapers are primarily worn by [[baby|babies]], and by [[child|children]] who are not yet potty trained or who