              "kor": "",
              "count": 3959,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #4784981 thumb #287426 thumb #3550029 thumb #1399175 thumb #4802759 thumb #4676034\r\n\r\n[b]Lolita[/b] is a Japanese street fashion style inspired by the fashion of the Victorian and Rococo periods. The style emerged in the 1980s and is still going strong, having evolved greatly and becoming arguably the most well-known J-Fashion style. It primarily utilizes bell-shaped [[skirt]]s and [[dress|dresses]] (using a [[petticoat]] for the \"poofy\" look), usually adorned with lace and ribbons. In the real world, Lolita has a strict modesty guideline, but in artwork, that tends to be disregarded. Lolita is a highly diverse style with many different substyles. The main three substyles are [[gothic_lolita|Gothic]], [[classic_lolita|Classic]], and [[sweet_lolita|Sweet]].\r\n\r\nLolita fashion is seen as a reactionary movement to Japanese gender norms and cultural standards, choosing to wear \"childish\" clothing and exaggerate femininity to an extreme degree. While not all who wear the fashion do so as a statement, many do, and it is an important part of the culture associated with the style.\r\n\r\nOutfits in Lolita are commonly referred to as \"coords\". Not all coords will fit into a single, or any, substyles - substyles are aethetics and themes that are common enough to have been named by the Lolita community and do not cover all the possible ways one can wear Lolita.\r\n\r\nThe masculine counterpart of Lolita is [[ouji_(fashion)|Ouji]], which it is often paired with in matching coords.\r\n\r\n[section=Lolita substyles]\r\n* [[Casual Lolita]]\r\n* [[Classic Lolita]] - Traditional/Victorian\r\n* [[Cosplay Lolita]]\r\n* [[Country Lolita]]\r\n* [[Cyber Lolita]] - Fusion with [[Cyberpop]]\r\n* [[Deco Lolita]] - Fusion with [[decora_(fashion)|Decora]]\r\n* [[Ero Lolita]] - Features erotic elements\r\n* [[Gothic Lolita]]\r\n* [[Guro Lolita]]\r\n* [[Hime Lolita]] - \"Princess\" aesthetic\r\n* [[Kuro Lolita]] - Primarily black, often paired in duo coords with Shiro Lolita\r\n* [[Military Lolita]]\r\n* [[Nurse Lolita]]\r\n* [[Pirate Lolita]]\r\n* [[Punk Lolita]]\r\n* [[Qi Lolita]] - Traditional Chinese inspiration\r\n* [[Sailor Lolita]] - Nautical aesthetic, inspiration from sailor uniforms\r\n* [[Shiro Lolita]] - Primarily white, often paired in duo coords with Kuro Lolita\r\n* [[Steampunk Lolita]]\r\n* [[Sweet Lolita]]\r\n* [[Wa Lolita]] - Traditional Japanese inspiration\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nh4. External links\r\n* \"Lolita fashion\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lolita_fashion on Wikipedia\r\n* \"Lolita styles\":https://lolitafashion.fandom.com/wiki/Category:Lolita_Styles on Lolita Fashion Wiki\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[ouji_(fashion)]]\r\n* [[victorian]]\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
