        "slit_pupils": [
          {
            "tag": "slit_pupils",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 52101,
            "wiki_body": "Characters with straight or oval shaped pupils. It's a feature commonly associated with predators or nocturnal creatures, and has been used in art for a long time to make characters seem more inhuman or evil.\r\n\r\nThe most commonly known example of this is [[cat|cats]], but this does not apply to most [[felines]], as most big cats ([[lion|lions]], [[panther|panthers]], [[tiger|tigers]], etc.) have round pupils. Some species of [[reptile|reptiles]], such as vipers (but not all snakes) and crocodiles also have those. Which animals have them or not seems to have more to do with their hunting style (usually ambushing) and their activity periods (crepuscular or nocturnal) than their biological family.\r\n\r\nOval or slit pupils can also be [[horizontal_pupils|horizontal]], as is the case for [[goat|goats]] and [[horse|horses]], among others. Sometimes drawn with [[no iris]] and a \"[color]_sclera\":/tag?name=*_sclera.\r\n\r\nh4.Examples:\r\nthumb #776302 thumb #836098 thumb #787835\r\n\r\nh4.Pupil (anatomy) tags:\r\n\r\n[section=Colors]\r\n* [[amber_pupils]]\r\n* [[black_pupils]]\r\n* [[blue_pupils]]\r\n* [[cyan_pupils]]\r\n* [[brown_pupils]]\r\n* [[green_pupils]]\r\n* [[grey_pupils]]\r\n* [[orange_pupils]]\r\n* [[pink_pupils]]\r\n* [[purple_pupils]]\r\n* [[red_pupils]]\r\n* [[tan_pupils]]\r\n* [[teal_pupils]]\r\n* [[white_pupils]]\r\n* [[yellow_pupils]]\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\n[section=Other]\r\n* [[big_pupils]]\r\n* [[horizontal_pupils]]\r\n* [[no_pupils]]\r\n* [b]slit_pupils[/b]\r\n* [[small_pupils]]\r\n* [[square_pupils]]\r\n[/section]"
