      "alternative_fashion": {
        "_Self (alternative_fashion)": [
          {
            "tag": "alternative_fashion",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 36610,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #3227267 thumb #2128528 thumb #3542963 thumb #3400607 thumb #2970353\r\n\r\n[b]Alternative fashion[/b] is an umbrella term for fashion styles that deviate from the mainstream norm, which are typically associated with specific subcultures.\r\n\r\nNote that in this context, \"fashion\" refers to the character's overall presentation and personal style, rather than clothing in particular.\r\n\r\nIf a character seems to not fit into any particular style, or take elements from a number of styles, it may be a better fit to simply use this umbrella tag rather than tag several styles.\r\n\r\n[section,expanded=Common elements of alternative fashion]\r\nAlternative fashion tends to be darker and more macabre than mainstream fashion, often heavily featuring black and themes of horror and gore. Other styles tend to overexaggerate bright colors and sweet themes to a degree that is not present in mainstream fashion.\r\n\r\nAlternative fashion is also very counterculture - [[politics|political]] iconography is common, especially left-wing and [[anarchism|anarchist]] iconography in particular. Gender norms are often either disregarded entirely or exaggerated to make a point (see [[lolita_(fashion)|lolita]] and [[gyaru]]).\r\n\r\nIn regards to specific visual elements:\r\n* [[Tattoo]]s and [[piercing]]s\r\n* Studs, [[spikes]], [[straps]]\r\n* [[Lace]], [[ribbons]] and [[bow_(feature)|bows]]\r\n* [[Pattern clothing]], particularly [[stripes]], [[animal print]], [[plaid]] and [[checkered]]\r\n* [[Converse]], [[platform shoes]], [[boots]]\r\n* [[patch_(fabric)|Patches]] and [[pin button]]s\r\n* [[Belt]]s, sometimes multiple, and [[chain]]s\r\n* [[Arm warmers]] and [[leg warmers]]\r\n* [[Fishnets]]\r\n* Religious symbols such as the [[cross]] and the [[pentacle]] (both normal and inverted)\r\n* Mismatched clothing and accessories\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\n[section=Alternative fashion styles]\r\n* [[emo]]\r\n* [[flapper]]\r\n* [[glamrock]]\r\n* [[goth]]\r\n** [[cybergoth]]\r\n** [[mall_goth]]\r\n** [[pastel_goth]]\r\n** [[trad_goth]]\r\n* [[greaser]]\r\n* [[grunge_(fashion)]]\r\n* [[hippie]]\r\n* [[hipster]]\r\n* [[j-fashion]]\r\n** [[cyberpop]]\r\n** [[decora_(fashion)]]\r\n** [[fairy_kei]]\r\n** [[gyaru]]\r\n** [[gurokawa]]\r\n** [[jirai_kei]]\r\n** [[lolita_(fashion)]]\r\n** [[menhera]]\r\n** [[ouji_(fashion)]]\r\n** [[visual_kei]]\r\n* [[metalhead]]\r\n* [[punk]]\r\n** [[crust_punk]]\r\n* [[rockabilly]]\r\n* [[scene_(fashion)]]\r\n* [[steampunk]]\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]] - Tag group for fashion styles, includes visual guide\r\n\r\nh4.Not to be confused with\r\n\r\n* [[alternate_costume]] - When a character is wearing clothing different from their usual outfit."
          }
        ],
        "goth": {
          "_Self (goth)": [
            {
              "tag": "goth",
              "kor": "고스",
              "count": 14258,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #2184419 thumb #628227 thumb #3711535 thumb #3554982 thumb #4706842\r\n\r\n[b]Goth[/b] is a music-based alternative subculture that began in the late 80s in the United Kingdom. While the music is what defines goth in the real world, this tag refers to the fashion style.\r\n\r\nGoth fashion focuses on the dark and mysterious, often heavily utilizing black. There are many variations of goth fashion. It is often confused for other alternative styles such as [[punk]] and [[emo]]. Common elements of goth fashion include [[fishnet]] and [[lace]], [[long gloves]], [[bdsm]] imagery, religious iconography, colors such as red and purple, roses, dark [[makeup]], etc.\r\n\r\nh4. Goth substyles\r\n* [[Cybergoth]]\r\n* [[Gothic lolita]]\r\n* [[Mall goth]]\r\n* [[Pastel goth]]\r\n* [[Trad goth]]\r\n* [[Victorian goth]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
            }
          ],
          "mall_goth": [
            {
              "tag": "mall_goth",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 229,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #3484119 thumb #248835 thumb #4125269\r\n\r\n[b]Mall goth[/b] is a subcategory of [[goth]] that started in the 1990s. The term was originally a derogatory term for \"fake\" goths, but eventually developed into its own subculture. Mall goths are primarily associated with nu metal and industrial music, and of course, malls, especially [[Hot Topic]]. \r\n\r\n[quote]\r\nh5. Elements of mall goth fashion\r\n* [[Platform boots]], chunky [[sneakers]], [[combat boots]]\r\n* [[Oversized clothing]]\r\n** Especially sweatshirts and pants\r\n* [[Stripes]] and [[checkered]] patterns\r\n** Particularly on [[legwear]]\r\n** [[Plaid]] is common on [[skirt|skirts]]\r\n* [[Straps]], [[studs]] and [[spikes]]\r\n** [[Bondage pants]]\r\n* [[Choker|Chokers]]\r\n* All kinds of [[piercing|piercings]]\r\n** [[industrial piercing|Industrial piercings]]\r\n** [[lip piercing|Lip piercings]]\r\n* Heavy [[makeup]]\r\n** Raccoon-like [[eyeliner]]\r\n* Dyed hair\r\n[/quote]\r\n\r\nh4. External links\r\n* \"Mall goth\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mall_goth on Wikipedia\r\n* \"Mallgoth\":https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Mallgoth on Aesthetics Wiki\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
            }
          ],
          "trad_goth": [
            {
              "tag": "trad_goth",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 213,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #3008515 thumb #4286055 thumb #1340700 thumb #4257479 thumb #517414\n\nA shortening of \"traditional [[goth]]\". Goth as a style has evolved and diversified greatly over the years, and \"trad goth\" is a term used to refer to the roots of the overall style, primarily drawing from the gothic aesthetics of the 70s and 80s. \n\n[section,expanded=Common elements of trad goth fashion]\n* Androgyny\n* Dark colors, primarily black, grey, red, and purple\n* [[Torn clothing]]\n* [[piercing|Piercings]]\n* [[Platform boots]]\n* Traditional gothic iconography: the [[cross]], [[bat|bats]], the [[pentagram]], [[skull|skulls]], etc.\n* [[Beaded jewelry]]\n* [[Fishnet]] and other mesh clothing, particularly [[gloves]] and [[legwear]]\n* Heavy [[eyeliner]]\n* [[Messy hair]]\n* [[Lipstick]] in shades of red, black, and purple\n* [[leather_jacket|Leather jackets]]\n* Studded accessories\n* [[hat|Hats]] and [[veil|veils]]\n[/section]\n\nh4. See also\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
            }
          ],
          "pastel_goth": [
            {
              "tag": "pastel_goth",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 192,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #1636674 thumb #2128528 thumb #3993738 thumb #1339025\r\n\r\n[b]Pastel goth[/b] is a fashion that is a mix of [[goth]] fashion with a girly/[[pastel]] elements. It incorporates traditional gothic elements like skulls, crosses, bats, and occult symbols, but in softer and more whimsical forms. \r\n\r\nIt is somewhat similar to [[gurokawa]] and [[menhera]], but the three are distinct – while all mix pastel colors with darker themes, gurokawa focuses on macabre and gory imagery, and menhera focuses on medical imagery.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
            }
          ],
          "cybergoth": [
            {
              "tag": "cybergoth",
              "kor": "사이버고스",
              "count": 118,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #1509567 thumb #97361 thumb #1122256\r\n\r\n[b]Cybergoth[/b] is a subculture that is derived from [[goth]], [[rave]] and [[cyberpunk]] fashion. Cybergoth is primarily associated with raves, techno music, and industrial music.\r\n\r\nIt is not to be confused with [[cyberpop]], a [[j-fashion]] style it shares some similarities with.\r\n\r\n[section,expanded=Elements of Cybergoth fashion]\r\n* Androgyny\r\n* Bright, contrasting colors against black\r\n* [[rubber_clothing|Rubber]], [[latex_clothing|latex]], and PVC\r\n* Brightly colored hair and [[makeup]]\r\n* [[Cybernetics]] and general technology elements\r\n* Body modification\r\n* [[gas_mask|Gas masks]]\r\n* [[Goggles]]\r\n* [[Bondage pants]] and [[phat pants]]\r\n* [[Hair extensions]], particularly [[dreadfalls]]\r\n* Artificial hair like [[cyberlox]]\r\n* [[fishnet|Fishnets]]\r\n* [[Fluffies]] and [[platform boots]]\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
            }
          ]
        },
        "punk": [
          {
            "tag": "punk",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 6087,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #4109614 thumb #161499 thumb #4314620 thumb #3697559 thumb #3211014 thumb #1246541\r\n\r\n[b]Punk[/b] is a subculture associated with punk rock originating in the 1970s, or aspects of the subculture such as fashion, ideologies, and visual art. Punks are associated with [[anarchism]] and other left-wing ideology, and rebellion from social norms. Punk fashion is derived from multiple older alternative styles such as [[greaser]]s and [[glamrock]]. Clothing is often DIY or thrifted, due to punk's association with anti-consumerism.\r\n\r\nHairstyles are often brightly colored and spiky, with a particularly common style being the [[mohawk]]. Clothing often features a [[plaid]] pattern or [[print_clothing|prints]] related to bands or political iconography. [[Combat boots]] and [[sneakers]] are the most common types of footwear. [[Leather jacket]]s are also common, typically decorated with [[patch_(fabric)|patches]] and pins.\r\n\r\nh4. Punk substyles\r\n* [[Crust punk]]\r\n* [[Punk lolita]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
        "emo": [
          {
            "tag": "emo",
            "kor": "에모",
            "count": 2817,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #2570954 thumb #551144 thumb #3588501 thumb #88089 thumb #3985077\r\n\r\n[b]Emo[/b] is a subculture that originated in mid-1990s alternative music scenes, being primarily associated with pop punk, post-hardcore, and the namesake genre, emo. The fashion style associated with the emo culture is often mistaken for [[goth]] and [[scene_(fashion)|scene]] fashion, despite the three being very visually distinct.\r\n\r\nThe most recognizable element of emo fashion is the iconic [[emo haircut]], which has straight, flat, choppy bangs that usually cover one eye. [[Scene hair]] styles are also common, which have the same fringe bangs but with a focus on the hair being larger, fluffier, and often more colorful.\r\n\r\n[section,expanded=Elements of emo fashion]\r\n* Thick rimmed [[glasses]]\r\n* [[Skinny jeans]]\r\n* Tight [[t-shirt|t-shirts]]\r\n* Band merch\r\n* [[studded_belt|Studded belts]]\r\n* [[Sneakers]] such as Vans and Converse\r\n* [[wristband|Wristbands]]\r\n* [[Eyeliner]]\r\n* [[Black fingernails]]\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
        "gyaru": {
          "_Self (gyaru)": [
            {
              "tag": "gyaru",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 2540,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #4657254 thumb #4099855 thumb #382823 thumb #3368554 thumb #1116968\n\n[b]Gyaru[/b] is a [[j-fashion]] style that began in the 1980s. It is a rebellion against the typical Japanese beauty standards, instead drawing more inspiration from Western fashion and adopting a \"party girl\" aesthetic. The term gyaru is a transliteration of the english word \"gal\".\n\nGyaru fashion is typically characterized by having heavily bleached or dyed hair (mostly shades from dark brown to blonde), highly decorated nails, tanned skin, and dramatic makeup. The makeup typically consists of dark eyeliner and fake eyelashes used in ways intended to make the eyes appear larger, as well as contouring of the face for a slimming effect.\n\nMale gyaru are known as [b]gyaruo[/b].\n\n[section,expanded=Common elements]\n\nh5. Hair\n* Big hair, often teased or [[curled_hair|curled]]\n* Hair extensions\n* [[Blonde hair]] and [[tan_hair|light brown hair]]\n\nh5. Makeup\n* Long, colorful [[nails]]\n* Emphasis on the eyes\n** False eyelashes\n** Light or colorful [[eyeshadow]]\n* Colorful [[lipstick]]\n* Dark tans\n\nh5. Clothing\n* Fur, faux fur, and [[leather]]\n* [[Animal print]] and [[camo]]\n* [[Denim]]\n* Designer brands\n* [[Crop top|Crop tops]], [[bikini top|bikini tops]]\n* [[Hotpants]] and [[miniskirt|miniskirts]]\n* [[Arm warmers]], [[leg warmers]] and [[loose socks]]\n* [[Fishnet|Fishnets]] and [[stockings]]\n* [[High heels]], [[platform shoes]], [[fur boots]]\n\nh5. Accessories\n* [[Bangle|Bangles]] and [[necklace|necklaces]]\n* [[Hat|Hats]], [[hairclip|hairclips]], [[tiara|tiaras]], big [[hair bow|hair bows]]\n* [[Sunglasses]], often worn on the head\n* [[chain_accessory|Chain accessories]]\n* [[Beads]]\n* [[Phone charm|Phone charms]]\n\nh5. Motifs\n* [[Hibiscus]] and [[tropical]] aesthetics\n* [[Gem|Gems]], [[gold_(metal)|gold]], and other \"luxury\" materials\n* The colors pink, black, tan and gold\n* Hip hop and [[streetwear]] influences\n[/section]\n\nh4. Substyles\n* [[ganguro (fashion)]] - Very dark tans with light hair.\n** [[manba (fashion)]] - A form of ganguro with a focus on bright makeup and colorful accessories.\n* [[kogal]] - A substyle that utilizes school uniforms.\n* [[onee gyaru]] - A \"classier\" gyaru substyle, that tends to be more mature.\n* [[tsuyome]] - A flashy style that usually includes bold makeup and print clothing.\n* [[rock gyaru]] - A substyle inspired by [[punk]] fashion.\n* [[hime gyaru]] - A substyle somewhat related to [[lolita (fashion)]], that is \"princess-like\" and often focuses on pink, frilly clothes, and jewelry.\n* [[amekaji]] - A substyle inspired by American fashion.\n\nh4. See also\n* [[bimbofication]]\n* [[y2k_(fashion)]]\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
            }
          ],
          "kogal": [
            {
              "tag": "kogal",
