            "kor": "",
            "count": 1711,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #3158329 thumb #2721517 thumb #2103295 thumb #1208798 thumb #580123 thumb #259188\r\n\r\n[b]Scene[/b] is an alternative subculture that originated in the [[2000s]]. Members of the subculture are called scene kids, scenesters, or scene kings and queens. The subculture is associated with various music genres, but particularly crunkcore, metalcore, electronic, and pop punk.\r\n\r\nThe most distinctive feature of scene fashion is [[scene_hair|the hair]], which borrows the fringe bangs that often cover one eye from typical emo hairstyles, and instead focuses on the hair being big, bright, and fluffy. [[coontail_hair|Coontails]] are a staple of scene hair. \r\n\r\nScene is often confused with [[emo]], although the two have distinct differences. Scene fashion is much more colorful, as it tries to draw attention to the wearer.\r\n\r\nMany scene kids were furries, and a subgenre of [[sparkledog]] art involved scene fashion, particularly with the gaudy accessories and big fluffy hair. These types of sparkledogs were known as scenedogs. \r\n\r\n[section,expanded=Elements of scene fashion]\r\n* Brightly colored clothing, and clothing with prints and patterns\r\n* [[Skinny jeans]]\r\n* [[Sunglasses]], particularly [[shutter shades]] and any oversized sunglasses\r\n* [[Piercings]]\r\n* [[studded_belt|Studded belts]] and belts with large buckles\r\n* [[wristband|Wristbands]]\r\n* [[necklace|Necklaces]] with large plastic symbols, such as diamonds and skulls\r\n* Copyrighted characters such as [[hello_kitty_(series)|Hello Kitty]], [[GIR]] from [[Invader Zim]], [[Gloomy Bear]] and [[Domo]]\r\n* [[dinosaur|Dinosaurs]] and the word \"rawr\"\r\n* Large, colorful [[hair_bow|hair bows]]\r\n* Raccooon-eye [[eyeliner]]\r\n* Plastic [[pearl_necklace|pearl necklaces]]\r\n* Colorful beaded bracelets known as [[kandi_(jewelry)|kandi]]\r\n* [[Bondage pants]]\r\n* Band t-shirts\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
        "cyberpop": [
          {
            "tag": "cyberpop",
            "kor": "사이버팝",
            "count": 892,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #4501500 thumb #3920107 thumb #4608515 thumb #3526191 thumb #4808726\r\n\r\n[b]Cyberpop[/b], known as simply \"Cyber Fashion\" in Japan, is a [[j-fashion]] style that originated in Harajuku in the 1990s. \r\n\r\nThe definining aspect of the style is its use of artificial-looking materials such as [[latex]], vinyl, and faux fur. Clothing is often baggy and loose-fitting, flared pants are common, and bright fluorescent colors are a staple. Some elements of the outfit might be [[glowing]] or [[translucent]]. Accessories are often made of brightly colored plastic, and [[headphones]] are also frequently worn.\r\n\r\nThe style is associated with the [[cyberpunk]] genre and [[rave]]s, and sometimes overlaps with other fashion styles.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
        "ouji_(fashion)": [
          {
            "tag": "ouji_(fashion)",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 701,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #4007754 thumb #4659611 thumb #4369464 thumb #2854200 thumb #2824247\r\n\r\n[b]Ouji[/b], also sometimes referred to as [b]boystyle[/b], is a Japanese street fashion style inspired by the fashion of the Victorian and Edwardian eras, especially young boys’ clothing, and the masculine equivalent of [[lolita_(fashion)|Lolita]]. Both men and women wear Ouji, with women often engaging in [[crossdressing]].\r\n\r\nIn contrast to the skirts commonly used in Lolita, Ouji typically uses [[pants]] of various lengths, along with [[vest]]s and [[blouse]]s. Accessories include [[top hat]]s, [[pirate hat]]s, [[military hat]]s, [[pocket_watch|pocket watches]], [[necktie|ties]], and [[jabot]]s. More traditionally feminine accessories such as [[bow_(feature)|bows]], [[crown]]s, [[headdress|headdresses]] and [[brooch]]es may also be worn.\r\n\r\nIt is often paired together with Lolita in duo pictures:\r\n\r\nthumb #3992909 thumb #2982537\r\n\r\n[section=Common elements]\r\n* [[ascot]]\r\n* [[bow_(feature)]]\r\n** [[bow_tie]]\r\n* [[cape]]\r\n* [[cloak]]\r\n* [[coat]]\r\n** [[tailcoat]]\r\n* [[dress_shirt]]\r\n* [[frilly]]\r\n* [[gloves]]\r\n** [[elbow_gloves]]\r\n* [[hat]]\r\n** [[top_hat]]\r\n* [[shorts]]\r\n* [[suit]]\r\n* [[suspenders]]\r\n* [[vest]]\r\n** [[sweater_vest]]\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[formal_wear]]\r\n* [[lolita_(fashion)]]\r\n* [[steampunk]]\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
        "hippie": [
          {
            "tag": "hippie",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 346,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #642831 thumb #3419283 thumb #2708085 thumb #196942\n\nThe [b]hippie[/b] movement was a counterculture movement in the 1960s. Hippies are heavily associated with psychedelic music, drugs, sexual liberation, and a desire for peace. This tag in particular refers to the fashion associated with this movement.\n\nCommon elements of the style include [[tie-dye]], [[bandana|bandanas]], handmade jewelry, clothing with fringe, and [[sunglasses]]. Hippie fashion often incorporates iconography such as [[peace symbol|peace symbols]], [[smiley face|smiley faces]], [[flower|flowers]], and [[mushroom|mushrooms]].\n\nh4. See also\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
        "jirai_kei": [
          {
            "tag": "jirai_kei",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 299,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #4564883 thumb #3453607 thumb #3561502 thumb #4214779\r\n\r\n[b]Jirai Kei[/b] is a style of popular Japanese fashion that originated in the Kabukicho district and started to gain popularity in the 2020s. \r\n\r\nCommonly translated as \"Landmine Type\", the term is taken from the wearers of this fashion, with \"jirai joshi\" (landmine girl) being used to refer to girls who wear girly fashion and have mental health issues. The term has since been largely reclaimed as a neutral descriptor of fashion, but still has some loaded connotations behind it.\r\n\r\nIt has associations with [[lolita (fashion)|lolita]], [[menhera]], yami kawaii, and other forms of feminine [[j-fashion]].\r\n\r\nJirai Kei fashion often features muted pinks paired with black, frilly high-waisted skirts, ribbons, pearls and other jewelry, blouses, platform shoes, leg warmers, and more. [[Sanrio]] characters are also heavily associated with the style, particularly [[Kuromi]] and [[My Melody]].\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
        "decora_(fashion)": [
          {
            "tag": "decora_(fashion)",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 243,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #3208623 thumb #4712779 thumb #4688493 thumb #4191285 thumb #3433924\r\n\r\n[b]Decora[/b] is a Japanese street fashion style popularized in Harajuku. It focuses on bright colors and an excessive amount of accessories.\r\n\r\nCommon accessories include [[hairclip|hair clips]], [[mask|masks]], [[band-aid|band-aids]], [[ribbons]] and bows, [[bracelet|bracelets]], [[necklace|necklaces]], [[ring_(jewelry)|rings]], and more. Other common aspects of Decora fashion are cartoon [[print_clothing|prints]], brightly-colored patterns, layered clothing, mismatched clothing, and frilly/puffy skirts.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
        "flapper": [
          {
            "tag": "flapper",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 188,
            "wiki_body": "A young woman from 1920s America with short hair cuts, given to wearing clothing generally much more revealing than a \"proper\" lady would wear, smoking, drinking, and generally flouting the social and sexual norms of the period."
          }
        ],
        "menhera": [
          {
            "tag": "menhera",
            "kor": "멘헤라",
            "count": 187,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #3326933 thumb #3860348 thumb #2597534\r\n\r\n[b]Menhera[/b], sometimes also known as [b]Yami Kawaii[/b], is a [[j-fashion]] style that aims to de-stigmatize mental health issues, especially in women. It originated in Japan's Harajuku district, and derives its name from \"menhera\" being a shortened slang term for \"mental healther\".\r\n\r\nThe style incorporates [[medical]] and [[gore]] imagery and themes of mental health, mixing them with \"kawaii\" aesthetics and [[pastel]] colors for a sickly-sweet overall appearance. Common iconography includes the [[red cross]], [[box cutter]]s, [[razor blade]]s, [[syringe]]s, [[pills]], and more. [[Eye patch]]es are often worn, along with [[nurse clothing]].\r\n\r\nIt has some similarities visually with [[nurse lolita]], [[guro lolita]], [[gurokawa]] and [[pastel goth]], and thematic similarities with [[jirai kei]].\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
        "visual_kei": [
          {
            "tag": "visual_kei",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 139,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #4214920 thumb #15283 thumb #4155084\r\n\r\n[b]Visual Kei[/b] is a genre of Japanese rock music and an associated fashion style that originated in the 1980s. It is similar to the western [[Glamrock]] style, focusing on flashy costumes and androgyny.\r\n\r\n[[Leather]], PVC, [[lace]], and [[latex]] are commonly used in visual kei clothing. Hair is often dyed bright colors and men tend to have long hair.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[cybergoth]]\r\n* [[glamfur]]\r\n* [[glamrock]]\r\n* [[mall goth]]\r\n* [[punk]]\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
