        "greaser": [
          {
            "tag": "greaser",
            "kor": "그리서",
            "count": 117,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #2972911 thumb #3119595 thumb #1834493 thumb #4297356\r\n\r\n[b]Greasers[/b] are a youth subculture that was prevalent in the United States in the 1950s and early 1960s. The name comes from their [[hair]] which is typically greasy from heavy use of product. It is often styled into a [[pompadour]] or otherwise coiffed, necessitating a [[comb_(brush)|comb]] be carried for frequent restyling.\r\n\r\nTypical fashion consists of a [[leather jacket]], tight [[t-shirt]], [[jeans]], and [[boots]] or [[chuck taylor all stars]].\r\n\r\nThey are often pictured with vintage [[car|cars]], [[motorcycle|motorcycles]], [[cigarette|cigarettes]], [[knife|knives]], and [[baseball bat|baseball bats]], as well as hallmarks of '50s culture, such as [[rock_(genre)|rock and roll]], [[drive-in|drive-ins]], and classic [[diner|diners]].\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
        "grunge_(fashion)": [
          {
            "tag": "grunge_(fashion)",
            "kor": "",
            "count": 112,
            "wiki_body": "thumb #4575286 thumb #1402055 thumb #2592342\r\n\r\n[b]Grunge fashion[/b] refers to the clothing, accessories and hairstyles of the grunge music genre. This subculture emerged in mid-1980s Seattle, and had reached wide popularity by the mid 1990s.\r\n\r\nGrunge fashion is characterized by durable and timeless thrift-store clothing, often worn in a loose, androgynous manner to de-emphasize the silhouette.\r\n\r\nNot to be confused with [[grunge]], referring to grime/dirt/rust on objects.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[tag group:fashion]]"
          }
        ],
