            "lyre": [
              {
                "tag": "lyre",
                "kor": "",
                "count": 149,
                "wiki_body": "The [b]lyre[/b] is a string [[musical_instrument|instrument]] known for its use in Greek classical antiquity and later periods. The lyre is similar in appearance to a small [[harp]] but with distinct differences.\r\n\r\nThe lyre of classical antiquity was ordinarily played by being strummed with a plectrum (pick), like a guitar or a zither, rather than being plucked with the fingers as with a harp. The fingers of the free hand silenced the unwanted strings in the chord. However, later lyres were played with a bow, including the Europe and parts of the Middle East. One example from England that has been resurrected recently is the crwth.\r\n\r\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyre"
              }
            ]
          },
          "violin": [
            {
              "tag": "violin",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 539,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #102062\r\n\r\nThe [b]violin[/b] is a wooden string [[musical_instrument|instrument]] in the violin family. It is the smallest and highest-pitched instrument in the family in regular use. Smaller violin-type instruments are known, including the violino piccolo and the kit violin, but these are virtually unused in the 2010s. The violin typically has four strings tuned in perfect fifths, and is most commonly played by drawing a [[bow_(stringed_instrument)|bow]] across its strings, though it can also be played by plucking the strings with the fingers (pizzicato). The violin is sometimes informally called a fiddle.\r\n\r\nhttp://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violin\r\n\r\nh4. See also:\r\n* [[bow_(stringed_instrument)]]\r\n* [[musical_instrument]]\r\n* [[music]]"
            }
          ],
          "cello": [
            {
              "tag": "cello",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 515,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #502850\r\n\r\nThe [b]cello[/b] is a bowed string [[musical_instrument|instrument]] with four strings tuned in perfect fifths. The strings from low to high are generally tuned to C2, G2, D3 and A3, an octave lower than the viola. It is a member of the violin family of musical instruments. A person who plays the cello is called a cellist.\r\n\r\nhttp://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cello.\r\n\r\nh4. See also:\r\n* [[bow_(stringed_instrument)]]\r\n* [[musical_instrument]]\r\n* [[music]]"
            }
          ]
