              "kor": "",
              "count": 645,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #2223987 thumb #2800529 thumb #1521015 thumb #1906356\r\n\r\nFlags used to showcase lesbian pride specifically, in contrast to the broader [[rainbow_pride_colors|rainbow flag]] that's also frequently used. They come in several variants:\r\n* [[orange_lesbian_pride_colors]] — A flag with seven stripes or five stripes, arranged orange-white-pink, popularized in 2018.\r\n* [[pink_lesbian_pride_colors]] — A pink flag with seven stripes most commonly used between 2013 and 2018, visually the same as [[lipstick lesbian flag|lipstick_lesbian_pride_colors]] without the kiss symbol.\r\n* [[lipstick_lesbian_pride_colors]] — A pink flag with seven stripes and a kiss symbol meant for femme lesbians, visually the same as [[pink lesbian flag|pink_lesbian_pride_colors]] with a kiss symbol.\r\n* [[labrys_lesbian_pride_colors]] — A purple flag featuring a white labrys symbol inside an inverted black triangle.\r\n* [[alternate_lesbian_pride_colors]] — Catch-all tag for other lesbian flag designs.\r\n\r\nh4.See also:\r\n* [[rainbow_pride_colors]]\r\n* [[bisexual_pride_colors]]\r\n* [[pansexual_pride_colors]]\r\n* [[transgender_pride_colors]]\r\n* [[nonbinary_pride_colors]]"
            }
          ],
          "nonbinary_pride_colors": [
            {
              "tag": "nonbinary_pride_colors",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 630,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #1892751 thumb #2085357 thumb #2796172\r\n\r\nA horizontal tetraband of yellow, white, purple and black.\r\n\r\nh4.See also:\r\n* [[rainbow_flag]]\r\n* [[lesbian_pride_colors]]\r\n* [[bisexual_pride_colors]]\r\n* [[pansexual_pride_colors]]\r\n* [[transgender_pride_colors]]"
            }
          ],
          "vincian_pride_colors": [
            {
              "tag": "vincian_pride_colors",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 549,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #3054402 thumb #2779116 \r\n\r\n“Vincian Pride Colors” or “trans-inclusive gay male pride colors”\r\n\r\nVincian is one of multiple terms used to describe gay men or men-aligned people. It is one of the most widely recognized alternatives to the term \"gay men\" and is often used as the masculine equivalent of lesbian (referring to exclusive attraction). It is sometimes also used as a variant of achillean (referring to any MLM attraction, exclusive or not), due to miscommunication on the part of the coiner.\r\n\r\nThe term \"gay\" is used as an adjective to describe homosexuality, and is also often used by the entire LGBT community in general. For this reason, various proposed terms for homosexual men were coined, including vincian. Some individuals also argue that \"gay\" should just mean homosexual men all together, due to the fact that gay women are referred to as lesbian. Other people simply refer to homosexual men as \"gay men\"."
            }
          ],
          "aromantic_pride_colors": [
            {
              "tag": "aromantic_pride_colors",
              "kor": "",
              "count": 103,
              "wiki_body": "thumb #2093839 thumb #2787827 thumb #5628356\n\nA flag used to represent aromanticism, an identity defined by little to no romantic attraction. It features five stripes: green, light green, white, gray, and black.\n\nThese stripes represent aromanticism, the aromantic spectrum, aesthetic attraction, gray-aromantic and demiromantic people, and the various sexualities an aromantic person may be.\n\nNot to be confused with \"asexuality\":/wiki_pages/27890, which is the lack of [i]sexual[/i] attraction."
            }
          ]
        }
