          "tag": "kemono",
          "kor": "케모노",
          "count": 130759,
          "wiki_body": "Japanese for \"beast\" or \"animal\" (獣, けもの), this tag is for a specific style of anthropomorphic art associated best with Japan, but may also be found in Korea, China, Thailand, Taiwan, and other Asian countries and regions. Characters portrayed are usually very \"[[anime_style|anime]]\" in style, though this can vary. Most notable features include large, human-like eyes and otherwise anime-like expressiveness. A mainstream media example of this style is [[Sajin Komamura]], a character as depicted in [i][[Bleach (series)|Bleach]][/i].\n\nNot to be confused with [[kemonomimi]], a different Japanese term used to define a different style and level of anthropomorphism, or with [i][[Kemono Friends]][/i], a popular series about kemonomimi characters. Also not to be confused with [[kimono]], a Japanese garment.\n\nWhile the typical [[chibi]] style is more obvious, it's far from the only variation. Some consistent stylistic choices include:\n\n* Large, box-shaped head and snout (often rounded), with more realistic proportions; common with masculine characters and/or canids, dragons, and big cats.\n* Triangular faces with a prominent muzzle, similar to the box-faced variant; common with canids, feminine characters, and slim male characters.\n* Faces with a simple muzzle and a humanlike head shape. Mostly seen with feminine characters.\n* Circular, flatter/blunter face that have exaggerated facial details (eyes, etc); common with cutesy or chibi characters and also domestic cats.\n\nh4. Examples\n[section,expanded=Box-shaped head and face/snout]\nthumb #1190286 thumb #1467515 thumb #1525696\n[/section]\n\n[section,expanded=Triangular face]\nthumb #2932956 thumb #2109492 thumb #3336662\n[/section]\n\n[section,expanded=Humanlike face]\nthumb #3002240 thumb #3685896 thumb #3584885\n[/section]\n\n[section,expanded=Round type face]\nthumb #3708722 thumb #3789920 thumb #1241902\n[/section]\n\n\nh4. See also\n* [[japanese]]\n* [[toony]]"
