          "wiki_body": "Images or animations that depict two or more different species who, in real-life, would be the predator or prey of the other(s). Note that this label by itself doesn't imply anything about the characters' interactions. Characters could act [[angry]], [[happy]], [[naughty face|naughty]], [[scared]], [[love|romantic]], etc.\n\nIn most cases, the predator species is usually a carnivore, meaning an animal who only consumes other animals. Pictures also often depict omnivores, which are animals that can consume both other animals and [[plant|plants]]. The prey can be almost any species. Many carnivores and omnivores are 'apex predators'. This means that they that turn animals that would otherwise be doing the hunting into prey. A direct example of this is a [[fox]] seeking out a [[bird]] that itself hunts [[fish]].\n\nThe [[domination]] and [[submissive]] tags and their subsets may also apply to such posts where a power relationship is established.\n\nTag with [[role_reversal]] too if the would-be prey character is dominant and the predator is submissive.\n\nh4.Partial list of Carnivorous Species\n[section,expanded]\n* [[Canine]]\n** [[Domestic_Dog]]\n** [[Fox]]\n** [[Wolf]]\n* [[Crocodile]]\n* [[Felid]]\n** [[Cheetah]]\n** [[Leopard]]\n** [[Lion]]\n** [[Tiger]]\n* [[Hyena]]\n* [[Polar_Bear]]\n* [[Shark]]\n[/section]\n\nh4.Partial list of Herbivorous Species\n[section,expanded]\n* [[Buffalo]]\n* [[Cow]]\n* [[Deer]]\n* [[Elephant]]\n* [[Giraffe]]\n* [[Goat]]\n* [[Horse]]\n* [[Koala]]\n* [[Rabbit]]\n* [[Sheep]]\n[/section]\n\nh4.Partial List of Omnivorous Species\n[section,expanded]\n* [[Ape]]\n** [[Chimpanzee]]\n** [[Human]]\n* [[Bear]]\n* [[Chicken]]\n* [[Crow]]\n* [[Mouse]]\n* [[Pig]]\n* [[Raccoon]]\n* [[Skunk]]\n* [[Sloth]]\n[/section]\n\nh4. See also:\n\n* [[Interspecies]]\n* [[Mount/Rider_relations|Mount/rider relations]]\n\nforum #186154 - predator/prey relations and interspecies implication (Mar. 2016)"
