        {
          "tag": "autumn",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 3217,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #3039087 thumb #3006100 thumb #1697176\r\n\r\n[b]Autumn[/b] (sometimes referred to as \"Fall\" in North America), is one of the four seasons experienced in temperate climates. \r\n\r\nAutumn marks the transition from [[summer]] to [[winter]] which begins in September (\"Northern Hemisphere\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Hemisphere ) or March (\"Southern Hemisphere\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Hemisphere ). During this season, temperatures cool considerably and the arrival of night becomes noticeably earlier; the most visible and emblematic feature of the season is the leaves of deciduous trees changing colours, those leaves shedding, and falling to the ground; because of this, \"autumnal colours\" are orange, red, brown, and yellow.\r\n\r\nAutumn is generally associated with harvest festivals, the celebration of [[Halloween]] (31st October), and in North America with [[Thanksgiving]] (taking place in October in [[Canada]], and November in the [[United States of America]]). Art depicting autumn scenes frequently show characters outdoors enjoying the milder temperatures; sitting in parks or around a [[campfire]], carving a [[pumpkin]], drinking hot beverages, [[rake|raking]] leaves, or playing in [[leaf pile]]s.\r\n\r\nh4. See Also:\r\n* [[Spring]]\r\n* [[Summer]]\r\n* [[Winter]]\r\n"
