        {
          "tag": "honorific",
          "kor": "존경하는",
          "count": 1084,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #4818990 thumb #1669985 thumb #2941524 thumb #3557643 thumb #2460686\r\n\r\nAn [b]honorific[/b] is a title that expresses esteem, courtesy or [[respect]] for a position or [[rank]] when used to address or refer to a person.\r\n\r\n[b]Honorific titles[/b] can be used as \"prefixes\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prefix or \"suffixes\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suffix depending on the situation and context, and there are many factors to take into consideration when using a title.\r\n\r\nIt can depend on:\r\n* The style\r\n* The custom of the country (e.g. Japanese titles can be totally different from those in Spain or France)\r\n* The listener's gender\r\n** Their age (e.g. [[toddler]], [[adolescent]], [[young adult]])\r\n*** Their [[family]] function (e.g. [[Parent (lore)|parent]], [[older sibling]], [[in-laws]])\r\n* Hierarchical position (e.g. [[boss]], [[employee]], [[principal]])\r\n** Their job (e.g. [[teacher]], [[coach]], [[police officer]], [[nurse]])\r\n* Whether it's a [[roommate]] or a [[stranger]], etc.\r\n\r\nGenerally, these titles are used as a third-person grammatical style and as a second-person form of address. \r\n\r\nMany titles are positive and complimentary, showing respect for the listener, such as \"Mister\", \"Sir\", \"Ma'am\", \"Miss\", \"Professor\", \"Doctor\", and \"Master\". However, the opposite also exists: negative, derogatory titles are used to degrade the listener, like \"Servant\" or \"This weirdo\".\r\n\r\nIn all cases, the \"honorific\" tag is used when an honorific title is used to address a character in a post, whether it's complimentary [i]or[/i] derogatory, and no matter the language of the title. (e.g. \"Mademoiselle\", \"Señor\", \"Herr\", \"太太\", \"さん\", \"후배\", \"พยาบาล\")\r\n\r\nh4. List of [[English honorific|English honorifics]]\r\n[section=Honorific titles]\r\nh4. Common titles\r\n* \"[[Master]]\"\r\n* \"Mr\"\r\n* \"Miss\"\r\n* \"Mrs\"\r\n* \"Ms\"\r\n* \"Mx\" (a gender-neutral/non-binary honorific)\r\n\r\nh4. Formal titles\r\n* \"Sir\"\r\n* \"[[Gentleman]]\"\r\n* \"Sire\"\r\n* \"Mistress\"\r\n* \"Madam\" or \"Ma'am\"\r\n* \"Dame\"\r\n* \"Lord\"\r\n* \"Lady\"\r\n* \"Esq\"\r\n* \"Excellency\"\r\n* \"Your/Her/His Honour\" (\"Honor\" in American English)\r\n\r\nh4. Professional titles\r\n* \"Dr\" (\"[[Doctor]]\")\r\n* \"Professor\"\r\n* \"[[Teacher]]\"\r\n[/section]\r\n\r\nh4. Related tags:\r\nh5. Specific languages:\r\n* [[english honorific]]\r\n* [[japanese honorific]]\r\n\r\nh5.Other:\r\n* [[greeting]]\r\n** [[greeting viewer]] – When the greeting is made for the viewer\r\n* [[name in dialogue]]/[[name drop]] – When a name is placed before or after a title\r\n\r\nh4. See also:\r\n* [[nobility]]\r\n* [[profanity]]\r\n** [[insult]] – Used also for derogatory titles\r\n* [[term of endearment]]"
        },
