        {
          "tag": "hibiscus",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 735,
          "wiki_body": "[b][i]Hibiscus[/i][/b] is a genus of flowering plants in the mallow family, Malvaceae. The genus is quite large, comprising several hundred species that are native to warm-temperate, subtropical and tropical regions throughout the world. Member species are renowned for their large, showy [[flower|flowers]] and are commonly known simply as [b]hibiscus[/b], or less widely known as [b]rose mallow[/b].\r\n\r\nThe leaves are alternate, ovate to lanceolate, often with a toothed or lobed margin. The flowers are large, conspicuous, trumpet-shaped, with five or more petals, color from white to pink, red, orange, peach, yellow or purple, and from 4–18 cm broad. Flower color in certain species, such as [i]H. mutabilis[/i] and [i]H. tiliaceus[/i], changes with age. The fruit is a dry five-lobed capsule, containing several seeds in each lobe, which are released when the capsule dehisces (splits open) at maturity. It is of red and white colours. It is an example of complete flowers.\r\n\r\nThe hibiscus is the national flower of [[Haiti]] and is used in their national tourism slogan of [i]Haïti: Experience It![/i] The hibiscus species also represents several other nations. The [i]Hibiscus syriacus[/i] is the national flower of [[South Korea|South]] [[Korea]], and [i]Hibiscus rosa-sinensis[/i] is the national flower of [[Malaysia]]. The red hibiscus is the flower of the [[Hindu]] goddess Kali, and appears frequently in depictions of her in the art of Bengal, [[India]], often with the goddess and the flower merging in form. The hibiscus is used as an offering to goddess Kali and Lord Ganesha in Hindu worship.\r\n\r\nThe hibiscus flower is traditionally worn by [[Tahiti|Tahitian]] and Hawaiian girls. If the flower is worn behind the left ear, the woman is married or in a relationship. If the flower is worn on the right, she is single or openly available for a relationship. The hibiscus is [[Hawaii]]'s state flower.\r\n\r\n[b]Source:[/b] \"Wikipedia\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hibiscus"
        },
