          "kor": "",
          "count": 616,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #1323965\r\n\r\nThe [b]perineal raphe[/b] is a visible line or ridge of tissue on the body that extends from the [[anus]] through the [[perineum]] to scrotum ([[male]]) or labia majora ([[female]]). It is found in both males and females, arises from the fusion of the urogenital folds, and is visible running medial through anteroposterior, to the anus where it resolves in a small knot of skin of varying size.\r\n\r\nIn males, this structure continues through the midline of the scrotum ([[scrotal raphe]]) and upwards through the posterior midline aspect of the [[penis]] ([[penile raphe]]). It also exists deeper through the scrotum where it is called the [[scrotal septum]]. It is the result of a fetal developmental phenomenon whereby the scrotum and penis close toward the midline and fuse.\r\n\r\n\"Perineal raphe - From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perineal_raphe"
