          "wiki_body": "thumb #489664   thumb #2403149   thumb #3496597   thumb #3645120   thumb #4198522   \r\n\r\nThe [b]buccinator[/b] muscle (Latin: [i][b]Musculus buccinator[/b][/i]; also called the [b]maxillary rictus[/b], [b]mandibular rictus[/b], or just the [b]rictus[/b]) is a thin quadrilateral muscle occupying the interval between the maxilla (upper jaw) and the mandible (lower jaw) at the side of the face.\r\n\r\nIn most species, [[human|humans]] for example, the buccinator is not visible on the face as it forms the anterior part of the cheek/the lateral wall of the oral cavity, and can only be seen in a [[mouth shot]]. It is more apparent in [[scalie]] species ([[reptile|reptiles]], [[dinosaur|dinosaurs]], [[dragon|dragons]], etc.) when they have their [[open mouth|mouths opened]], where it appears as a [[membrane (anatomy)|membrane]] at the corner of the mouth."
