          "tag": "japan",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 480,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #2058121 thumb #3190644 thumb #2298863 thumb #57461\r\n\r\n[b]Japan[/b] is an island nation on the coast of eastern Asia; the nation's capital and largest city is [[Tokyo]], which is also the most populous urban area in the world. Other major cities include Osaka, Sapporo, and Kyoto.\r\n\r\nJapan is the world's third largest economy and a major cultural exporter, with global influence and popularity of Japanese food, electronics, brands, music, movies, video games, and subculture (particularly [[anime]] and [[manga]]). \r\n\r\n[quote]\r\nh5. [i]Important Note:[/i]\r\n\r\n[b]This tag is specifically intended for scenes set in Japan[/b]\r\n\r\nPosts tagged as `japan` should depict Japanese locations, landmarks, or scenes that heavily imply the characters are in Japan; This tag also applies to the Japanese flag, Japanese symbols, etc. \r\n\r\nFor broader Japanese aesthetics, or \"themeing\", as well as a list of tags related to Japanese culture, history and in general — [b]see [[Japanese]][/b]. \r\n\r\n[/quote]\r\n\r\nh3. \"Japan\" Tags\r\n\r\nh5. Locations\r\n* [[tokyo]]\r\n* [[mount fuji]]\r\n\r\nh5. Structures and Landmarks\r\n* [[himeji castle]]\r\n* [[tokyo big sight]]\r\n* [[tokyo tower]]\r\n* [[tokyo skytree]]\r\n* [[torii]] \r\n* [[shrine]] (in the context of [[Shinto]] shrines)\r\n\r\nh5. Symbols\r\n* [[japanese flag]]\r\n* [[rising sun]]\r\n** [[rising sun flag]]\r\n\r\nh4. Related Tags\r\n* [[japanese architecture]]\r\n\r\nh4. See Also\r\n* [[japanese]]\r\n* [[tag group:countries]]\r\n\r\n"
        },
        {
          "tag": "battery",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 480,
          "wiki_body": "\"An electric [b]battery[/b] is a device consisting of one or more electrochemical cells with external connections provided to power electrical devices such as [[flashlight|flashlights]], [[cellphone|smartphones]], and electric [[car|cars]]. When a battery is supplying electric power, its positive terminal is the cathode and its negative terminal is the anode. The terminal marked negative is the source of electrons that will flow through an external electric circuit to the positive terminal. When a battery is connected to an external electric load, a redox reaction converts high-energy reactants to lower-energy products, and the free-energy difference is delivered to the external circuit as electrical energy. Historically the term \"battery\" specifically referred to a device composed of multiple cells, however the usage has evolved to include devices composed of a single cell.\r\n\r\nPrimary (single-use or \"disposable\") batteries are used once and discarded; the electrode materials are irreversibly changed during discharge. Common examples are the alkaline battery used for flashlights and a multitude of portable electronic devices. Secondary (rechargeable) batteries can be discharged and recharged multiple times using an applied electric current; the original composition of the electrodes can be restored by reverse current. Examples include the lead-acid batteries used in vehicles and lithium-ion batteries used for portable electronics such as [[laptop|laptops]] and smartphones.\r\n\r\nBatteries come in many shapes and sizes, from miniature cells used to power hearing aids and wristwatches to small, thin cells used in smartphones, to large lead acid batteries used in cars and trucks, and at the largest extreme, huge battery banks the size of rooms that provide standby or emergency power for telephone exchanges and computer data centers.\r\n\r\nAccording to a 2005 estimate, the worldwide battery industry generates US$48 billion in sales each year, with 6% annual growth.\r\n\r\nBatteries have much lower specific energy (energy per unit mass) than common fuels such as gasoline. In automobiles, this is somewhat offset by the higher efficiency of electric motors in converting chemical energy to mechanical work, compared to combustion engines.\"\r\n\r\n[b]Source:[/b] \"Wikipedia\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_battery"
        },
