          "tag": "superflat",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 478,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #686561 thumb #3162451 thumb #2594124 thumb #2401645 thumb #4575665\r\n\r\n[b]Superflat[/b] is a postmodern art movement that originated in Japan in the early 2000s, coined by artist [[Takashi Murakami]]. Superflat is inspired by manga and anime, particularly the visual aesthetics present in them and in other Japanese pop culture, as well as being inspired by Japanese consumerism. Superflat aims to blur or \"flatten\" the line between high and low art.\r\n\r\nSuperflat has been embraced by American artists, with artists in southern Florida creating their own spin on the genre, \"SoFlo Superflat\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SoFlo_Superflat. Murakami also designed the album cover for [[Kanye West]]'s Graduation.\r\n\r\n[quote]h5. Common elements of Superflat\r\n* Vibrant colors\r\n* Geometric shapes\r\n* Bold, thick [[outline]]s\r\n* Flat design\r\n* The use of 3D\r\n* \"Kawaii\" imagery and [[toony]] illustrations\r\n** [[Chibi]] (super-deformed) characters\r\n* [[Flower]] shapes\r\n* [[Graffiti]]\r\n* [[Otaku]] culture\r\n* Themes of sexuality\r\n* Blending [[inspired_by_formal_art|formal art]] with commercial art\r\n[/quote]\r\n\r\nh4. External links\r\n* \"Superflat\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superflat on Wikipedia\r\n* \"Superflat Pop\":https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Superflat_Pop on Aesthetics Wiki\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n* [[frutiger_metro]]\r\n* [[kemono]]\r\n* [[pop_art]]\r\n* [[y2k_(graphic_design)]]"
