        {
          "tag": "half-identical_twins",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 171,
          "wiki_body": "Half-identical twins, or Semi-identical twins, scientifically known as [i]\"Sesquizygotic Twins\"[/i], are [[twins_(lore)|twins]] who share 75% of their DNA, having nearly identical physical traits accompanied by notable differences\r\nthumb #681165 thumb #4448746 thumb #499546\r\n\r\nHalf-identical twins are the result of one [[ovum|egg cell]] being fertilized by two separate [[sperm cell|sperm cells]], after which it successfully implanted in the [[uterus|womb]] before undergoing cell division and separating completely into two [[fetus|embryos]].\r\nBoth siblings share 75% of their DNA as a result. This means that the twins have more in common genetically than normal [[sibling_(lore)|siblings]] or [[fraternal twins]] and less in common genetically than [[identical twins]]. This means these siblings will develop to have many visual similarities, but with at least some visual differences from one another, which can include but aren't limited to, having differing sexual characteristics or hair, eye, and fur colors.\r\n\r\nDuring development, both [[fetus|embryos]] share the same placenta and amniotic sac.\r\n\r\n[u][b]Half-identical twins have the potential to be visually indistinguishable from either normal [[siblings]] or [[identical twins]].[/b][/u] To [[TWYS|Tag What You See]] (TWYS), this should only be tagged given evidence in the image.\r\n\r\nThis tag should be used if one of the following differ in otherwise identical twins:\r\n* [b][[Fur]], [[Skin]], or [[Hair]] Color, Patterns or Traits[/b]\r\n** Examples: Different [[countershading]], skin tone, or type of hair.\r\n** Exclusions: [[scar|scars]], [[tattoo|tattoos]], bleached or dyed, or differing hairstyles\r\n* [b][[eyes|Eye]] Colors[/b]\r\n** Examples: Differences to sclerae, iris, and pupils.\r\n*** Even if all three are different, for the sake of tagging, it counts as one difference.\r\n** Exclusions: [[Ocular Prosthetic]], [[glass eye]], or other non-genetic differences in eyes.\r\n* [b]Body Composition[/b]\r\n** Examples: differences in height, greater or fewer body parts, or genetic defects.\r\n** Exclusions: amputation, [[prosthetic|prostheses]], or evidence of surgical alterations.\r\n* [b][[tag group:genders|Sexual Characteristics]] (genders)[/b]\r\n** Examples: male + female, herm + female, or male + andromorph.\r\n*** Even if [[trans (lore)]] applies to one or more twins, [[TWYS]] is not overridden.\r\n** Exclusions: One twin is [[visibly trans]] or if their breasts or genital configuration have otherwise been altered or removed through surgical or other means.\r\n\"Mirror Twins\", Twins who appear to be [[Symmetry (Reflectional)|reflectionally symmetrical]], can be distinguished by their features and are considered half-identical unless one twin's mirror image differs from their twin in greater than one of the above traits.\r\nDifferences of all twins in more than just one of the above traits should normally be considered fraternal twins.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[twins (lore)]]: Tag for characters that are twins, regardless of how much DNA they share.\r\n* [[fraternal twins]]: Tag for characters that share 50% of their DNA.\r\n* [[identical twins]]: Tag for characters that share 100% of their DNA.\r\n* \"Semi-identical (sesquizygotic) twins on Wikipedia\":https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin#Semi-identical_(sesquizygotic)_twins: Information about Sesquizygotic twins."
        },
