          "wiki_body": "An [b]elytron[/b] (plural: [i]elytra[/i]) is a modified, hardened forewing of certain [[insect|insects]], commonly seen on [[beetle|beetles]]. The elytra primarily serve as protective wing-cases for the [[wings|hindwings underneath]], which are used for [[flying]]. To fly, a beetle typically opens the elytra and then [[spread wings|extends their wings]], flying while still holding the elytra open, though some beetles can fly with the elytra closed. In some species, the elytra are fused together, rendering the insect flightless.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[tag_group:feral_anatomy]]\r\n* [[arthropod]]\r\n** [[insect]]\r\n*** [[beetle]]\r\n* [[exoskeleton]]\r\n* [[wings]]\r\n** [[insect_wings]]"
