          "kor": "",
          "count": 886,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #3214542 thumb #3462292 thumb #3111317 thumb #3168087 thumb #3145569\r\n\r\n[b]Wasp waist[/b] describes a character with an [i]exceptionally [/i][[small waist]]. The term \"wasp waist\" comes from the visual similarity to a [[wasp|wasp's]] segmented body. \r\n\r\nA wasp waisted character's body will usually have an abrupt, or extremely curved transition from an average width upper body (or rib cage) to a dramatically narrow cinched waist, followed by curving out to \"normal\" hip width. \r\n\r\nDuring the 19th and early 20th centuries, using [[corset|corsets]] to \"shape\" the waist was a beauty trend for women in the western world. In artwork, wasp waists are commonly used to give a character an exaggerated feminine form. \r\n\r\nWhile wasp waists are often unnaturally narrow in art, extreme waist binding and use of corsets can create seemingly impossibly narrow waists in real life. \r\n\r\n\r\nh5. Related Tags\r\n* [[hourglass figure]] - a character with a wasp waist will often have an exaggerated hourglass figure\r\n* [[small waist]] - for less narrow, but still notably narrow waists \r\n* [[wide hips]] - wasp waisted characters often have wide hips in contrast to their extremely narrow waist\r\n\r\nh5. See Also\r\n* [[tag group:figures]]"
