          "wiki_body": "[b]Contact onomatopoeia[/b] is an umbrella tag for [[onomatopoeia]] representing contact focused sounds. This tag commonly applies to sound effects related to rubbing, gripping, and light or heavy impact themes.\r\n\r\nThe source of the sound effect does not need to be visible for this tag to apply.\r\n\r\nh4. Contact sound effects / themes\r\n\r\n* [[gripping_sound_effect]] - \"Grab\", \"Grasp\", \"Grip\", \"Grope\", \"Hold\"\r\n* [[rubbing_sound_effect]] - \"Rub\", \"Grind\"\r\n* [[scratching_sound_effect]]\r\n\r\n* [[petting]]\r\n* [[poking]]\r\n* [[prodding]]\r\n* [[stroking]]\r\n\r\nh5. Impacts and collisions\r\n\r\nConsider using this base tag for generally light contact examples, and [[impact_onomatopoeia]] for heavier contact / collision sound effects. Most common impact sound effects will have their own dedicated sound effect tag.\r\n\r\nh6. Light impact themes\r\n\r\nThe intensity of the impact can be used to change a light impact theme to a heavy impact theme.\r\n\r\n* [[bumping]]\r\n* [[clacking]]\r\n* [[clattering]]\r\n* [[clinking]]\r\n* [[knocking]]\r\n* [[patting]]\r\n* [[tapping]]\r\n* [[thumping]]\r\n\r\nh6. General exclusions\r\n\r\nSound effects listed here probably shouldn't be treated as an contact sound effect. This list is subject to change.\r\n\r\n* [[compression_sound_effect]] - For now sound effects that fall under compression are left out of the contact onomatopoeia tag. Many of these though, particularly squeezing variants, are probably compatible with the tag. \r\n** [[crushing_sound_effect]]\r\n* [[plop]] - If it seems like a derivation of \"[[Plap]]\", then it counts. It could refer to \"[[pop_(sound_effect)|Pop]]\" instead.\r\n* [[pulling_sound_effect]]\r\n* [[pushing_sound_effect]]\r\n* [[splash]]\r\n* [[splat]]\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[tag_group:sound_effects]]\r\n\r\n* [[sound_effects]]\r\n** [[onomatopoeia]]\r\n*** [b]contact_onomatopoeia[/b]\r\n**** [[impact_onomatopoeia]]\r\n*** [[motion_onomatopoeia]]"
        },
        {
          "tag": "impact_onomatopoeia",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 4928,
          "wiki_body": "An umbrella tag for [[onomatopoeia]] associated with [[hitting]], and other forms of collisions. Most sound effects representative of a forceful impact, or striking blow can be considered an impact / collision onomatopoeia.\r\n\r\nh4. Tagging guidelines\r\n\r\n* Individual sound effect tags may be used for a variety of spelling variations. Check for missing letters as it may affect whether a sound effect form qualifies as an onomatopoeia.\r\n* This tag, and even some of the individual sound effect tags have multi-language support. If the sound effect form in another language doesn't quite translate well enough into any specific form tag, this tag serves as the fallback tag for foreign, and English sound effect usages.\r\n* Impact onomatopoeia can be tagged even if the impact is hidden from view.\r\n* Some lighter (softer) impact concepts are usually better tagged under the parent concept: [[contact_onomatopoeia]]. [[Knocking]], [[tapping]], and [[thumping]] are the main examples.\r\n** The parent tag contains numerous themes related to touching, and collision. The themes mentioned are impact related, and can be considered `impact onomatopoeia`; though it is useful to mention that there is flexibility available to users in how benign impacts are tagged. \r\n\r\nh4. List of impact onomatopoeia\r\n\r\n[table]\r\n  [thead]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [th] Sound effect form [/th]\r\n      [th] Sound effect tag [/th]\r\n      [th] Extra information [/th]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n  [/thead]\r\n  [tbody]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Bap\", \"Bam\", \"Blam\", \"Bop\" [/td]\r\n      [td] N/A [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Bash\" [/td]\r\n      [td] N/A [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Bonk\", \"Bnk\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[bonk_(sound_effect)]] [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Bump\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[bumping]] [/td]\r\n      [td] This concept is sometimes treated as a light impact similar to [[knocking]], [[tapping]], and [[thumping]]. [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Clap\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[clapping]] [/td]\r\n      [td] The [[twerking]], and the [[applause]] kind. [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Clash\" [/td]\r\n      [td] N/A [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Crash\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[crash]] [/td]\r\n      [td] Not yet really tagged as an impact term yet; it is used similarly to \"Smash\" [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Flump\", \"Flmp\", \"Pomf\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[flump]], [[pomf]] [/td]\r\n      [td] These terms generally are used to describe a character falling over, usually into something soft. Pomf is sometimes used as an alternative to \"Poof\". [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Plap\", \"Pap\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[plap]] [/td]\r\n      [td] Not to be confused with [[plop]] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Pound\" [/td]\r\n      [td] N/A [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Slam\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[slam]] [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Slap\", \"Shlap\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[slap]] (action)\r\n[[slap_(sound_effect)]]\r\n[[shlap]] (alternative form of \"Slap\") [/td]\r\n      [td] The presence of this sound effect doesn't always mean the action tag should be used. [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Smack\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[smack_(sound_effect)]] [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Spank\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[spanking]] (action)\r\n[[spank_(sound_effect)]] [/td]\r\n      [td] The action, and sound effect term usually both apply. This tag might be reformatted to `spanking_sound_effect` in the future. [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Smash\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[smashing]] [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Thud\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[thud]] [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Thunk\" [/td]\r\n      [td] N/A [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Thwap\", \"Thwack\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[thwap]] [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Whack\", \"Wack\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[whack]] [/td]\r\n      [td] Perhaps this could use a suffix. [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Wham\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[wham]] [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Whap\", \"Wap\" [/td]\r\n      [td] [[whap]] [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n    [tr]\r\n      [td] \"Whomp\", \"Whump\" [/td]\r\n      [td] N/A [/td]\r\n      [td] [/td]\r\n    [/tr]\r\n  [/tbody]\r\n[/table]\r\n\r\nThese may conditionally apply as impact onomatopoeia: \"Bang\", \"Crack\"\r\n\r\nh5. Exclusions\r\n\r\nFor tagging purposes, the following concepts should not be considered a part of this tag:\r\n\r\n- Try to keep [[heartbeat]] sound effects out of this tag. [[Thumping]] can still be used for the \"Thump\" sound effect.\r\n- Kissing, licking, or biting\r\n- Impact terms exclusively related to water, or other liquids ([[slosh]], [[splashing|splash]]). These are better placed in their own sound effect category.\r\n\r\nh4. See also\r\n\r\n* [[tag_group:sound_effects]]\r\n\r\n* [[sound_effects]]\r\n** [[onomatopoeia]]\r\n*** [[contact_onomatopoeia]]\r\n**** [b]impact_onomatopoeia[/b]\r\n*** [[motion_onomatopoeia]]"
