        {
          "tag": "soviet_union",
          "kor": "",
          "count": 541,
          "wiki_body": "thumb #1868367\r\n\r\nThe [b]Soviet Union[/b], officially the [i]Union of Soviet Socialist Republics[/i] (often shortened to USSR) was a transcontinental Eurasian country that existed between 1922 and 1991. The Soviet Union was nominally a federal union of fifteen constituent countries under a Marxist-Leninist socialist republic - a [[Communism|Communist]] state -  in reality, it was a totalitarian [[Russia]]-dominated one-party state governed by a party dictatorship. \r\n\r\nThis tag should be used for posts depicting characters in Soviet uniforms, characters with Soviet aesthetics, images displaying Soviet flags or symbols, and scenes associated with or set in the Soviet Union.\r\n\r\nh5. Notes\r\n* [i]CCCP[/i] is the Russian Cyrillic acronym for USSR - [i]Союз Советских Социалистических Республик[/i], this acronym is often in Soviet Union related posts\r\n* The USSR is often represented by a bear; Soviet and Russian furries are frequently ursine. \r\n\r\n\r\nh5. Tags Associated With or Related to the Soviet Union\r\n\r\n* [[ak-47]] - A Soviet made automatic or semi-automatic rifle\r\n** [[ak-74]] - The follow-up to the AK-47\r\n* [[animal farm]] - A novel by George Orwell that satirized the history and politics of the early Soviet Union\r\n* [[communism]] - The claimed ideology of the Soviet Union\r\n* [[hammer and sickle]] - Communist symbol featured on the Soviet flag\r\n* [[red star]] - Another symbol used by the USSR \r\n* [[ushanka]] - A winter hat worn by the Soviet Army, and popularly associated with the USSR and Russia in general \r\n* [[vladimir lenin]] - one of the founders of the USSR and first head of the Soviet government \r\n* [[joseph stalin]] - leader of the Soviet Union from 1922-1952, infamous as a dictator\r\n* [[in soviet russia]] - a joke spoofing the differences between the USSR and the West\r\n\r\nh4. See Also\r\n* [[Russia]] - the largest part of the USSR, and as the Russian Federation, the direct successor state to